        <head>• gig;t:k Xi, '••*, • /- t , . .</head>
        <p>• gig;t:k Xi, '••*, • /- t , . .</p>
        <p>corner to have her boots cleaned by a boot-black, and one, by writing to a London paper, and who is evidently not equal to such an ordeal, wants to know why there are net feminine boot-blacks. This suggests a new pro- fession for working girls. There are few walks in life they have not essayed, and at one of the oldest Tele- graph Agencies in London,girl messengers have already superseded the bey messenger. But hitherto the shoe- blacks have been free from the competition of girls, not that they would suffer much from a feminine insti- tution if it were set up in the ladies waiting rooms at the railway stations. Many women can obtain all the attention they require at their clubs, but there are vast numbers of women who come into town for a day's shopping, and who, in bad weather, would be glad of being made a little more presentable about the feet, before perhaps making a few calls, if such a convenience was available without drawing undue notice upon one- self. MAD GE.</p>
