        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Campbells.—The Duke and Duchess of Argyle intend to spend some months in Bgypt, and will also visit Italy on their way home. The Duchess who is an able and gifted artist, as well as an admirable sculptress proposes to sketch a good deal while she is away. Like our Queen, the Duchess has been very successful in re- taining her youthful appearance, though she will be fifty-eight in March. The Duke is nearly three years her senior and is now in his sixty-first year. His brother Lord Archibald Campbell,who is heir-presumptive to the dukedom, is a managing partner in Coutts Bank, and is bothan artist and writer. His published works include 1 a volume on" Highland Dress, Arms, and Armaments," and another on the" Records of Argyle." His wife Lady Archibald Campbell is said to be the best woman piper in Scotland, and is also a very clever amateur actress,_ _</p>
        <p>On Manners.—A woman as just produced a book which ought to have been published a month ago in order that candidates for Parliament might have had an opportunity of studying it. It purports to convey the secret of popularity, and contains many hints to people who desire to make a good impression without expend- ing money on the business. It would perhaps be unkind to say that there is nothing new in the volume,but some of the best things in it are certainly to be found in the Earl of Chesterfield's letters to his son—a book with a great many recommendations and not a few faults. For example this is what the authoress tells us to do when we are introduced to anybody :—" Never treat the old and beautiful ceremony of greeting or introduction in a cold, careless, and perfunctory way. Offer your hand with a prompt, spontaneous gesture,infuse an expression eloquent of glad surprise with your eyes." There are many other rules for the adoption of an amiable manner. Some people do not need them, because they always greet a new acquaintance as if be were one dear soul whom they had been intensely longing to meet all the years of their life. Sometimes they secure popularity by such methods, but more often a reputa- tation for insincerity.</p>
        <p>it New Book.—Those among us familiar with ." The Silence of Dean Maitland," The House of Hidden Treasure," "The Last Sentence," and " Four Leaved Clover," by Maxwell Gray, will rejoice to hear that yet another work from the pen of this famous authoress, is in the press, and will shortly appear. The title of her forthcoming book is "The Great Refusal," and the publisher is Mr. John Long. Women Politicians.—Lady Warwick has had the satisfaction of seeing many of the Labour and Socialist candidates, for whom she worked, returned to Parlia- ment, but most of the Society women, who have been active on behalf of candidates are Unionists, and have *been disappointed in their labours. Lady Castlereagh is perhaps an exception, but though she greatly helped her husband, who is one of the few Unionists who have captured a Liberal seat, her father, the well-known Mr. Chaplin, was defeated in the Sleaford Division of Lincolnshire. Lord Castlereagh is the only surviving son of the Marquis of Londonderry, and he was not only ably supported at Maidstone by his wife, but also by his mother Lady Londonderry, who is a woman of great beauty, high intelligence, and charm of manner. She is an experienced hand at electioneering and has always been of the greatest assistance to her husband, ever since the early days when she helped him to gain 'his seat as a member of Parliament.</p>
        <p>Society and Parliament.—The new House of Commons will reflect many of the social changes that have taken place in the last twenty-five years. High society as it was formerly called has moved a long way in the direction of democracy and cosmopolitanism, and at the same time the upward movement of the working classes has been continuous, until we have those of the Board Schools and the Universities occupying side by side the high places in Parliament. Theold families remain,but many of their representatives, have disappeared from Westminster, and the House of Commons will no longer be the rendezvous of fashionable society during the season. From the point of view that Parliament has serious business of the nation to attend to this may be no loss, but socially it will tend to still further break -up into fragments the fashionable classes, which were formerly so closely allied to the Court and to Parliament.</p>
        <p>Early Spring Colours.--In Paris, brown is the pre- vailing hue, but so far we have shewn no inclination to follow the gay capitals' lead in this direction. Quite a new shade, which appears to be fisding favour with dressmakers, tailors, and milliners alike, is raspberry, hut those women who have to be content with a very limited number of costumes in the year will act wisely in passing it by. The colour is a striking one,and all too easily recognisable, moreover it is likely to quickly become much too popular for good taste. Authorities on the nubject predict another black and white season, and already black and white checks are in considerable request. Fur-trimmed Gowns.—Many dressmakers are pinning their faith to fur trimming for house gowns, the long skirt being bordered at the hem, and the bosiice touched with it. There is nothing new of course in the idea of trimming spring toilettes with fur, but it is a charming one, and last year many ball and dinner gowns prepared for mid.aummer wear, were adorned with ermine or mable. Strips of fur shout three or four inches in width, are being offered at the furriers' sales at marvellous prices, and these are bargains which should be secured without delay. In the matter of renovations, it is marvellous what a touch of fur will accomplish, a fact those among us who possess a gown which shows sign of age about the bodice and hem, will do well to con- alder.</p>
        <p>Corsets.—The high corset is much less worn than formerly, numbers of women preferring the hip and waist belt, which undoubtedly is far more comfortable to weer, and infinitely better for our health, than the mass of stiff whalebone with which we have so long encompassed ourselves. Very few who try the hip and waist corset, go back to the high style, and an excellent idea is to cut down an old pair of corsets, remove all the bones that can be dispensed with, and bind the cut edge with stout ribbon, or velvet. The experiment can thus he cheaply tried, and if approved of, all corsets in wear can be similarly treated. Way not Girl Shoe Blacks?—The woman requires sorne amount of courage who will stand at a busy street</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
