      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>NEW USE FOR PETROLEL7M.</head>
        <p>A NEW USE FOR PETROLEL7M.</p>
        <p>ADVOCATED BY AN EMINENT PHYSICIAN. The public is acquainted to some extent with the claims in favour of petroleum as an internal remedy in the treatment of coughs and lung affections. It would seem that eminent medical authorities now regard it as of equal or even greater value in the treatment of typhoid or enteric fever. In a lecture recently delivered at St. Gsorge's Hospital by the eminent physiologist, William Ewart, M.D., Cantab., F.R.C.P., and fully reported in the " British Medical Journal " of December 9th, this use for Petroleum is discussed at some length. The argument advanced in its favour appears to the lay mind as reasonable. It is shown that petroleum. unlike animal or vegetable oil, undergoes no chemical change in the stomach and is not absorbed; hence its lubricating, soothing effects are exerted-throughout the entire length of the alimentary canal. It is stated that properly purified petroleum is as tasteless as it is harmless and is therefore acceptable to all patients in almost any dose. Some years ago another eminent medical authority, Dr. Wm. Duffield Robinson, strongly advocated the i internal use of petroleum n Phthisis as well as in tubercular and other forms of diarrhrca, but apparently lit did not gain much vogue at the time. At present, however, it seems in favour generally, the well-known Angier's Emulsion of Petroleum being immensely popular both with the medical profession and the public.</p>
        <p>A RUMOUR DEFINED.</p>
        <p>A Welsh witness at the Carnarvonshire Assizes on Tuesday puzzled Mr. Justice Lawrence by saying that after a bottle had been thrown he heard a rumour. " A rnmour ? " remarked the judge. " What was the rumour about ?"</p>
        <p>I "Oh, I mean tho noise of the bottle striking a gate," replied witness araid laughter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CPUr.I TENPTATI.N.</head>
        <p>CPUr.I TENPTATI.N.</p>
        <p>Plorence Hodgkinson,</p>
        <p>Au;:hor of " TIN3EL AND .GOLD," Chc., U.?</p>
        <p>; '1 ._:,</p>
        <p>of Charles Gresham's wooing and her own reluctant half-promise; she, poor girl, honestly regarded it as a half-promise and nothing more. , " You know, Hildred," she said simply, " I really like Charles Gresham very much. When I was a child and father and I lodged with his mother he used to be very good to me, and the times when he was on shore used to be quite red-letter days.' But lately I seem to have grown away from him ; it isn't that there's anyone I like better, 'but I feel that there are heaps of things I care for , that Charles takes no interest in at all, and somehow I don't feel as if his society would content me all my life." I "Then why did you, promise to marry him. childie?" asked Hildred gently. "Because he kept on telling me my love would come in time, and another thing, Hildred, he pictured me growing old and lonely and I felt quite frightened and began to think that perhaps the liking I had for Charles was really enough to marry on. Besides, Hildred, he asked me at first to try being engaged to him for six months, and 'as I knew he would be away all the time I thought it didn't make any difference. I never dreamed he'd consider me really bound to him and tell his mother I was to be her daughter." " Has he told anyone else so? " No." said Helen quietly, " and I ,think Mrs. Gresham will keep it 'a secret. She isn't over-pleased at the engapement ; you know she is much better off than in the old days when we lodged with her and I believe she thinks her boy might luive done better." " It's a way mothers have," said 'Eldred with a little smile. " I 4rn afraid she will be rather a thorn in your flesh, Helen, for she may think it her duty to keepl a particular watch and ward over you in her son's interest during his absence." , •.. . " Well, she won't be able to begin vet," said Helen cheerfully , " for she's going to spend six weeks‘on her brothel's farm more than a hundred miles away . and she starts to- morrow. What's that? "as her eyes sudden- y caught sight of a thick-looking envelope on he mantelpiece. " A letter for me? Well, wonders never cease." Hildred went into the tiny kitchen to )repare the evening meal, leaving Helen to 7ead her letter. She was away perhaps ten ninutes and when she came back she felt )ewildered for Helen's face was transfigured. ler blue eyes shone like stars, there was a leeper colour in her cheeks ; she flung hi r irms round her friend's neck and kissed her is she said eagerly : • "Oh, Hildred, it can't be true I It must m some wonderful dream." "What is it, dear ? " asked Hildred. " I :an see by your face it is good news; who las written to you ?," "The letter's from Melville and Brett. Oh, ilildred, I thought you would be surprised." Little as Hildred Jasper knew of Melbourne .he was aware that Melville and Brett Were me of the best known legal firms there ; she tad seen their offices in Collins Street, and mew that the partners stood at the very head tf their profession. " Read the letter for yourself," said Helen, ' or shall I tell you about it ? " " I should like that best," was the quiet • o.nswer. Well, you know father was unfortunate. . have told you his story so often, how .that us family *turned their backs on him_ when he narried mother, and as he couldn't get on in England he came out to Australia when I was :hree years old. He would never talk of the xist. only sometimes the sight of a picture of in English landscape would bring the tears nto. his eyes and then, though he never said a word, I knew how homesick he felt and how le longed after the old country. "He would never tell me what relatives he tad in England or where they lived, oily when he was very ill and knew he should never •ecover he wrote 'Home,' posting 'the lecter iimself, so that I never knew how it was tddressed. Then he waited almost in agouy or the answer which never came. I have :elt, Hildred, that I • well-nigh hated my English relatives just ,because they took no iotice of that dying letter. "Father knew Missy Murray, who had :hen just come out from England to be Principal of S. Monica's. I think her family Ind his had been friends for Iyears iii Englan, I. He went to see her and asked her WI help when he was gone; it was she who got me my present engagement, and she has always bent very kind to me, but though I knew she :ould have told me all about my relatives in c.ngland I never asked her a single question." " And now have they relented and written to ask these lawyers to find you ? " suggested Hildred. " Not quite ; my grandfather is dead. It seems he repented at the last of not answering Dad's letter, and left a very kindly message for me. But that is" lot_ all, Hildred. It seems I ant his heiress.; .fancy, Hildred, I, a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>CHAPTER L</head>
        <p>CHAPTER L</p>
        <p>"It's no use, Helen. I've loved you, man and boy, for seven years, and I'll never give you up. Why are you so hard-hearted, dear; why can't you promise to make me happy ? " The speaker, Charles Gresham, was a sailor, first mate on the " Albatross," as fine a ship as ever plied in the Merchant Service ; he was a good-lookinc, fellow, but there was something hard about Os lower jaw, and anyone skilled in physiognomy would have declared that the young man had something of the bulldog about him. The girl beside him was a very different type. Helen Jardine was an orphan and worked hard for • her living. She and her father had lodged with Charles's mother in old days ; .since Mr.. Jardine's death his daughter had become a teacher in one of the big girls' schools the other side of Melbourne, and chummed with another assistant to be nearer the College. The acquaintance, how- ever, with the Greshams had never dropped, and Helen was bidden as a matter of course to ;the picnic at Studley Park, though the mother guessed perfectly that Charley would propose to her if he got the chance. Helen was, the prettiest girl at the picnic ; a slender creature with dark, star-like blue eyes and masses of soft brown hair. She had no relatives in Australia, she had kept herself for , more than two years, and worked sufficiently hard to do it, yet she looked quite different from the ordinary run of girls who earn their own livelihood. There was something high bred and distinguished about her and even Mrs, Gresham, who had known Mr. Jardine, who was a poor bookkeeper and often tried her patience by being behindhand with rent, his yet admitted that his daughter 'looked like princess." Charles Gresham waited for Helen's answer with an eager hungry expression in his dark eyes. It was quite true he had loved her ever since he knew her; seven years ago she and her father had corn e from Sydney and taken up their abode at Mrs. Gresham's. Helen was a bright- eyed fairy of thirteen then, and Charles a youth just home from his first voyage, but she had been his one object in life ever since, and to win her for his very own was his ambition in life. "I don't love you," said the girl sloi,vlp "Charles, you know I like you as a friend, but I am not in the least bit in love with you, and I don't want to marry anyone." "You are much too pretty to be an old maid," retorted the sailor; "so long as there is no one else you like better I am not afraid. It's all very well now, Helen, when you are young and full of energy, and teaching seems just pleasant employ ment, but when you've been at it year after year till you are thirty, you'll begin to tire of it and want a 'home of your own: you'll be sorry then you sent aWay the man who loved you better than his own life. Look here, Nell, my next voyage is to be a sh9rt one, only six months ; let us be engaged now and /when I come back you'll have got used to the idea of being married. You're such a shy, wild bird you won't' let a fellow show your how much he cares; just promise to be my wife and see if during the next six months that I am away your heart does not turn to me." " It's an awful risk, Charley," she said wistfully, " but if your test fails and still feel I don't want to marry you, you'll have to release me from my promise." It was a lonely part of the park, and there was fib one to see the sailor put his arm round her waist and seal the compact with a kiss; no one to see the tremor which passed through the slender frame as though already she felt she had made a mistake and would ha% e taken back her promise if she only could. " You are mine now," said Charley. fondly ; "my little wife that will be ; I shall look forward to my next homecoming as I never did before, for I shall be coming home to you." They sauntered back to tne rest of the guests. Mrs. Gresham's quick eyes saw what had happened, and she managed a word alone with Helen. "So that you make my boy happy I am content," she said gravely.. " You are a Lucky girl, Helen, to have won a heart like his.' But Helen did not feel at all lucky ; she was thankful that she and Charles had to drive home with a waggonette load of other people 30 that further lo %e-making was impossible ; she was not happy or content in her engage- ment, for she felt rather as though a con- ditional promise had been eittorted from her by Gresliam's persistence, as though the meshes of a net had closed round her, robbing her of her freedom, and she was only certain of one thing—that she did not love Charles Gresham in the least as he loved her. Mrs. Gresham kept Helen to " see the last of Charles," and so it was evening when the girl reached the quiet lodgings which she shared with Miss jasper, the music mistress at St. Monica's College.</p>
        <p>Hildred Jasper was a grave, wistful-faced poor little school-teacher,. am ' mistress of girl, more beautiful many would have thow,lif Mandate Hall, in Yorkshire, and. ten thottsan.! than Helen, and the strangest thing about this friendship was that it was the younger a year. Don't 'think me a hateful huh wretch to be glad when my *good fortunc girl who played the part of garetaker and Comes rine . lt new •comes through my grandfather's death. protectress, but then Helen Jad " " I don't think you a wretch at all." %aiC Melbourne well and how to get the nimasi %alue for her money, While Miss 'Jasper was Mildred. " But, oh, how I,sball miss you ! " lately out from England and st- • ,an to ge „-" you'll come, too," • 'said. Helen tenderly Colonial life. tI declare I'll stay in Melbourne and go or teaching at ISt. Monica's .unless you agree tt Her salary was larger than Helen's. and vet leave Australia and go with me to England she had suffered many hardships and dis- comforts before her ,friend proposed they "But / haven't come Into 'a. fortune , Helen." should " chum " togetlwir. She loved 'Heleii " Look here," said ,Helen gravely ;'• " I with a warm devoted affection, but she never told the younger girl the story of her bin . mean every word I say. I'd rather give nT there had been trouble and pain in it lIelei; my felt sure, and some bitter sorrow had driven fortune than lose you. :\ Ir. :Moly w Mi. il. the beautiful . musician away from her native writes that he is quite satisfied ith is :dm ray's evidence of my identify (fancy his toI land ; but she knew no details. 3 -et They were a great contrast, and ling her everything befolv me) 311,1 ilia: he . empowered to advancii' me three himilr, strangely enough the same description would have applied to both : " Blue eves and wavy have for my 'outfit ~and passage ruo.,-•• That will be' ample for you to come. toc, .. brown hair, good complexion ' and . slender if you won't be just my visitor, let me call )..,' figure." Those words described both the friends, and yet those who knew them would my companion, and pay you the sante iir ,N as you have at St. Monica's. You are Ettglt.i; have declared they saw no resemblance. Helen Jardine had the deep-blue eves. which born and bred, you haven't been out a '. and you know all about English ways an are the exact shade of a purple customs, so that you will .be abie. to help ii pansy, and , " her soft hair had a glint of gold among the to pick t brown. She was slender, but only with the that you will be invaluable tome if only a few years hence hem up. Hildred, never ime iiii y'.) thinness of early youth ; will cast in your lot with mine." • d into a graceful roundness. Mildred Jasper's ey es There is just One thing you have for she would probably have develope - gotten, Nell," said the elder girl thoughtfully were the real Irish eyes of blue-grey " your engagement with Charles Greslia.n." , "It was only a half-engagement'" proteste( fringed with very dark lashes, her pal colourless skin was as fair as Helen's thougeh 1 Helen. "He asked me to try it fof ---ii months, surely he can't refuse to let in, it lacked her wild rose bloom, and Hildred's t brown hair was so dark that many people I claim my freedom. You ,!Sbe, Hildred, I niT. going to England, and—Oh, he must releas( would have thought it black. She was too 1 thin for health; Helen told her that she fretted ; me.' . . Mildred Jasper knew it was no mercenar, the flesh off her bones ; she was not emaciated, I ' but she was fragilely slender. , i spirit which made the girl speak, nor was it because she was now far richer than Mr. Mildred was sitting by, the [round table in Gresham, only she wanted her freedom., She their little parlour, copying a piece, of music, had been persuaded into the ' engagement and looked up to welcome Helen with a smile, against her will and once free from Mr. " Did you have a good time, dear ? Was Gresham's presence she regretted her reluc- the picnic as pleasant as you expected ? " I wish to goodness I had never gone to it,,, taut consent and longed to break her bonds. " said Helen frankly. " Oh, Hildred, why . . didn't you let me stay at home with you ? " CHAPTER If Mildred Jasper put one hand caressingly MONEY smoothes things Wonderfully and on Helen's pretty head as she aiked, " What 'Makes difficulties lights The lawyers, Mel- is it, dear? lam quite sure something has vine and Brett, were only too -glad to be of happened to trouble you ? " use to Miss Jardine of Alandale; Miss Murray, And Helen told her. She kept back nothing who loved the girl dearly, went with her to</p>
        <p>their office, and told them 05 her nesire to engage her late fellow-teacher Hildred Jasper as her companion. They thou^t it an excel- lent arrangement that the heiress.should have a friend with her on the long voyage and declared that Lady Mary Jardine, who was Helen's guardian under the Earl's will, was not likely to make the least objection ; if the sum first mentioned was not sufficient for the double passage and expenses they would not hesitate to advance more. Miss Murray, however, thought it ample. For- tunately a month still remained of the holi- days so she would have time to secure two new teachers in place of the frimds she was generously releasing without notice. One other suggestion she made to the lawyers for which Helen was intensely grateful, viz., that, as Miss Jardine had no wish to be the subject of newspaper paragraphs or to have her romantic story in everyone's mouth, no news of her great inheritance should be published in the city which liaci been her home so long and that to guard against her meeting any of her former acquaintances she should travel overland to Adelaide, whence she could sail for London in a vessel which had not touched at Melbourne.</p>
        <p>Everything went smoothly and within ten days of that eventful picnic Helen Jardine and Hildred Jasper found themselves on board the good ship " Cygnet," whose only Aus- tralian stopping place on the homeward voyage would be Fremantle, a towu where neither of the friends had 'ever been'. " It is just like a fairy tale." Said Helen as they sat on the sofa in their little cabin for a rest before unpacking. " 1 keep thinking it must be some dream that won't last., Hil- dred, I have longed to go back to England ever since I can remeMber, just because father loved it so, and you who only left the old country' last year, how glad you'll be to get back to it and see your friends." " I have no friends," said Hildred Jasper. "Helen, you have been very good to me in taking me upon trust and asking no ques- tions about my past, but now that .1 am to be your companion and meet your people I think I ought to tell you something about myself. " I was engaged,'y said Hildred, " engaged when I was a mefe child, and nearly four years ago I-I lost my lover. ' I was almost beside myself for weeks, and then a good Samaritan took me as het secretary and tried to comfort me, but I felt I was like a shadow on her happy home, and I think even she realised at last I was a failure. Still, she did not give me up; she found out that I had a real gift for music, and paid my expenses at a college where my. talent could be cultivated. I passed my last exam. a week before she died ; then I knew I must .begin to earn my living at once, so I applied for the post at St. Monica's, then being advertised in the scholastic papers." The friends unpacked and arranged their belongings in the little cabin ; they both wore mourning. Miss Murray had decided that aTs Hildred was to reside at Alandale Hall it was advisable for her to .put on black as a mark of respect for the family's bereavement. Helen had refused to wear crepe on her dresses for the grandfather she had rkever seen, and as everything had to be bought ready made, it ended in her things being almost identical with her companion's. " I call it delightful," said Miss Jardine, "it will be just one long picnic till we reach England." But Hildred could not share her sentiments. She was emphatically a bad sailor, and by the time the " Cygnet ' had been ia motion two hours she. was so hopelessly ill that she took. refuge in her berth, leaving Helen to go into dinner without her. , The stewardess was kind and attentive, Helen was unremitting in her sympathy, but for all that it was full three days after leaving Adelaide before Hildred felt well enough to go. on deck though they assured her the fresh air would be her best remedy. Helen looked a trifle embarrassed when her friend was ready to leave the cabin.</p>
        <p>" I want you to do me a great favour, Hildred," she said coaxingly. "Now promise before I tell you what it is." " I can't promise in. the dark, Helen, but you know I'll do my best." . .</p>
        <p>The heiress blushed and then explained how on the first night of the voyage when she ap- peared at dinner, the captain and the others qf his table had taken it for granted that she was Miss Jasper and that Miss Jardine was confined to her cabin by illness. "But ru_set:them right, dear?" -</p>
        <p>. " No, I didn't," retuned the heiress. " I thought it the most delightful mistake. You know, Hildred, Miss Murray spoke to the 'captain about us and called us "Miss „Jardine, Lord Alandale's granddaughter, and her companion." Our Christian names were never mentioned, so if I call you Hildred and you say- Helen, the mistake will never be dis- covered." • . " But, Helen, it isn't right ; it is deceiving people, and--and 1 don't like masquerading as an heiress." " Hihlred, do it for my sake let me have just these It'ls? weeks of gladness without having to woader if people are nice to me because" of my fortune.' She spoke with a strange seriousness; which went to Hildred's-heart. " am quite sure you have some reason for this, .Helen, but I can't guess what it is." " Well, there is someone on board very nice whom I should like to make friends with, Hildreci, and he simply detests heiresses." " Did he tell you so ? " - -A; - .</p>
        <p>" Not in so many words, but he inferred it when he condoled with me or being com- panion to the rich Miss Alandale. Of course, I told him you were the dearest girl in the world and we were like sisters, but--" " But you let him go on thinking you Miss Jasper ? _</p>
        <p>- "'Yes. Ilihired, you Won't betray me, will you? I shall be so ashamed if you do that I shah not ehjoy the voyage one little bit." " But, Htlen--what about Mr. Gresham? I don't think you ought to take so much interest in a stranger when you are engaged." " I am not engaged," said Helen frankly. " I have written to tell Charles 1 am leaving Malbourne and t claim my freedom as I feel sure :no happiness would 'come 'from' a mar- riage -without iove on both sides. I am perfectly fru% flihlrcd ; besides, Mr. Stuart and I are only If'ends."</p>
        <p>The two girls found a very pleasant corner on de* where their chairs c9uld stand, and there Hiliired, Ni-as proppcd up- with pillows and soon declared she left much better for the air And ,change. Helen sat by her for some time a_nd.then took a stroll on her -own account frcim wi.ich she rttermd accompanied by a tall, Aistingui:lled-loohi4, man whom she introduced to her fri.nd as Mr. Stuart. Ronald Stuart did not 'look more than thirty, he had regular features, and dark, expressive grey eyes—in a word "handsome" was the best term .to describe him, while his manners had a polish and , fascination which were a happy contrast to Charles Grcsnam's free and easy behaviour. _</p>
        <p>It he hated 'heiresses as a. general thing, Ronald Stuart was perfectly polite to this particular one, perhaps because she was Helen's friend. .</p>
        <p>I am SO, glad to see you on deck, Miss Jardine. I hope it is a sign that you are better."</p>
        <p>Hilary(' found that Mr., .Stuart's seat was next theirs at table and that he was foe of the most popular and sought-after passengers. She telt, it is true, a vague anxiety at ,the extent ot his intimacy with Helen, but she was faithful to her promise and never at- tempted to betray the deception practised on him ; perhaps she felt that after once letting Ronald Stuart adtiress her friend as Miss Jasper she had crossed the rubicon and retreat was impossible. . . . -</p>
        <p>hired used to watch Helen and Ronald Stuart and wonder what would be the end of their acquaintance. That he was deeply attracted she felt sure, while the girl seemed another creature in his presence, a totally new Helen. Hiltlred felt little doubt Mr. Stuart had won the heart Charles Gresham had failed to touch. but what would be the .end of it ? Sooner or later the truth must come Out; would his bye survive the know ledge of Helen's deception'</p>
        <p>"Dear," pleaded the elder girl, " woulant, it be better if you let me tell Mr. Stuart the truth? I can explain to him that it was really a mistake at first and after you had let it pass uncorrected you did not like to put it right. Only think, Helen, he must find out the deception some time, and every day's delay makes it worse."</p>
        <p>" Don't rob me of this happy time, Hildred," pleaded Helen. "This voyage is just like a touch of Paradise for me; don't spoil it by telling Ronald I have deceived him." "Ronald ! " If it had got to Christian names, surely it .was time . to interfere, but Hildred could not resist her friend's entreaty. After all, she tried to persuade herself, the deception had nothing mercenary about it; Helen had not pretended to be richer than she was, but poorer': surely, oh surely Ronald Stuart would forgive her. - There was no breath of quarrel bytween the two girls Hildred was glad to .1 remember afterwards ; she never spoke one harsh. word to Helen, they were just as intimate and confidential as they had ever been, only that there was one subject which they both entirely avoided, the time when they must say good-bye to Ronald Stuart. "Even if he finds out the truth and can't \ , forgive me," said Helen Wistfully one night, "I shall be glad I met him. He's the one in the whole world to me ; it's just as though I had been longing for him and waiting for him always." Hildred choked back a sob. She, too, had had a roniance in her life, a romance which had brought her keenest pain ; what she had told Helen of her past was true and yet not the whole truth ; she had lost her lover, but the loss was not by death. The man she had once thought a hero lived still she feared, and that she should fear it , instead of hoping it showed pretty plainly what havoc he had made of her faith in him. It was late before she slept, and it seemed to her she could not have lost consciousness of the outer world more than an hour when she was roused by an almost suffocating smell of Fire. She climbed out of her berth and tremblingly opened the cabin-door. A rush af smoke nearly blinded her; then she knew the 'truth ; the " Cygnet " was on fire, and almost at the same moment the alarm bell rang out to rouse the sleeping passengers. Hildred dressed herself and Helen, for the I latter was • too frightened to be capable of I effort, then she wrapped a thick golf cape round her friend, and having put what few trifles Helen most prized into a handbag, htd her up on deck. • it was a strange sight .that met their eyes; the sun had not yet risen, but the sky was red- dened by the flames which rose up from' the "Cygnet," the passengers stood in huddled groups, the men with faces calm and set, the women. mostly a little pale and tearful as they :lasped their children to their hearts. Mr. 'Stuart crossed over and stood next Helen, and in all the agony of the moment Hildred noticed the action and tqok comfort from it. The fire had begun in the hold and had unfortunately become so fierce before it was discovered that it was impossible to save the thip. The captain's voice rang out in a few manly words as he explained this and added that he had given orders for the boats to be lowered ; there would be room he hoped for all the passengers, but the women and children were to go first- with only sufficient sailors in each boat to ensure its safe naviga- tion.</p>
        <p>Ronald Stuart, an expert oarsman and from natural temperament a born ruler of men, was asked by the captain to* make one of the oarsmen of the second boat ; many of his crew were foreigners and perhaps the brave officer reflected that he did not care to trust a party of women and children to their care without an English gentleman to, see that they were faithful to their trust. The first officer was in command of the largest boat, the earliest launched, but either it was overladen or the sailors had not taken the violence of the sea sufficiently into count ; anyhow, as it started it was suddenly turned over by the surging water ; some few of the passengers were rescued and hauled back into the ship, but the majority perished. And it was thu accident whizth made Hildred draw back when the second boat was ready. She saw Helen seated in it, and knew that Ronald Stuart was going to be there and would protect her with hi si own life if possible ; the number of those rescued from 'the capsized boat had made it plain that there would not be room for all in the few craft remaining; most of the passengers yet left had children depending on them, or were husbands whose wives would be heart-broken if forced tc leave the burning ship without them ; but nc one wanted Hildred. Helen loved her, but Helen would soon have other ties. If she stood back and let some other take het place it might mean the saving of some woman from despair. The boat started; Helen missed her friend' from her side and uttered a passionate cry of regret, but it was too late, no one would face the peril of turning back ; the boat sailed away and'Hildred was alone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CHAPTER Md.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER Md.</p>
        <p>A STATE ROOM of a large pleasure yacht which was returning to England afttr a few weeks' cruise -in summer seas, the stewardess and doctor anxiously watching the berth on which reclined a girl who had. never once recovered consciousness or memory since ten days before she was rescued truni the burning " Cygnet " with the captain. halt a dozen male passengers and the remnant oh the Crew.</p>
        <p>" I hope we may save her," said Dr. Story to Mrs. Ward, the stewardess. " The captain of the ' Cygnet ' says she was the only woman passenger lett on board and gave up her place in the boat in order that a wile and husband might not be parted. It seems to me that the life of a woman capable of such a sacrifice is worth a struggle." . " And you've done your utmost, I'm sure, doctor," said Mrs. Ward warmly. " They do say that she's a great heiress, Miss Jardine, of Mandate in Yomshire.",</p>
        <p>Suddenly the girl feebly moved her head and opened her eyes ; beautiful eyes of tender grey blue with black, brows and lashes. She looked round her in a bewildered way. " This is not our c,bir " sh said wonderingly-, " and, oh I where is—"</p>
        <p>She stopped abruptly ; surely memory had. Come bacx—the burning ship, the separation from her friend, the perdi oi that awful day and night on which she waited on board the " Cygnet," tor what seemed to her certain death. " I see that you remember," said Dr. Story very quietly. Miss Jardine, if it is any comfort to you; to know it, we took Ott everyone on hoard. The captain stood by his Ship to, the last, and the burning ship was actually going down when he came on board the ' Stella.' "</p>
        <p>" And the boats ? " ,breathed Hihired ; " I know the iirst was upset, but tne others ? " " When we touciic;cl at Naples the third boat had arrwed just a few hours before," the doctor assured her, and all on board were well,"</p>
        <p>"But the second—oh, sir, tell me the truth ; did she get sate to land ? " _ ' ' " ' ' "- •</p>
        <p>Dr. Story hesitated, then he felt that per- haps the truth was best. " The officer in charge of the last boat told Captain Maitland they had sighted the 'other boat a day after they left the ' Cygnet, she was floating bottom upwards and there can be no doubt that she capsized in a gale and all on board were lost."</p>
        <p>" How could you tell her, sir ? asked the stewardess a little later, "it was enough to kill her."</p>
        <p>" She can bear it better now when' she is too physically weak to realize it," he said simply. " Perfect rest and quiet are essential for ner, and if I had not told her her mind would have been racked by hOpes and fears." The Stella" would not reach England till a fortnight after the time at which the ill-fated " Cygnet " was due, as she had to call at Marseilles and Gibraltar, 'But from Naples the captain had cabled to the owners a full account of the loss of the ship, with the names of the passengers rescued I I,vith himself: a letter sent from the same port</p>
        <p>gave a list or those he helievea to have lost their lives either by the capsizing of the first boat as Soon as launched, or in the seccnd after she left the ship! Dr, Story had been right in thinking the invalid would feel the news of her friend's death easier to bear than the cruel uncertainty, but he little knew the agony of doubt which attacked the girl. She heard herself ad- dressed by all on board as "Miss Jardine " ; she knew that Helen was lying fathoms deep beneath the waves, that she would never again- need her name and place in the world, and Hildred needed both.</p>
        <p>Poor, lonely girl, on leaving Melbourne with the heiress she had, as it were, burnt her boats behind' her ; she could not return even it the passage money were forthcoming to St. Monica's because her place there was already filled her benefactress who had paid for her musical training was dead ; even if she could have brought herself to appeal to this lady's relatives for help it would have been uselesFi they would have blamed her too much for throwing up the post in Melbourne to help her to another.</p>
        <p>Besides, and this thought haunted her night and day, Helen was dead; there was no one nOw to free her from blame in that change of name. Helen would have taken all the blame of the deception on herself, but her relatives were not likely to believe that the penniless companion had not been anxious to pose as the heiress.</p>
        <p>Her own identity was stolen from her, she could not apply to Lady Mary Jardine for help, saying /she had been engaged by Helen as ,her companion and promised a salary of eighty pounds a year, and at the same time confessing that on board the 't Cygnet" she had borne Helen's name. When she reached England she would be penniless save for a fair'. shillings which she happened to have in the pocket of her serge dress ; her whole wardrobe had been lost with the doomed ship ; she had not so much as a hat and coat in which to face the chills of an English March. When she landed she would be homeless, friendless, destitute.</p>
        <p>And ,the alternative was so easy, so pain- fully easy. Lady Mary Jardine was ex- pecting her niece, the heiress of Alandale, and everyone on board the " Stella " was ready to identify Hildred as the Miss Jardine who- had left Adelaide on the " Cygnet. ' She had not even to tell a lie; just by holding her tongue she would gain a home, a name and. fortune. "Of course," thought the distracted girl, "Alandale Hall and all the money must belong to someone else now Helen is dead, but she loved me better than her unknown relatives ; she would like to help me; why shouldn't I let things be just as they are for a little time till I have saved enough for a fresh. start? it would only be robbing the new owner of Alandale for a little while and it is the only course to save me from the work- house." ,</p>
        <p>She, grew stronger rapidly as they, neared England, and she was able to come on deck and join the other passengers at meals. In the saloon everyone called her "Miss Jardine," people vied with each other in kindly, attentions to her, and Captain Mait- land told her she was the bravest woman he had ever sailed with. Yet, still she had not made up her mind to go on with the decep- tion ; still she hesitated whether on reaching Southampton she should go boldly on to Alandale or write a confession of the decep- tion to Helen's aunt. And after all the choice was taken out of 4her hands. When the " Stella " reached Plymouth she' was sitting on deck looking at the white cliffs of old England and wonder- ing why in spite of all she had suffered there, in spite of the ever-present dread of future- trouble at her heart, she was glad to see her native land once more. She never dreamed of anyone coming to meet her at Southampton, much less Plymouth, so she sat quietly on the 'quarter-deck, her eyes on the deep blue water, taking absolutely no notice of the people who . had put off from shore in a little boat and presently boarded the " Stella " ; she , never dreamed that they could concern her she had had never troubled when the mails came on board to ask whether there was a letter .for her.</p>
        <p>. 1</p>
        <p>, She looked up suddenly to see Captain Maitland at her side and with him a tall fair-haired man with dark lustrous eyes and. a grave earnest face. " Miss Jardine," said the sailor in a strange- ly earnest voice, " this is your cousin, the. Earl of Alandale, who has come to welcome. you to England. -I can only tell you, Lord Alat,dale," turning to the stranger, " that your cousin is the bravest. girl who has ever. sallPd with me."</p>
        <p>. , Alandale put out his hand and took Hildred's while he spoke a few kindly words ot greeting, and she ,found herself answering him yist as though she were really and truly Helen Jardine. "feu minutes later she was sitting by his 'side in a little boat which was:. .rowed, swiftly towards shore ; the die waa east, the rubicon crossed—she had robbed the dead. •</p>
        <p>\Vas she sorry ? For tin; first - half-hour she could not have told, but when, after lunching at the nearest hotel, she/passed down the railway platform on the Earl's arm on her way to the London Express something happened which decided her.</p>
        <p>"`What is the matter ? " asked Lord Man- dale as she suddenly turned deadly pale. How could she attswer him, how tell him the tteith that in the crowd she had seen a face he knew and dreaded, the too well-remem- bered face of the man who had blighted her life and whom she had prayed ne9r to see again. A moment before 'she had longed to confess all and throw herself on the Earl's compassion, brit at sight of the man she hated she hardened&amp; her heart and resolved to go on with her decep- tion to the bitter end.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
