      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FAMION-HOME.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATIONS AT COURT.</p>
        <p>Their Majesties the King and Queen will hold a series of Courts at Buckingham Palace during the coming season, at which presentations of ladies to their Majesties will be made. Attend- ance and presentation at these Courts will be by summons issued by the Lord Chamberlain. Ladies who have already been presented, and who are desirous of being summoned to these Courts, are now requested to send in their names, together with those of their husbands (if they desire to be accompanied by them, and if they have been presented), to the Lord Cham- berlain's office, St. James's Palace. Should they desire to make a presentation, the name of the lady to be presented should be sent in at the same time. A lady who makes a presenta- tion to their Majesties must be personally ac- quainted with and responsible for the lady she presents. She must herself attend the Court, and cannot present more than one lady in addition to her daughter or daughter-in-law. If it should not be convenient for a lady to attend or be presented at the particular Court to which she is summoned, it will be open to her to make her excuses to the Lord Chamberlain in writing, when her name can, if desired, and if 'possible, be transferred to another list.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CHARMING EVENING DRESS.</head>
        <p>CHARMING EVENING DRESS.</p>
        <p>Dainty filmy dance frocks are quite the order of, the day, particularly for young girls and debutantes. Our illustration pictures a charming little evening dress of spotted net suitable for ball or simple Cinderella, and quite inexpensive in material and fashioning. This pretty frock is peculiarly suited to a girl or woman with an ex- tremely alight figure, as it is exceedingly full.</p>
        <p>The Übe bodice has four or five rows of gaug- ing outlining the décolletage, similar rows of gauging forming the high waistbelt. Two shoulder straps of guipure connect back and front of the bodice, and serve as a hold for the puffed elbow sleeves, which are transparent and finished with wee frills. The simple, very much gathered skirt is absolutely plain except for a wide band of guipure inserted about eight or ten inches above the hem.</p>
        <p>THE MATRIMONIAL MARKET. Someone has been going boldly into figures over the matrimonial market. We are -Mid that there are not more than three hundred eligible bachelors in the West End of London, and but a "limited choice" of desirable unmarried men in society. A sense of gallantry has, no doubt, re- strained the same authority from telling us how many thousands of agitated, speculative spinsters are outbidding one another in order to snap up the present limited consignment of the desirable male, says "Miranda." and if more hopeful quotations are not forthcoming we shall certainly have to be prepared to face the awful possibility of a "corner" in bachelors. OvER-ZEALOITS POLITICIANS.</p>
        <p>It is regrettable that several feminine pon• ticians should have been ejected from meetings, says a ladies' paper. Incidents of that kind put back the "woman's cause" more certainly than any amount of mere masculine prejudice. The best friends of women will be the first to regret that it is to women that some of the most irri- tating heckling of candidates has been due. Of a surety these over-zealous politicians in petti- coats are doing the very worst possible service to the cause of Women's Suffrage. As it is, there are scarcely any Members of Parliament in favour of it, and if women take to political nagging," as I beard a man call it, by and bye there will be none. AFTERNOON BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>Our sketch shews an afternoon blouse carried out in cream cashmere, with trimmings of tucked Japanese silk. The bodice part is per- fectly plain, having the fulness gathered into tucks in front; and a collar of the tucked silk. edged with embroidered silk frills, is laid round the shoulders, as sham), in the sketch. The</p>
        <p>sleeves consist of a short puff and a band of ver- tical tucks reaching to the elbow, where a pretty finish is given by the double frills of the em- broidery; while the under-slpeves arc of tucked silk to match the collar. These under-sleeves could be made detached if so preferred; then they could be removed without trouble, so that the blouse could be worn in the evenings. QUEEN'S PRESENT TO JAPAN'S EMPRESS.</p>
        <p>- ------- Prince Arthur of Connaught carries with him a present which Queen Alexandria is sending personally to the Empress of Japan. It is the latest portrait of the Queen herself, with her beautiful little Japanese dog - Togo " on her knee, and is framed in a long, panel-shaped frame of dull gold, the, corners being all of another coloured burnished gold. It is sur-• mounted with the Royal crown, the Queen's own monogram depending therefrom by a rib- bon all of diamonds and rubies. This frame the Queen specially designed herself, and had made purposely for the portrait she sent to the Em- press of Japan, which she signed in the corner.</p>
