      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>NECKLACES.</head>
        <p>NECKLACES.</p>
        <p>The pretty strings of gold beads are already, like those of stained mother-o'-pearl, becoming too popular, but silver beads and those of gun- metal (or, as it is more fashionable to call it, platinum, whether it be platinum or not) make a nice change, says the Queen, especially if they have a dull, "frosted," or oxydised surface. Strings of copper, Or, at any rate, copper. coloured, beads are pretty and uncommon. They should all be graduated in size; they are much more effective, and inclined to "look good," as the children say. Perhaps. the new discovery of branches of black coral in profusion, hitherto such a rarity, may give us necklaces of that as well as red, pink, and white! By the way, white cornelian is charming, and all these "stone" beads, if strung upon gold thread with a knot between each and a loop and knob as fastening, Indian fashion, are delightful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>FILLETS OF PHE4BANT.—Roast a couple of pheasants. remove the white meat, and mince the remainder with a little chopped truffle, a- few cooked mushrooms, salt and pepper. Place the bones pf the pheasants in a saucepan with two glasses of white vine, any trimmings of trollies which may he left over, a leis, shallots and a bay leaf. Allow it to reduce three- quarters. then strain through a cloth,•. adding to the sauce two spoonfuls of clarified stock. Place half the sauce in a saucepan with the minced pheasant, and stir well over a slow fire till it comes to the boil, then strain and place to cool. Cut, the white meat—which has been put aside —into neat slices, and dress on an entree dish. placing a piece ot truffle between eacn ntiet Pour the puree of game into the centre and mask the whole wit h the remainder of the sauce, garnishing with a border of clear aspia</p>
        <p>, , ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, January 27,.1906. SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 27,.1906. SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>I lIISSITY'S PORTABLE BlLLlARD9.—Scientifically con- A TEST FOR TURPENTINE. 1 structed, perfectly accurate, excellent workmanshiP To test the purity of turpentine drop a small superior finish. bit.. 24 10s. ; 6ft., £5 15s. ; Vt.. R. 7 58. L quantity on a piece of white paper and expose Obtain from local ayent the Special Catalogue of GEO. Gi- • to the air. No trace will be left if the turpen- BUBSEY &amp; Co., Billiard Manufacturers. London. tine is pure; but if it contains oil or other i foreign matter the paper will be greasy. 1 NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>HELIUM A PERMANENT GAS.</p>
        <p>Helium proves, as yet, to be the one perma- nent gas.. Professor Olszewski has suddenly re- leased it from a pressure of 180 atmospheres at the temperature where hydrogen is about to solidify, but finds no sign of its liquefying, although the temperature developed was esti- mated at 271 deg. below zero cent. This tem- perature is within 2deg. of absolute zero.</p>
        <p>WEIGHT LOST BY HEATING. Whether a body gains or loses weight on heating, or neither, is a problem of possible im- portance. A comparison of the attraction of two bodies at different temperatures can hardly be made, and Professor Poynting has tried the plan of weighing a body when cold and again when heated. With great care, it was shewn that a solid body lost an infinitesimal amount of weight on being heated from 15deg. to 100 deg. C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>GOLD FROM THE OCEAN.</head>
        <p>GOLD FROM THE OCEAN.</p>
        <p>In the Belgian process for extricating gold from the ocean, a ton of water is treated with a spoonful of concentrated aqueous solution of chloride of tin, when the gold is converted into purple of cassius, and may be precipitated, to- gether with hydrate of magnesium and the ex- cess of tin, by adding a pound of slaked lime. i When this precipitate s dissolved in dilute solu- tion of cyanide of potassium, the gold may be precipitated with zinc or other reagent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>STRANGE FREAK OF LIGHTNING.</head>
        <p>STRANGE FREAK OF LIGHTNING.</p>
        <p>----- - During a storm on the Continent, lightning struck an, iron post in front of a house. The post was destroyed and a part of the kitchen. A knife in the cook's hand attracted the lightning and she was severely shocked. Tho main part of the bolt jumped from the post to a bed of white dahlias, in the centre of which stood a single red blossom. None of the white flowers was injured in the least, hut the red one and its stalk were completely wiped out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>LIFE IN THE PLANETS.</head>
        <p>LIFE IN THE PLANETS.</p>
        <p>--- Besides being an eminent composer, M. Saint-Saens has acquired distinction as an astro- nomer. He is a member of the Astronomical Society of France, and in a letter to M. Flana- merlon has raised again the interesting question as to other planets than the earth being in- habited. M. Saint-Saens is a strong advocate for the theory that they are inhabited, and M. Flaintnarion records in emphatic terms his agreement with M. Saint-Saens. As he ex- pressed it, the notion that life exists only on this earth is as narrow as would be the idea of a fish —were, a fish ',capable of an idea—that existence would be impassible out of water. As for the habitability of the planet Mars, M. Flammarion thinks the climate there to be more agreeable generally than the average known upon earth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>NEW AIR PURIFIER.</head>
        <p>NEW AIR PURIFIER.</p>
        <p>A new ," ozone generator" has been exhibited in Glasgow by its inventor, Mr. J. Richardson Craig, jun. It is intended for purifying the air in public institutions and houses and for the supply of ozone for all purposes. The apparatus is enclosed in a metal cylinder, through which a current of atmospheric air is drawn and brought in contact With a high tension electric current. The electric effluvia diffused generates ozone, which is forced out of the opposite end of the cylinder. The apparatus is also designed to dis- tribute ozone for ozonising the atmosphere. It is self-contained and is operated either by means of direct or alternating current electricity. It re- quires no auxiliary machinery, and has only to be attached to any part of the electric light cir- cuit by the use of a short length of flexible cord, with suitable connections. The generator does not involve any structural alterations in build- ings to which it may be fitted, and there is no interference with any existing heating ap- pliances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>TEMPERATURE OF THE SOIL..</head>
        <p>TEMPERATURE OF THE SOIL..</p>
        <p>- The temperature of the soil no less than its moisture and chemical constituents, exercise an influence on its crop-growing capacity. Among the advantages derived from the use of farmyard manure is the warmth it imparts to the soil, as well as the increased retention of moisture, . A series of useful experiments have been carried out with the view of ascertaining the difference of the temperature in cultivated and unculti- vated soils. The results indicate that plough- ing and fallowing promote a more. rapid ex- change of temperature. But the rise and fall of temperature were found to be considerably greater in the ploughed and mellowed soil than in the unploughed land, the difference between the mean temperatures of the two during the summer weather reaching two degrees and be- tween the extreme .maximum temperatures five and more degrees. On the other hand, minimum temperatures were lower on ploughed land than on unploughed by almost one degree.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>SOCIAL SPIDERS.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL SPIDERS.</p>
        <p>sSinrJyamefbuanasptheaeniesdeesfetsinbeeisal stpheiderhainbitas recent nurnber. of the Smithsonian Misc. Collec- andlrlife tions. This spider lives in a sponge-like nest of branching canals, often attached to the branch of a tree or the leaf of a prickly pear. In their habits these social spiders differ widely from the 'solitary and pugnacious species of this country, which do not tolerate the presence of .one of their own race. They live in communities of from forty to 100 individuals, males and females together. A number of them often c - operate in securing victims, and the food is shared without quarrelling. Among the spiders of our gardens the females are much larger than the males, and not infrequently devour them. The males and females of the social spiders, on the other hand, are almost alike in size and colour, and live happily together in the same nest. The devotion of the females to their off- spring is said almost to verge on self-sacrifice. A species of social spider has been recorded by Pickard from South Africa, and another from Venezuela by Simon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>VELOCITY OF LIGHT.</head>
        <p>THE VELOCITY OF LIGHT.</p>
        <p>Think of the velocity of an express railway train as it dashes past a platform. It seems to approach, to pass, and to vanish in a moment. Then, stand near a rifle range, and note how rapidly the impact of the bullet on the target succeeds the flash accompanying its discharge. The rifle-bullet takes but little more than a second to traverse the same distance which the ! train accomplishes in a minute. Rise one step higher: look at a meteor as it dashes into our atmosphere, to perish in a streak of splendour. 1 rho meteor moves far faster than any rifle-bullet —in fact, its velocity is nearly one hundred times greater than that of the missile from any weapon which human hands have ever fashioned. Surely, says The Story of the Sun," it would seem, at first sight; that the speed of a meteor must be as great as any speed whiels it would be possible for us to investigate? But we have not yet nearly reached the velocity which wt. have to • deal with in the case of luminous undulations. We have to make a far greater advance. Think 11 the speed at. which a little child runs across the room, and think of the speed with which a shooting star darts across the sky. Then work out the following sum in proportion: As the velocity of the shooting star exceeds the velocity with which a child can toddle, so does the velo- city of light exceed the velocity of the shooting' star.</p>
        <p>UNIMAGINABLE PERIODS OF TIME. It u.sed to be that astronomy. with Its stupen.- dous magnitudes, incredible velocities. and . in. conceivable distances, seemed to make the greatest demand on man's belief. To-day it is physics; but, says a Daily Telegraph writer, . there is a difference. Astronomy deals with the I immeasurably great, and physics astounds us with the almost infinitely little. We read, for instance. that Hertz's oscillations give rise to ! 500,000.000 oscillations per second,. Where is the man who can conceive of anything happening in, I the five hundred millionth part of a second? I But this is quite a long period compared to some of those now accepted as inevitable in optics. According to Maxwell's great theory, a light wave is a series of alternating electric cur- rents, flowing in the air, or interplanetary space, and changing their direction 1,000,000,000,000.000 times per second. And this ! is suppo,ed to be true of every form of light coming from the sun, the electric lamp, or a Lucifer match. Who can think of anything happening in the thousand million millionth part of a second? The reader to whom the I statement is new may perhaps say. "Thanks. 'tis enough; that way madness lies." Yet it ; agrees fairly well with what has long been ; known of light. Its velocity is about 186.000 miles per second, or nearly 12,000,000.000 inches, I and in each are crowded some 50.000 waves, I every one of which takes sonic time to nags over ! even an inch. Tune is ot all things external to In the most difficult to conceive; its sub- divisions, however great, must be real. All real and all conceivable, only ea necessitica tai _ . thought. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>RESULTS OF SATURDA.Y'S MATCHES.</head>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDA.Y'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P, G. T. P. Wigan 1 311 Leeds linnelet 3 1 9 Salford 1 1 5 Halifax St. Helens ° 8 28 Normanton 0 1 3 Brighouse Rangers 0 3 9 Pontefract Runcorn 4 7 29 Millom 0 0 0 Leigh 1 4 14 Widnes • 0 0 0 Warrington 43radford R'dale Hornets ... 1 3 11 Morecambe 0 0 0 Brought'n Rangers 2 4 16 Oldham 1 1 5 Huddersfield Wakefield Trinity 6 8 36 Castleford York Hull Kingston R... 2 7 25 Bramley</p>
