      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>Warrington Beat Swinton.</head>
        <p>Warrington Beat Swinton.</p>
        <p>There was a crowd of about 5,000 people to witness the match with Swinton at Warrington on Saturday,and much interest was evinced in the appearance of Ike Taylor (Widnes) in the home threequarter line. Swinton were at full strength, but Fish, Isherwood and Hocken- hall were still noticeable absentees from the home team. The weather was drizzly, and the ground in very heavy condition. Warrington: Berry, back; Widdeson, Taylor, Kenyon and Harris, three-quarter backs; Lloyd and .1. Davies, half backs; Boardman, Th3mas. Heath, Harmer, Preston, Swift, Naylor and Belton, forwards. Swinton: Dixon, back; Flynn, Wallwork, Smith and Oldfleld, three-quarter backs; D. Davies and MolYneux, half backs: Barnett, Simister, Lloyd, Cheetham, Evans,Hobson. Coletrtm and Yates, forwards. Referee, Mr. E. H. Smirk, Wigan. Touch judges Messrs. W. Gregory, Runcorn, and H. Horrocks, Warrington had the advantage of the breeze but Smith and Oldfleld by the ad of quick passing placed the visitors in a strong position. The Warrington for- wards, headed by Naylor and Preston, retaliated. and in quick succession Maris and Widdison were palled up at the corner. Dixon performed splendidly at back, but eventually he was brushed aside, and Heath scored, Preston missing the goal. Warrington maintained the upper band and Widdison got over, but the try was not allowed. Berry missed a couple of penalties, and at half-time Warrington led by 1 try (3 points) to Swinton nil. _ _ . . .</p>
        <p>Turning round Swinton showed up in promising fashion, but the home forwards retaliated, and Dixon had all his work cut out to prevent disaster. Wicldison had hard luck in being grassed at the corner, and after Smith had checked Boardman near the line Davies dropped a goal.Swinton next became dangerous through the medium of Davies, but little support was forth- coming, and .Warrington won by a goal and a try (5 points) to Swinton nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>RUGBY UNION.</head>
        <p>RUGBY UNION.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON IitATIE v. NORTH 11ANCITESTER. Played at Manchester on Saturday. The home team won the toss and Barker commenced for Stockton Heath againsta slight wind. After the usual exchanges a series of scrummages took place about midfield. From the base of the scram the ball was sent to Harling who finding it impossible to break through dropped at goal and favoured by the wind managed to put his side 4 points ahead. Stockton Heath now assumed the aggressive and made several assaults on the home line but failed to cross. The homesters by good forward play transferred the game to the visitors half but W. Davenport relieved. Stockton Heath were now doing the whole of the attacking snd had hard lines in not scoring. Half- time came with the score: North Manchester, 4 points ; Stockton Heath, nil. From the restart the visitors again assumed the attack, and the forwards who were controlling the scrums gave the backs plenty of chances. A score should have been registered when Daintith securing the ball about half way made a good run but be held the ball too long. The visitors still maintained the attauk, and from a scrum the ball was handed to B. Bradshaw, who beat his man and scored. K. Gibbs failed with a difficult place kick. North Manchester now paid a visit to the other end, but were soon repulsed, sad Stockton Heath kept up the attack, 'but failed to augment their score. Result: North Manchester, 1 dropped goal, 4 points ; Stockton Heath, 1 try, 3 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>THOUGHT SHE WAS PARALYSED.</head>
        <p>THOUGHT SHE WAS PARALYSED.</p>
        <p>A:Widow's Rheumatism Cured by Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>Rheumatism in all its forms is essentially a disorder of the blood. That is why rheumatism is successfully created by a course of Chas. Forde's Bile Beans, which purify, the blood by correcting and stimulating the natural functions of the liver. "The neighbours said I was done for,'" said Mrs. M. A. Nettle, a widow, living at 11, Barton Street, Liverpool Road, Manchester, to a "Manchester Weekly Chronicle" reporter. "I am 45 years of age, and for a number of years my life was made miserable by chronic rheumatism. T had terrible pains in my feet and legs, which prevented me from sleeping. At last I had to go about on crutches, for my legs were so swollen I dare not put any weight on them. I thought I was paralysed, and this distressed me so muck that I was reduced to sore straits through buying medicines. I was under treatment at the infirmary for some time, but my sufferings were not relieved in the slightest. "I was being carried about the house when a friend came in and recommended Bile Beans. They had cured her of ametnia. I bought a box of the genuine medicine. Wken these were done I was feeling much better; the pains were not so severe, and I could move about a little. I continued with the Beans, and after taking only four boxes my lameness had completely vanished, and I was able to lay aside my crutches. Now I cam do my housework with ease, for the pains are all gone. I am very grateful for what Bile Beans have done for me." _</p>
        <p>•-••• Chas. Forde's Bile Beans for Biliousness cure disease by helping Nature to put things right. What they did. in 31rs.tiettle's case the genuine medicine trill acecmplish in your case also. They are ars. excellent remedy for rneuruatism, lumbago, neuralgia, influenza, colds and chills, and are invaluable fo biliousness, indigestion, constipation, piles, amernia, female ailments, weakness, dizziness, insomnia, and liver troubles. Of all chemists, or post free from the Bile Bean Mfg. Co., Red Cross- street, London, E.C., for Is. lid.; or 2s. 9d. (containing three times the quantity). Beware of substitutes and imitations.</p>
