      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>FAMILY'S DEBT TO ZAM-BUK.</head>
        <p>FAMILY'S DEBT TO ZAM-BUK.</p>
        <p>Breadwinner's Eczema Ended.</p>
        <p>Mr. Michael James, of 6, Great Thompson-street, Witton Park, Durham, said to a local newspaper reporter :—" About three years ago I happened a slight accident, bruising the skin of my left leg. Some weeks later I found that some form of skin-disease was spreading to both my legs and parts of rev body. I stuck to my work (as a coke-drawer at the Ge orge Pit) as long as I could, but eventnally I was obliged to give up, and was laid up for fifteen weeks. got a little better, and worked for other eleven weeks, ,but then it broke out afresh, and I was off work for sixteen weeks. I consulted. a local doctor,and he told me I was suffering from eczema. I took three bottles of his medicine, but it did me no gmod, and the doctor him- self told me nothing would cure me. Irve had it myself for 25 years, off and on,' he said, 'and if I can't cure myself I can't cure yon ' ! I was in a dreadful condition. With the exception of my faceand neck, I was covered from head to foot with angry sores, some big ass saucer. My head was a mass of sores, and when the places peeled, large quantities of hair came away. Around my elbows the sores were so numerous and painful that I could not raise my hands to my head. For eleven whole weeks the itching was so irritating 1 and painful that I daren't face the warmth of a bed ,and had to sit or lie on a couch downstairs. "I went to a hospital on the other side of Durham city, travelling backwards and forwards fez., 22 weeks. The treatment I received sent the disease back a little, but it did not rid me of it, and it came back as had as ever, I had been so long off work that I could no longer afford to go to the hospital. "Eventually some Zam-Buk was given me. I just rubbed some on the back of my left hand, and next morning there was a decided difference. I continued the applications, washing my hands in oatmeal water, before using the Zam-Bnk. It did me so much good that in the course of a week or two I was able to go to work again, and with the first lot of money I got I bought a proper supply of the balm. I used about three' boxes, and by that time I was practically clear. Zara- -IBuk did not take more than six weeks to clear me of the disease altogether." Zam-Buk is a powerful remedy for eczema, bad legs, diseased ankles, inflammation, yieling or scaling skin, I sore backs, poisoned or festering sores, piles, psoriasis, Iscalp diseases, barber's rash, abscesses, boils, ringworm, ' chafed skin, colds, chapped hands, chilblains, Sze. Price Is. ILd. or 2s. 9d. per box (2s. 9d. size contains nearly , four times the Is. ; of all chemists, or the Zam- Buk Co., -1, Red Cross Street, London, E.C. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>FREE CHURCHMEN AND THE ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Naturally, the only topic this month is the Elections. The stupendous results of the pollings has indeed staggered the country. It would seem almost an unnecessary task to analyse the reasons for the tremendous defeat of the late Government. There can be no doubt, however, that one of the great issues which turned the opinion of the country was the Education Question. It will be remem- bered that it was at the time of the intro- duction of Mr. Balfour's ill-fated Educational proposals that the Bye-elections began to go against the Tory Government. Canvassers in the constituencies have found that the resent- ment against the Education Acts was as strong as over, and this largely contributed to unite the Free Churches in support of a,Government which promised to aniend this unjust legisla- tion. The National Free Church Council has been taking an active part in the struggle, and there can be no doubt that its efforts contributed to the result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>FREE CHURCH DEMONSTRATIONS.</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCH DEMONSTRATIONS.</p>
        <p>As soon as the Elections were fixed great Free Church Demonstrations were arranged in various centres. The Queen's Hall, London, meeting r ill long be remembered. In the East- end Dr. °afford had a most rousing reception, and in other districts enthusiastic meetings responded to the call. In the great provincial centres demonstrations were held, and these showed the unity and enthusiasm of the Free Churches in the struggle. Some millions of leaflets have been circulated dealing with all phases of the Education Question. Posters, illustrated by F.C.G., were also largely distri- buted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MOTOR CAR CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>MOTOR CAR CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>A unique feature of the National Free Church Council's propaganda was the running of a series of motor-cars with great Free Church leaders. The first of these conveyed the Rev. F. R. Meyer and Rev. Thomas Yates to the Western counties for a week. The tour was a great success. Crowds gathered en route, and open-air meetings were addressed. Big indoor meetings were held, and in all respects a spirit of fervour and enthusiasm was revealed which augured well for the Elections. The following week Mr. Meyer and Mr. Yates went on tour through the Kentish constituencies. The first day's run included meetings at Sevenoaks, Tonhridge, Tunbridge Wells, Hythe, and Folkestone. This day's record was typical of the tour. In many places the meetings were crowded, and occasionally it was necessary to hold overflow meetings. The next day Dr. Clifford started for the Eastern Counties, and in spite of some misfortunes with the motor-car, some very good meetings were held. He was joined later on in the tour by the Rev. G. Campbell Mor- gan, D.D. Another tour was that of the Rev. C. Silvester Horne, M.A., and Rev. J. D. Jones, M.A., DD., into-the Midland district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>FREE criuncrt</head>
        <p>FREE criuncrt</p>
        <p>The coming Parliament should be notable in many ways, and not in the least that it should contain a larger number of Free Churchmen than have appeared at St. Stephen's for some generations. It is too early as yet to say how many Free Churchmen will find places in the House, but the list is already growing. With four Free Churchmen in the Cabinet, others in the Ministry, and an able body among its supporters, the Free Church position should be well represented.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>mtssioNany coNrEfteNcE.</head>
        <p>mtssioNany coNrEfteNcE.</p>
        <p>In the midst of the political excitement, the National Free Church Council found an oppor- tunity of holding a meeting of quite unique a nature, and of quite a distinct type from those being held at the same time throughout the country. Dr. Horton, the President of the National Council, has always taken a great interast in missionary endeavour. He has had as one of his aims to make his year of office a missionary year, and to tale steps to link up the missionary work with the Free Church Councils. Early in the year a Missionary Committee was appointed, and quite recently an organising secretary has been appointed for tJo purpose of formiug Study Circles in all parts of .the country. in pursuance of his missionary aims, Dr. Horton held a Reception to Migsiorntri.s at the present timo on furlough in this comitrv. A lar,ie narthor of those mire pri tj,t at the Memori.d Hall, and a memoral,le Irc,l 1,7h.11 rosultoid. In the afternoon a C .i,fr.,uc,- if om,-wc of the various Missionary Stwietititi anti Couticil was bola to conti.lor work in new fields, when important la•epwails were made, of which more should be heard lati,r.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>HOPEFUL OUTLOOK.</head>
        <p>A HOPEFUL OUTLOOK.</p>
        <p>After tea, Dr. Horton, in the name of the Cm mei!, received the Missf. maries. They were introduced by the Seeretu ies of their re-peetive bodies. Dr. Horton affet wards gave an iiltere?..t- ing address, in whi.;lt li. ui te. I kelt roem features of the Ii oe Church Nioveruent home; how the Federation had welded tog&amp; hl,r the Free Church denominations, and how this new union and solidarity brought into. relief the great need for united missionary work. The Free '''..,hurch Council, he said, was trying to find a suitable sphere for its endeavorn. of course, they did not want to create a sepa- rate Missionary orgsnisation. much could he done in the way of stimulating and arousiug enthusiasm. He gave a short resmral of the position on the Mission field, saying that never in .the lifetime of those present was the outlook so hopeful. A few representative Missionaries from various fields then spoke. dwelling on the benefits of union and the signs ot revival. It is under:4(RA that this Conference will be repeated in fuiorf3 years.</p>
        <p>RE-UNION PROSPECTS.</p>
        <p>The prospects of re-union, despite the bitter- ness occasioned by Educational strife. annex;</p>
        <p>to be not so remote as to no unconsidered. Of course, re-union among the Free Churches is proceeding apace. This month's _nee Church Chronicle gives information of a remsrkable union just effected between the Congrega- tionalists, Methoilists, and Presbyteriaus io Canada.</p>
        <p>ENO LISII RE-UNION.</p>
        <p>As to the prospects in England of a wider union, the colorful Dr. Horton has just published will he read with intense intereht. It is entitled " Thu Re-union of Errglir=h Christendom?? In the introduction Dr. Horton says:•• The writer, as years go on, feels that there is no end more worth striving for than rbe solidarity of those who or the name of. Christ. If be saw a church -u liili would make all Christendom one, he trould certainly enter in. and waive his private predilections. , But if he were to enter the Roman Church be would Ire compelled to unchurch all the most enlightened and the most progressive part of Christendom, and to harden his heart against all that is best and noblest in .the history of his country. If he were to enter the Church of England, he would,hecome a member of a comparatively small and exclusive Church, which, in return for being unchurched by the great Western Church, michurches all other Churches except the one that unchurches her. Where, then, is tiler way of unity and peace to be sought? Only in that idea of the Free Churches, which recognises the diversity of operations and the one Spirit, and can give a true meaning to the great Pauline principle of 'one Body, one Spirit, one Lord, one faith, one baptism,' because it does not demsnd ecclesiastical uniformity as the condi- tion of Christian unity."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Mr. Dam and the Proposed Alterations.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Mr. Dam and the Proposed Alterations.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present :—Messrs. T. Baxter, J. Balshaw, H. Eyes, P. Mounfield, G. Egerton Warburton, J. Stokes, C. Roylance, C. E. Linaker. J. H. Davies, .1. Smith, T. Wright, H. Mawdsley, T. Guest, S. Woodward, George Pilling, S. Horton, J. L. Jones, E.Bretherton, J.Spruce, W. M. Morris, T. Wright, J. Rutter, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, W. Weir, A. G. Guest, T. Antwis, G.F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), Cook, de Prez and Pearson (relieving officers), and J. Cunnah (Work- house master). Mr. Dancy, Local Government Board Inspector, was also present.</p>
        <p>THE COST OF VACCINATION. Mr. Woodward presented a comparative statement of the cost of vaccination. The total cost under this head for the quarter just ended was £B7 us. Id. as against £lOB 10s. id. for the previous quarter, and £lO7 18s. 4d. for the Michaelmas quarter. For the corresponding period of last year the cost was £lll Gs. 10d. The total cost of vaccination during the year had been £406 18s. lid.</p>
        <p>TIIE PROPOSED ALTERATIONS. Mr. Dancy said he would like to speak with reference to the plans for the proposed alterations at the Work- house, which, he thought, were rather cu.nberous. On the plans for the new mortuary very little was stated and he would like to know more about ventilation drainage &amp;c. Then with regard to the nurses' home. The passage was a little too cramped and he suggested that it should be six or seven feet in width. Mr. Baxter: Surely a nurse can walk down a passage 4 ft. 9 inches in width. (Laughter.) Mr. Dancy: I have seen some nurses who would have a difficalty in doing so. (Renewed laughter). Mr. Dancy, continuing, made a number of other suggestions. leMr. Woodward thanked Mr. Dancy for his advice which he said would be fully and favourably considered. Mr. Stokes : Will this mean reduction in the cost ? (several members " yes ") lam pleased to see that the Local Government Board representative is trying to reduce expenses.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dariey assured the Board that they were 'wrong in thinking that the Local Government Board desired to increase expenses. Their desires were to the contrary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHURCIL—A ten days mission was com- menced on Saturday by Mr. Joseph Stringer, of Don- caster. On Tuesday night the Church was well filled to hear a sermon on "The Greatest Gift." The missioner is a man with a message fsr the people. He is supported by the local preachers and leaders. Mr. Hall,Mr. Robert Miller and Miss Dutton are the Secretaries for the- Mission. This Sunday there will be special singing.. Mr. Stringer will speak on" The Staffordshire Revival' and on Tuesday will lecture on " The Undaunted Man." Chairman Mr. T. W. Hayward J.P. PETTY SESSTONS.—The Cheshire Standin,. Joint Corn mittee, at their quarterly meeting, on Saturday, adopted a recommendation of the justices sitting at Daresbury that the Petty Sessions be held alternately at Daresbury and Stockton Heath. It was represented to the com- mittee that a convenient room could be obtained at Stockton Heath, in which Petty Sessions could be held, for a rental of £l2 per annum, subject to the committee providing the necessary fittings to make the room avail- able for the purpose.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED NEGLE( T OF CHILDREN. _</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on morn- ing, Patrick and Catherine Ryan, 2, Warburton's-yard, were charged with neglecting their three children.—Mr. Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. That case, he said, was one of the worst that had been before the Court. The youngest child was only eight months old, and was in a shocking condition. When Dr. Peacock sea it he immediately ordered its removal to the Workhouse Hospital ; it only weighed (illbs. A tele- phonic message had been received that morning stating that the child was in danger of its life. The prisoners were also charged with neglecting two other children, and they were also in a had state. He asked for a remand filiy a week to see how the youngest child went ou.—The remand was granted.</p>
      </div>
