      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <p>Now that the election is WHAT WILL THEY nearly over, and that it DO WITH IT? is certain Sir HEN Y CAMPBELL BANNERMAN will have a big majority over all the other parties combined, interest is transferred from the doings of the late Government to the prospective doings of the new one. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN and the Unionist party generally are already beginning to suggest that the Liberals will be able to accom plish very little, but it is noticeable that during the last few days we have heard nothing about their remaining in office only a short time. It is recognised that with what is the biggest majority since the passing of the great Reform Bill the Liberal party is in office and in power for the normal term—five or six years. As the Prime Minister said the other day, the placing of such a majority behind the new Cabinet imposes a very great responsibility on them, and the country will undoubtedly look for many of those measures of social reform for which we have already waited far too long. Yet we hope, as we said the other day, that there will be no attempt to crowd the pro.. gramme of the first year. If the Govern- ment will carefully plan out the reforms they hope to accomplish during the life of the present Parliament, and deal with a few each year, the country will be satisfied. When measures are promised in the KING'S Speech which have perforce to be dropped towards the end of the Session for lack of time,great disappoint- ment is the result. Truly,a big programme awaits the new House, and it would probably be policy to put in the forefront those measures which, under ordinary circumstances, might be thrown out by the House of Lords. The House of Lords will not dare to refuse to pass measures which have behind them the recently-elected representatives of the people in such overwhelming numbers, but if measures to which they have rooted objections are not brought in for a year or two then it is possible that they may begin to suggest that the country ought to be consulted on them. The first of such measures will undoubtedly be the Education Bill promised by Mr. BIRRELL. Had there been a small majority such a Bill as will be introduced would certainly have failed to pass the Upper Charober. The Bishops would have been there in great force, and would have called on their colleagues to reject the measure, but we question whether they will now dare to do more than enter a mild protest against some of its pro visions. Then there is the land question. The House of Commons, even the late House of Commons, has, on at least two occasions, carried to its second reading a Bill for the Taxation of Land Values. It may safely be prophesied that a similar Bill will now be carried to its third read- ing. UM the Lords dare to oppose that? We think not, especially if it is done soon. Such a majority as has been given to Sir HENRY Goo- sEr.r.-BANNEsatex will chew even the members of the House of Lords that the country is in no mood to be played with, and the measures sent up to them will, we venture to think, be pretty generally allowed to go through. Before the next</p>
        <p>General Election, too, a measure of electoral re- form is much needed. Reform of the registration laws, one man one vote, and an extension of the hours of polling are prime necessities. The:Labour party will demand that the Trades Dispnte Bill shall be carried, and Parliament will no doubt agree. All they ask is that Trade Unions shall be put in the position which nearly everybody thought they were in up to the time of the Taff Vale decision, and in the position which the Parliament of a former day thought it had secured ' for them, but which on investigation by the Judges proved not to be the case. Such are only a few of the tasks that await our newly-elected legisla- tors. Much is expected of them, much will come, but that they will satisfy everybody even of their own party cannot be expected. Tnis much, however, is certain from the composi- tion of the Cabinet, that there will be honest and straightforward dealing, that 'there will be a genuine attempt to put into practice while in power the same principles on which they acted while in opposition, that it will be the interests of the community rather than class interests which will guide their policy, that there will be no con: tempt for the House which contains the elected representatives of the people, and that there will be such an overhauling of the great spending departments as has not been known for many years. The people have given their confidence in full measure, and the men are worthy of the trust reposed in them.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire cheese is urnin Cans CHEES 't hrough. Cut Liberal right through. Cut it where you will and you will find no trace of the Tory or Protectionist maggot. Every one has been expelled by the elec- tion, and the unique spectacle is presented for the first time of agricultural, aristocratic, fox-hunting Cheshire without a single representative of the old Tory squirearchy in Parliament. Some of the divisions have always been Tory, and six months ago their political colour would have been con- sidered as unalterable as the laws of the Medea and Persians. But the Liberal Free Trade wave has swept the county from end to end, and it presents to-day one of the most remarkable instances of political conversion ever recorded in the history of parties. At the dissolution nine county and borough seats were held by Unionists and four by Liberals ; now the whole 13 (assuming the Liberal retention of Crewe) are in the hands of the Ministerialists. The climax arrived yesterday (Thursday) when Mr. Coningsby Disraeli, whose only claim on the constituency is apparently one of descent, was sensationally defeated by Mr. W. J. Crossley,who transformed an adverse majority of 1,50 S to one of 12,619 in his own favour. When an unemotional county like Cheshire plumps for the Free Trade candidates in this fashion there can be no doubt- ing the fact that Protection, to use the words of ' a greater Disraeli than he late of Altrinehaw, is both " dead and damned."</p>
        <p>Since our last issue both KNUTSFORD AND these neighbouring eon- NEWTON. stituencies have polled and, to the delight of the Liberal and Labour parties, have rejected the sittiNg Con- servative members. The return of Mr. A. J. King for the Knutsford Division is one of the greatest triumphs which the Liberals have secured the whole country through--in fact it transcends even the Warrington victory which ranks high in the list in the estimation of local Liberals. Mr. King could not have contested a more Conservative constituency. For a century the Egerton influence had been paramount and a member of the family had always represented the division in Parliament. The sons of the house had succeeded to the representation as naturally and regularly as they succeeded to the family acres. The late member is an Egerton who had I sng held the seat, and a more typical Tory landlord could not be found. As lord of the manor he expected the votes of the dwellers on the land, and until recently there was never any doubt about his getting them. But this last two years has seen an awakening of the electors to their national responsibility. To "vote for the squire " instead of remaining the "be all and end all of their political faith began to he regarded as a course to which there was an alternative worth considering. The agriculturists of the Knutsford Division began to look beyond the end of their noses, and the first unmistakable evidence of the political awakening was tha rejection of Mr. Eger- ton for the County Council. Mr. King thereafter prosecuted his candidature with renewed vigour, and the enlightenment of the constituency on the questions of the hour resulted on Saturday in the rejection of Mr. Egerton by 700 votes. It was a severe blow to the territorial member, and a great triumph for the Manchester business-man. We heartily congratulate Mr. King, M.P., on having bioken through the hide-bound Conservatism which has so long stagnated the Knutsford Divi- sion, and in its stead supplied real, intelligens representation of the people. Labour has left its mark on the Newton Division, so long held sacred to the Cross's and Pilkingtons, by returning Mr. J. A. Seddon with the substantial majority of 541. The fight has been ail exceptionally keen one, and while the Labour nominee has relied upon the principles of Free Trade, no slavery, and fuller recognition of the rights of the workers, the Conservatives, as else- where, have been obliged to descend to personali- ties in order to influence the electors against their opponent. As an instance of unscrupulous politi- cal warefare we should think the attempt to brand Mr. Seddon as an atheist will rank among the worst. But the Tories were only hoist with their own petard. The Britisher's love of fa;r play always comes to the rescue when a man is hit below the belt in this fashion,end it hardly needed the iprompt refutation of the charge to ensure Mr. Seddon's return. The victary for Labour and the Progressive cause generally in this division is a great one. The miners and workmen have realised their power and demonstrated it in unmistakable fashion. They were determined to return Mr. S Woods, and it speaks well for the resolute character of the workers that they gave such a solid backing to a much less well-known man in the person of Mr. J. A. Seddon. The latter has worked hard and thoroughly earned the honour which has fallen to him. We wish him every success in his new sphere of " labour."</p>
        <p>-- Truly, there is nothing new LABOUR. under the sun. It is 1900 years since the Roman epic poet Virgil declared that "Labour conquers all things." Little did he think that to-day that say- ing would be a new one to many people in the sense that the Labour party has carried everything before it in the Parliamentary election. But while "to labour " usually implies mannal toil, it has many other meanings. For instance, mental labour is often as painful, fatiguing, and exhausting to the body as physical exertion. Shakespeare gives the word a nautical signification when he speaks of " the labouring bark." The adj-ictive so used conveys to the mind the picture of a vessel rolling and Pitching heavily at sea. But Dryden uses the worl in another sense when ha writes. shepherd, like a plant of sturdy oak, and labuUr bim with many a sturdy stroke." The ternptatioa of sudden accession to power will excite the imagination of many of the new legislators, and I suggest all sorts of ways of displaying their strength. The words of Ariosto, "He that labours is tempted by the devil." should therefore be remembered ! and the real triumph of the new party will be to shew that they are strong enougn to restrain themselves, however sanguin3 they may be, from allcwing ambition to overcome discretion.</p>
        <p>James Conniway, a Kentucky farmer, has died from eating a dozen large oranges and two dozen apples at one meal. He undertook the gastronomic feat for a wager of £2.</p>
