      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Toni</p>
        <p>from</p>
        <p>voti</p>
        <p>~',.,t.'f,;1'...:.:.':,.{),ti.,,:.i;....;:...;.'...'...,...2::,:::.... .i,?,...:.''''.;'::F:'.,..:..i:'..,:.'.':::::,.:.:•.-...!.'.:,-.'L.:.-,:'',.:1'..•:::::•.'.,,</p>
        <p>We wonder if Mr. Balfour has read the newspapers during the past fortnight? * * It is considered very probable that the Liberal Government will at an early date bring in a Bill to extend the Workmen's Compensation Act to all trades.</p>
        <p>* ;* Mr. W. H. Lever's majority in the Wirral Division was 1,701--almost as symmetrical as that in War- rington, where his victory has given great satisfaction to the Liberals. * *</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Seddon, the newly elected Labour member for Newton, has been a grocer's assistant and a com- mercial traveller. In 1002 he was President of the Shop Assistants' Union. . .</p>
        <p>*s* The scarcity of conveyances working on his behalf on Tuesday did not dismay Mr. J. A. Seddon M.P. • He cheerfully advised the electors to get in Colonel Pilkington's motor-cars and "vote for Seddon." Judging by the result the advice was largely followed. * * * The new Liberal Government has been given 18 months lease of life—by the Tories. They forget that the old Government of unhappy memory taught the nation a never-to-be-forgetten lesson in the art of retaining office. And they never had the majority the Liberals have to-day. 554 Mr. John Burns attributes the increase in pauper- ism to Mr. Chamberlain's " plunging policies" abroad. His war and its waste has added 115,000 more depend- ents on local rates in England and Wales "to signalise his blunders, monument his folly, and mark his political defeat, as well as his fiscal rout."</p>
        <p>*s* , The Warminster Board of Guardians is somewhat perturbed by tho legacy of a recently-deceased inmate, who on quitting the rough roads of life, bequeathed her wooden leg to the Guardians, for the use of the Board if required. In default of a better suggestion, if the limb is " solid, substantial, and thick," the Chairman of the Board might use it to keep order with. „ * The " revival "in Knutsford Parliamentary Division has evidently left a nasty taste in the mouth of the Hon. Alan de Tatton Egerton. He explains his defeat thus : " The reasons that have brought about my defeat are Ananias pervading the whole constitu- ency and the action of traitors to the Unionist cause." Fancy, both Ananias and Judas at Isrge in Knutsford, and the gaol so handy too !</p>
        <p>* * * As all but one person expected Mr. R. A. Naylor merely helped Mr. Lloyd George, M.P., to a record majority by challenging his representation of Carnarvon Boroughs. Where the strongest local Welshmen had failed It was little use Mr. Naylor venturing. But he would apparently rather be trodden on by the great than remain entirely unnoticed. We hear that he is going abroad—but not to" die for the working man !" , At a meeting in support of Mr. Tomkinson the Liberal candidate for Crewe Division on Wednesday night, Mr. William Stubbs, aged 82, an old Cheshire man, related on the platform his experiences in the old days of Protection. He said that his father had 99. a week and six children. Many times he wished he had never been born. Their food was black bread and treacle and potatoe peelings, with skim milk. * * Much sympathy will be felt with Sir John Brunner, Bart., M.P., who on Wednesday sustained a painful accident in the hour of his victory at the polls. He was getting into his carriage when he ruptured a large muscle above his right knee and has been , ordered complete rest for a fortnight by a specialist. Among the many messages of sympathy the popular baronet received was a telegram from Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P.</p>
        <p>* * * One of the most striking Liberal victories has been achieved by Mr. Robert Cameron, the veteran repre- sentative of the Houghton-le-Spring division of Durham. The Conservatives had the temerity to oppose him, with the result that he has been returned by the magnificent trajority of 5,790. Warrington people, who have often had the pleasure of bearing eloquent addresses from Mr. Cameron, will congratu- late him upon this signal expression of the confidence of his constitnents. * * * There have been a good many " cleitn sweeps in the present election, but the cleanest i-knd completest has been effected in Wales, says the London corres- pondent of the "Liverpool Daily Post and Mercury," who declares that there is not the slightest chance of a single Tory member being returned from Wales, and that Monmouthshire also will probably tell the same tale. The Highland counties in Scotland ate doing the same thing, and if the late Lord Salisbuig were alive he would have something to say about "The Celtic fringe."</p>
        <p>I'</p>
        <p>* * * Stockton Heath Liberals are highly delighted at the success of Mr. A. J. King is the Knutsford Division and intend celebrating the victory in a fitting manner. It was expected that Mr. King would visit the village on Wednesday evening and a royal welcome was arranged. Torches were purchased and a band engaged and everything was in readiness for the holding of a triumphant procession when a tele- gram was received from the member to say that pressure of other engagements would prevent him keeping the appointment. The arrangements for the procession had to be hurriedly countermanded but the Heathens decline to be robbed of an opportunity for rejoicing and Mr. King will' be feted to-mght (Saturday.) * * * Mr. B. Pierpoint was in the not very happy position on Monday evening of having to speak upon his recent defeat. With the bulk of his remarks to the Junior Conservatives we have no fault to find. They were in good taste; but to one sentence we must take excep- tion. Our late member is reported as haring said " the other side had not been too particular in its statements, and its placards had not been entirely satisfactory to the pure lovers of truth." This is a comment which comes with ill grace from the nominee of the party which abandoned principles for personali- ties on placards at the very beginning of the contest. The "foreign labour " cry offended a good many " pure lovers of truth" on Mr. Pierpoint's own side, and most certainly lost him many votes. The Liberals refrained front retaliating with anything of a like personal nature, and are entitled, we think, to all the credit for having fought fairly. * •* * Mr. Chamberlain has a reputation for being vitriolic, or more attention would probably have been paid to his rude allnsions to Mr. John Burns. Speak- ing on Monday evening, as he stood amidst the ruins of the once great Unionist party, "Joe " said that " Sir H. Campbell-Bannerman had left the unemployed problem in the hands of Mr. Burns, a developed hack- official in whom the people failed to recognize the mob orator of Hyde Park." Possibly this ill-tempered sneer was in some part due to the recollection that theduring jeigleeiting j ion oe, campaign " Honest :John " com- pelled to acknowledge a serious error in some statistics he had been quoting in regard to pauperism. We notice that on Tuesday, Mr. Lloyd George, M.P., took up the cudgels on behalf of his fellow-Minister and retorted that he would rather be a " mob orator "than a " mob organiser." There is point in this allusion which is not likely to be lost on" Joe." He represents the people who once mobbed Lloyd-George. * * The scholars at one of the Southport day schools held a " mock election " the other day and seem to have extracted a great deal of amusement from it. Candidates were duly nominated and appealed to their youthful audience on points dear to the schoolboy's heart. Thus, the Radical candidate declared himself in favour of longer holidays (cheers), the abolition of the cane (general cheers), pocket money (loud cheers), the provision of free bicycles for scholars living at a distance (cheers from a section), the provision of a motor omnibus for wet days (more sectional cheers), permission to use thick gloves and pneumatic seats in pants by boys about to be caned (sympathetic -; applause), and a variety of other things, such as a shooting club, gymnasium, swimming lessons, and so on. On the other side, the Conservative candidate • did not favour the abolition of the cane. He had been • through the mill, and he did not see why others should not go through the same experience. His • address was really a criticism of his or , ?nes declarations, finishing up with sundry items the orthodox programme of a Conservative politician. The candidates were heckled and then the rig took place. Somewhat surprising to note, this resulted in a victory for the "blue by 76 votes to 71. At the real election for the division on Wednesday, how- ever, the people made no mistake and Mr. Astbury, K.C., the Liberal candidate was returned in place of Mr. Marshall Hall, K.C.</p>
