      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, January 27, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 27, 1906.</p>
        <p>' Index to Inside Pages. . Emmanuel Tea-Party. , ' PAGE. The annual congregational gatheiing in connection with the Emmanuel (RE.) Church, was held in the 2. New Serial," A Cruel Temptation." Church Institute, Knutsford-road, on Thursday even- Women's Chat. ing, the proceedings being attended with success. 3. Football. About PO persons sat down to an excellent tea, which . The Free Churches. : was served by the lady members of the congregation. Runcorn Board of Guardians. ! An entertainment was subsequeatly held, over which Stockton Heath and Padgate. the Rev.F. Vaughan p.resided,and an excellent musical Women's Realm. ; programme was submitted. 6. Education Committee. WS, P.C.C. Work. Street Improvement. The Local Government The Warrington Branch of the National Society for Board Inquiry the Prevention of Cruelty to Children has during the Runcorn Rural Council. quarter ended 31st December 1905 inquired into 83 complaints of neglect ill-treatment and other wrongs Leech. 7. "Old Age Pension Schemes," by Sir Boadin T. , ;of childho xi, all of which were found to be true, Children's Corner. ! affecting 286 children and 132 offenders. Action was • • ! taken as follows :-76 cases were warned, 6 were The Labour Bureau. prosecuted (convicted 6 discharged nil), and I was Up to this (Friday) morning the number of otherwise dealt with. The Society's Inspector made unemployed registered at the labour bureau opened by T7O visits of suoervision. the Distres Committee was 106-97 men and nine women. Work has been obtained for six men, and four men have found employment themselves, thus leaving 96 on the register. The following is the list of the trades of the persons in need of work : General, 30 ; builders, 7 ; wire trade, 5 ; plasterers, 1 ; ironworks, 5; French polisher, 1 ; clerks, 2 ; tanners, 5 ; bricklayers,3 ; fustian cutters, 2 ; joiners, 3 ; painters, 20 ; drivers,l ; charwomen, 9 ; gardeners, 1 ; printers, 1. Employers are asked to make use of the bureau and thus co-operate with the committee LING BLELELtiTsH, lUREN.D_RAR, in finding situations for the deserving unemployed. dvTtli. B CLOCK FOR BEST PI. BoorsE Building Students' Association</p>
        <p>. Iron Fencing of every description.' Lowest prices.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge St., ' Warrington.—Advt. Padgate Lawn Tennis Club. A successful and enjoyable whist drive and dance in connection with the above club took place on Thurs- day at the WinnoarleighCaf4. An interesting item in the prodeedinga was the presentation of the prizes for the Club tournament, which was played at the close of the season. The winners were : —Ladies, Ist, Mrs. Adatnson ; 2nd, Miss Bortchier. Gentlemen : Ist, , Mr. E. England ; 2nd, Mr. S. Adamson. By a happy coincidence the day happened to be the birthday of the president, Dr. Bennett. The members took advan- tage of the unique opportunity by presenting to Dr. Bennett a pair of silver candlesticks as a souvenii of the interesting occasion, and in recognition of the enthusiasm with which he discharged his presidential duties.</p>
        <p>By the—kind permission of Mr. Henshall (director and general manager) the members of the above Association visited on Saturday afternoon the joinery works of Messrs. Naylor, Ltd., Howley. The parties xi ere met by hr. Daintith, who very ably conducted them through the various departments. These exten- sive works cover some 10 acres of ground, comprising piling ground, storing sheds, cutting out, moulding, turning, and joiners' shops. The motive p mer has recently been almost entirely changed from. steam to electricity, and the motors installed aggregate 250 horse power, in sizes varying from sto 60 horse power. The works abound in modern examples of labour-saving machinery, cross cut, log, deal, and circular saws, planing, moulding machines etc., all of which were set in motion and explained. C insider- able interest was manifested in the turning depart- ment, where the party watched the transformation of the rough timber into brush heads, etc, The class of work undertaken varies greatly in character, including every requirement of woodwork for dwelling-house, factories, hotels, churches, etc. The darkness brought to a close a highly interesting and instructive after- noon.</p>
        <p>LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREETi—Advt. Warrington Field Club. Mr. Fairhurst presided over the opauing meeting, for the second half of the Winter sessions, of the Warrington Field Club, at which Mr. Oarrut hers lectured on" Wasps." A large number of specimens of the insects and several fine nests- illustrated the subject. The lecture, which pleasantly combined personal observations of British wasps with references , to exotic forms of an inteiesting but not very con- ' vincing character, was subject to lively criticism by several members. Mr. Carruthers replied in a brief but effective speech. Mr. W. Mounfield exhibited a largo series of the magpie moth bred from lame captured on two visits to one spot and afterwards kept under conditions as nearly as possible identical. Their great variations of colour despite similarity of food were commented upon both by the exhibitor and by Mr. Greening as evidence that food is not a controlling factor in the coloration of moths. Mr. Madefity intimated that Mr. Dunlop, assistant curator of thii Museum is endeavouring to carry out one of the definite aims of the club, the compilation of a com- plete flora of the district, and that any information relating to or specimens of plants occurring within the district comprised within a radius of about seven miles would be thankfully received by Mr. Dunlop. Oddfellowship Prince of Wales Lodge Annual Meeting. The annual meeting of the Prince of Wales hodg of the Independent Order of Oddfellows was held a the Pelican Hotel on Thursday evening, N. C-. King presiding over a good attendance. The Secretary pre- sented his report in which he said the year had been a successful one, both in regard to numbers of mem- bers and financially although there had been an unusual liability for sickness, That was unfortunate but there was consolation in the feeling that the :C349 distributed amongst udfortunate members „must have been of great benefit to them. (Hear, hear.) The increases to the sick fund during the year amounted to MO5 Os. 7d., making a total of .C 3,073 2s. sd. After transferring :C25 to the sick fund there was left a balance in hand on the Management Fund of :C2O Os. 9d. On the Benevolent Fund a balance in hand remained of LI Os. ad. The total increase in Lodge funds amounted to £133115. making a total of :C3,097 Os. 9d., with a membership of 470. The auditors, P.G. It. Sumner and P.G. IL Lawrence, were heartily thanked for their past services. P.G. H. Farrar was appointed inside Tyler, V.G. J. Peers, warden, and P.G. H. Sykes,sick steward. P.G. G. F.:Freeman and Bro.Smalley were appointed auditors. The question of creating a distress fund Was discussed. Votes of thanks were accorded to the Grand Master of the District, Bro. T. P. Bradshaw, to the Lodge secretary, Bro. T. Wadsworth, PIG., and to the permanent officers for their services cluing the year.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. E. W. Bindloss. The death occurred at her residence, (i rappenhall- road, Stockton Heath, on Tuesday morning, after a brief illness, of Mrs. Bindloss, wife of Mr. E. W. Bindloss. The deceased lady, - who was widely respected in the district, had -b3en in failing health • for some time, but she passed away sotnewhar suddenly. The funeral took place at St. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, on Thursday, the officiating clergyman being the Rev. H. V. Pigot. The mourners present were : Mr. E. W. Bindloss (widower), Masers. R. ff., C. H. awl H. E. Bindloss (sons), Mr. J. Nish (brother), Mr. J. Wheeler (brother-in-law), Messrs. J. McGeoch and J. Mawson (sons-in-law), Dr. Collins, Messrs. J., H. and S. MeCalloch, Mr. Joseph limiter, J.P. Messrs. R. and R. V. Hunter, Mr. J. Watson, Mr. • P. MeGuffie, Mr. G Denser, Mr. H. Reddish, Mr. A. Henderson, Mr. Ti. G. S. Finney, Mr. Watson, Mr. and Nlrs.oAell,Dr and,Mrs.Prentice,Mrs.l.Stt wart, j Mr. and Mrs. Wheeler, the Misses Williams, the Ilex% J. and Mrs. Yongte, Miss A. Lythgoe, and representa- tives of the Office Staff of -Messrs. Pearson and J Knowles Ltd. Many beautiful floral tribute were placed on the grave including those from Mr. G. Bonsor (Liverpo A.), Mr. and Mrs. A. Carter (Birk.lale), Dr. and Mrs. Collins, Mr. and the Misses Crowther (Hoylake), Miss D alds, Mr. mid Mrs. F. C. Dwtton, j Miss K. L. Graham (Wigtown), Mrs Hamilton (Liver- pool), Mr. R. and Miss Hunter, Mr. and Mrs. W. Jackson (Liverpool), Mr. ,C. Hassan, Mr. and Mrs. MoGeoch, Mr. McCulloch and family, Mr. R. McSAlan, Mr. and Mrs. W. Harris, Mr. and M.'s. Nish and Miss Nish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>TRAINING COLLEGE.</head>
        <p>THE TRAINING COLLEGE.</p>
        <p>The annual reports and balance sheet in connection with the Warrington Training College have`just been issued.lt is stated that during 1905 the number of students was , 158 and of these 146 were residential andl2 day students The following are the results of the various examine- ' tions :—(1). Religious Knowledge.—Second year, class 1, 30, class 2,15, class 3, 5 ; First year, class 1,35, class 2,35 class 3, 5 ; (2). Government examination for certificates (for second year students only). Class 1, part 1, 50, class 2, 31, class 3, 1 ; part 2, class 1, 20, class 2, 45, class, 3, 17. The Reverend Prebendary Reynolds, the Archbishops' Inspector of Training Colleges,visited the college on Friday, May 26th. In his report he refers to the marked spiritual tone of the I College. Mr. Holmes, the Inspector in whose group this College is placed, has paid ,several visits to the College and has sent the following report :—" The popularity of this College, due to its deservedly high reputation, has cause I iii recent years a great increase ' in the number of its students. During the past year some of the classes have in consequence been almost unwieldly large, and I am glad to know that in future the students in each year will be divided into two or more classes for all subjects. An excellent class-room has recently been added to the College buildings, and it is probable that additional class-room accommodation will be provided. The teaohing in this College seems to be exceptionally thoughtful and intelligent. The practical training of the students is carried on at some disadvantage, owing to the distance of the Practising School from the College. 'lt is impossible for this school to be what every College Practising School ought to be, an experimedtal school and a model school, as .well as a practising school." Details of other examinations are submitted. Miss Ackerley, Lady Superintendent of the Orford Hall Hostel, resigned her post for domestic reasons. Her place has been filled by the appointment of Miss Gertrude Brown. Miss M. E. Brooke after eight years of service also sent in her resignation, having obtained a post which enabled her to live at her own home. Her place was filled by Miss Amy Lucan, 13. A„ Victoria University. IVliss Frodsham, B.Sc, of London, and of Newnham College, Cambridge; has also hen added to the Staff to strengthen it on the mathematical and science side. A course of lectures on Physiological Psychology has been delivered by Dr. Smith, and the senior students were examined in the subject matter of course. The Lectures dealt with some parts of the subject which were of more than ordinary difficulty to the average student, but the lecturer's report shows that good work was done on the whole. In addition to this course of lectures the students have enjoyed the advantage of a short course from Professor Richardson on Voice Production and Reading; by Rev. Hackett Smith on Egypt, by Professor Sherrington on Fatigue, by Mr. Herbert Garrison on Volcanoes, by Mr. E. Robson on American education, and by Mr. Enock on aquatic autocrats and fairies. After much deliberation the Trustees have decided to purchase the premises of the Clergy Daughter's School, with the adjacent land. What further action this pur- chase may entail and in what way it may modify the existing arrangement of the College is still under con- sideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>PARR'S BANK LIMITED.</head>
        <p>PARR'S BANK LIMITED.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting —A Pre'table Year.</p>
        <p>The forty-first animal ordinary general meeting of Parr's Bank (Limited) was hold on Thursday at the Cannon-street Hotel, London. Mr. Cecil F. Parr presided, and in moving the adop- tion of the zeport and accounts, said that the year just closed had been a remarkable one, whether regarded from a political, social, or industrial point of view. Trade and industry had been growing steadily month by month all over the world, with the exception of Russia, until at the end of the year they saw records on every side -in the cotton trade, in the shipbuild- big industry, in the Clearing House returns, and in both exports and imports. They had again to con- gratulate Lancashire on a most profitable year, and he hoped that its staple trade would continue pro- sperous for many years to come. He ventured, to express a doubt as to the wisdom of building so many more mills in Lancashire, in the face of the ever-grow- ing competition which was going calla other countries. Already Lancashire had added in the last twelve months over sixty additional mills, which meant an additional 5,000,000 spindles. India in the last twenty years had doubled the number of her spindles, Fratice had increased hers by morethan GS per cent., America had even increased hers by over 300 per cent., while China and Japin, to which countries Lancashire was un- doubtedly indebted for folly 50 per cent. of its profits, lathe last half-year, must become in a very short time more in the nature of competitors than customers. He advocated, however, by all means,'-and at all costs, the fitting up authe existing mills with the moat up-to- date machinery, and with the latest and very best motive power; for competition imperatively demanded it. There were two important trades which had not as yet shared in the general prosperity, namely, the silk trade, which seeeted to be unable to make headway against foreign competition, and the building trade, which latter, he thought, must surely improve within a reasonable period. The only industry regarding which he could say nothing com- forting or hopeful was agriculture. A remarkable phenomenon in the industrial world was the marvellous recuperative power shown by Japan, whose exports and imports almost created a record. though the ink was ' scarcely dry on the Treaty. of Peace with Russia. It could not be said that financial matters had run a smooth and even course in the last half-year; the oscillations between stringency and abundance in the I London money Market had been freanent and bewilder- ing in their suddenness and intensity. Their cash end surplus funds together amounted to 48 per cent of their total liabilities. They were putting the usual amount , to their pension fund, and to the fund for writing down 1 their premises account, and they were carrying forward 1 an increased amount of inst over ,8100,000. Mr. John Dun seconded the motion, which was carried unanimous( y•</p>
