      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>WATERWORKS CONTRACT.</head>
        <p>THE WATERWORKS CONTRACT.</p>
        <p>Expert's Report Received.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Garside, the expert engineer appointed to investigate the waterworks contract, has concluded his investigations and on Thursday his report was received.. It will in due course be submitted to the special committee elected to inquire into the matter. The report is a voluminous document of about 100 pages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>NURSING ASSOCIATION. -- Workingmen's Committee Formed.</head>
        <p>THE NURSING ASSOCIATION. -- Workingmen's Committee Formed.</p>
        <p>An effort is being made to interest workingmen more directly in the work of the Warrington District Nursing Association. A strong Workingmen's Com- mittee consisting of delegates from meet of the local manufactories has just been formed and the first general meeting was held at the Infirmary on Thursday evening. Mr. F. Monks, whose zealous services on behalf of the Nursing Association are well-known and appreciated, presided over a large attendance. Trio enthusiasm dis- played augurs well for the success of the movement. In the course of a short introductory address the CIINIRMAN seggested that the work of the committee should not be entirely confined to the Nursing Associa- tion. He thought the committee might be made an exceedingly useful one in many ways and particularly in rendering financial help in deserving end necessitous cases. They might also interest themselves in the poor children's summer camp. Mr. GEOFtGE WALKER raised the poifit as to whether the new committee would interfere at all with the work of the Infirmary Workingmen's Committee. The CHAIRMAN said the Infirmary Workingmen's Committee was consulted and decided that it would be better to form a separate committee. The object of their undertaking was an exceedingly worthy one and if a little extra financial help were accorded the Association might be ablate obtain two additional nurses. He would be very sorry to 'Olio a step which would lessen in the slightest degree the contributions of workingmen to the Infirmary. He had not the slightest fear that the new committee would interfere with the infirmary , Committee—on the contrary he believed it would stimu- late the members to greater exertions. • w Councillor Burrroic thought the new committee would be able to tap new sources of subscriptions. The election of officers was than proceeded with, and resulted as follows :—Chaireian Mr. G. Walker; vice- chairman, Mr. T. Wadsworth ; hon. secretary, Mr. C. J. Newton ; hon. treasurer, Mr. H. Hope ; trustees, the chairman, deputy-chairman and Mr. W. Haigh ; execu- tive committee, Messrs. Wilkinson, Habberley, 'Bailey, Ince, Broadhurst, Birchall, Miller, Ditchfield, Fargher, Middleton Charnock, and Tilling, with the four officers; auditor, 'Air. E. Bradshaw. It was stated that the committee hoped to be the I means of raising at least £250 per annum for the insti- tution.</p>
        <p>It was decided to hold a general meeting at an early date to discuss " ways and means."</p>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A. Society. The anniversary meetings of the above society were continued on Sunday, when there was again an excel- lent attendance. Mr. George Weav9r presided, and in a short address referred to the wonderful progress of the PS. A. movement throughout the country. The Rev. C. L. Stowe gave a very earnest address on " The World's Great Election," pointing out that in. the election now taking place irttlus country everybody could not vote, but in the election to which ho referred everybody was bound to make, a choice between " Christ or Barabbas "—good or evil. The choice must be made. Mr. H. Bedforth (solo euphonium in Messrs. Crosiield's band) rendered in excellent style two solos, "The Lost Chord " and " Les 131 vets."• Mr. B. Wadsworth was the accompanist. Councillor Clegg read the scripture lesson, and the President also took part in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
