        <p>THE FUNEItiLL.</p>
        <p>The large attendance at the funeral which took place at the Cemetery on Thursday afternoon testified to the respect in which the deceased gentleman was held. The members of the Corporation and magistrates assembled at the Town Hall and joined the cortege at the late residence of the deceased in Wilson Patten-street. The procession was headed by a detachment of police under the direction of the Chief Constable ( MaLuke Talbot) and a Volunteer escort was also in attendance. The service at the Cemetery was conducted by the Rev. 11. B. Firth (vicar of St. James', Latchfo • d), and the Rev. D. R. Davies (curate). Amongst the relatives and friends present were : Mr. W. Johnson, Mr. J. Johnson, Mr. T. Johnson, and Mr. H. Young (relatives) ; Messrs. Lawrence Bolton, W. Bibby, T. Mason, Spencer Wallhead, T. E. Gibson, J. Walton, Reid, Longshaw, and Dr. Langdale. The i following members of the Corporation attended :-- Aldermen Francomb, Smethurst, and Bennett, Coun- cillors Ellison, Benson, Wilkinson, Burton, Sankey, 1 Evans, Skelland, Barton, Fulton Smith, Brough and, Dickinson, with the following officials :—Messrs' A. T. Hallaway (deputy town clerk). J. Fairhursta (borouah treasurer), J. G. Gornall (medicil officer), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), W. S. Haddock (gas engineer), F. Taylor (secretary of the gas department), F. G. RuddoQk (borough analyst), E. Place (chief market inspector.) The Borough Benet) was represented by Messrs. F. W. Monks, C. S. Burgess, R. T. Fairclo ugh, Chas. White, A. J. Jolley and Dr. Fox. The Volunteer officers attending were : Colonel Edelsten, Major B. Fairclough, Captain Crosfleld, Captain Williamson, Captain Avery, Captain Potter, Lieutenant Lapage. Lieutenant Picton and Lieutenant Owen. The escort consisted of Sergeant-Major Ingram, Colour-Sergeant Willcocks,Colour-Sergeant Instructor Cleaver, Colour- Sesgeant Instructor Newall, and Colour-Sergeant Woods. The deceased was a well-known Freemason having been P.M. of the Gilbert Greenall Lodge. The Masonic representatives at the funeral were :—C. G. McDowell, WM., 1250; E. Rose, P.P.AD.D.G.D.C., Sec. 1250; H. F. Greenough, P.M.. P.P.G.5.8.; J. Lee, Pr.G.D.C., Cheshire; W. Hodgkinson, "P.M., 1250: .1. R. Griffiths, P.M., 1250; J. W. G. Craddock, P.M., 1230; W. H. J. Jenkins, P.M., 1250 ; Rev. B. Percival. chaplain, 1250 ; Rev. AV. Bracecamp, chaplain. 2651 ; T. Healing, J.W., 1250; F. Houghton, 1250 ; E. Sangster, 1250 ; if. J. Hampson, 1259 ; J. Gray, 1250 ; J. JR Dell, 1250 ; R. Chadwick, 1250 ; J. Birchall. J.G., 1250 ; T. Strinaer, 1250 ; A. Jolley. 1230 ; W. H. Owen, 1250 ; J. .17 A. Parfita, 12)0; W. Rogers, 1250 ; J. H. Heywood, 1250 ; E. Parry,23s ; J. Fulton-Smith, 2, Canongate, Scotland; J. T. Bennett, J.G.,143; J. Sampson, 149. The Conserva- tive Assoctation was represented by Councillor Fulton Smith (secretary),Alderman Bennett (treasurer) and Mr. W. Holbrook (agent), and the Canservative Club by Mr. T. E. Sangster (manager). Others present included the Rev. E. C, E. Carleton. Mr. Hobert Pierpoint, Mr. Win. Houghton,Mr. A. H. Gibson, Mr. W. H.Stansfield.Floral tributes were forwarded by Mrs. Hutchinson, Mr. J. Johnson and family,arrs. \V. H. Young and family. Mr. and Mrs. Smith. Tom and Annie Johnson, Mr. and Mrs. Fred Howard, Mr. and Mrs. Gibson, Mr. and Mrs. W. Johnson and family, St. James' Church Council, Lieutenant-Colonel Edelsten and Volunteer officers, Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst and family, Captain A. F. G. Potter, the deceased gentleman's employees. Mr. and Mrs. James Rushworth, Mr. and Mrs. Jahn Walton, Mr. and Mrs. Mainwaring and family, Mr. T. Mason, Alderman and Mrs. Francomb, Colonel .1. C. Ridgway, Mr. and Mrs. Lonadin, Mr. and Mrs Ralph Johnson, Mrs. Reid and tamily, the maid. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr. Ralph Johnson. REFERENCE AT THE EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Education Committee on Mon- day evening, the Chairman (Alderman Bennett', said he deeply regretted, in common with the rest of the mem- bers, to hear of the death of Alderman Hutchinson. Although he was nota member of the Education Com- mittee, the late alderman was one of the managers of St. James' School, and a very old and prominent mem- ber of the body from which that committee derived its origin. They all esteemed Alderman Hutchinson on account of his genial personality, and all entertained the highest respect for his public character, and the years of honest service he devoted to the town. He (Alderman Bennett) was sure be was only expressing the feelings of the whole of the committee, when he said how sorry they were to hear of his death. (Hear, hear.) MAGISTERIAL REFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Before the commencement of the business at the Police Court, on Tuesday, Mr. C. JACKSON HOLMES, the magistrate in attendance, said he would be expressing the feelings of his fellow magistrates when he stated how much they regretted the death of Alderman Hutchinson. He was quite sure that the town had lost a most faithful servant. He war a gentleman who took a very great interest in all affairs of the town, and he did his very best to serve it in every possible way. It had not been his (the speaker's) privilege to sit with him for a long period, but during the short time he had done so he had been particularly struck with the great pains he took to thoroughly investigate every case, and to see that justice was fully done. Mr. Witirtrg (the magistrates' clerk) said he could fully endorse all that had been said.</p>
        <p>Mr. linowNE said on behalf of himself and those practising in that court, they would much miss Alder- man Hutchinson. He had known him as a neighbour, townsman, town councillor, magistrate and, alderman of this town, and he sincerely regretted his death. He would be much missed in Warrington. Ile was a friend of all ; an enemy of none.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>DEATH OF ALDERMAN HUTCHINSON, J.P.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF ALDERMAN HUTCHINSON, J.P.</p>
        <p>THE NATIONAL OUTLOOK. Its Promises and Perils.</p>
        <p>Preaching on Sunday morning at the Latchford Baptist Church, the Rev. J. F. Matthews took for his sabject " The National outlook," basing his remarks upon Psalm cxliv., 13-15. In the course of the sermon he said the great movement on the part of the democ- racy, which the heavy polls of the last few days had revealed, cold not he accounted for on the " swing of the pendulum "theory, nor yet by the defence of a. negative policy called " Free Trade." It was a time- movement, part of a new European Renaissance. Austro- Hungary knew something of it. It lay beneath the passion for autonomy on the part of Norway. It was patent in the refusal of the French people to bear any longer the yoke of the Papal Church. It had broken all bounds in the red-handed fury of Russian serfs. With us it was more peaceful, but not less strong. New ininstrial forces asserted themselves in the persons of the labour leaders, whom every sane person welcomed 'to power. It was a time potential of good things, of freeing the wealth-creators of some undeserved encure- , brances. Moreover, here was a pledge that the question of poverty and nuemployment would meet with Isome satisfactory solution. For years men had been taught the immensity of their power, and the obligation and responsibility involved in its use and at last Demos had risen and shaken himself. Vy the aid of his vote he had beaten down the territorial land- lord, and wiped out the rule of the brewer. He had elected plain men, with clean hands and clean lips. He expected them to do clean business in a clean way or they too, would have to go. Political dodging,shufiling land prevarication bad failed. They could no longer be I regarded as synonyms for astuteness. Moral ideals in I public life were a quickening force, and they lald been I held hieh during the recent elections, end some before. The outlook suggested that there was a renaissance of the old Viking spirit, directed into ethical channels. Such was the outlook. Significant as it was it was also Pregeant with a threefold promise, the promise of justice and equity in the educational, administrative. and financial system of the country; of internatioml j Peace brought about by peace-loving men, using peace- ful methods; and of an illumined conscience in national I affairs. The "rights of conscience" had been sneered at, and the upholders of them had been insulted; but. the effect had been marvellous. Never again Would the nation be content to witness silently the passing of any measure, which violated the religious sensibilities of thousands of our fellow countrymen. The overwhelming Liberal majority, as in some sense embodying the national outlook, had its perils as well as its promises. Numbers had an awkward habit of begetting degeneracy and decay unless there was an adequate balance and corrective ; the safety of a big majority often lei to ignoring the voice of the people and failure to interpret. the signs of the time might bring about its complete disintegration and ruin. 'We were grateful for the out- look; we were glad of the promises of hope that seemed likely to realization ; we were alive to the perils inherent in the new movement. Its complete safeguard j lay in directing its spirit to the Cross. the symbol of all holy service and genuine sacrifice. It was not with- out meaning that in the thick of the new great party there were men of profound religious life, experience and service, who had been brought no in the midst of sacred influences, the true valuation of which could never be known. It was not too much to expect that a new mark might thereby be left upon the nation's life; at home there would be prosperity and content- ment; abroad, peace. The strife of theo- logical controversy over the people's sthools would cease, and "pure and undefiled " religion might at last take. its rightful place in training man for the manifold purposes of life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>INFLAMMATION OF THE KIDNEYS, DROPSY AND WEAK HEART</head>
