        <p>HIGH COURT OF JUSTICE- CHANCERY DIVISION. " PLASMON."</p>
        <p>On Saturday, January 13th, 1903, before Mr. Justice Warrington, the action of International Plasmon, Limited, v.Plasmonade,Limited, and others came up on notice of motion for judgment in default of defence. Mr. Sebastian, for the plaintiffs, said that when the case came before his Lordship on motion for an injunc- tion, his Lordship granted an intermim injunction against dgfendants, and said that, in his opinion, there had been a deliberate attempt by the defendants to steal a well-known name and get the benefit of the reputation of that name. Plaintiffs now asked for a perpetual injunction restraining Plasmonade, Limited, George Newitatu Jones, Gustav Welton, and Charles Ware from carrying manufacturers or vendors of any food or preparation for human consumption in solid or fluid form, and whether for use as a food or for tnedical purposes under the name of Plasmornde, Limited, or any other name or title of -vhich the word Plasmon formed part ; and from selling or offering or exposing or advertising for sale or procuring to be sold any such food or prepara- tion not being of the plaintiffs' manufacture, and not containing as the principal ingredient any article of the plaintiffs' manufacture ; and under any name or descrip- tion of which Plasmonade, Plasmonoid, or Plasmon formed a part. Further, from infringing plaintiffs' registered trade marks. Ills Lordship entered judgment fora perpetual injunc- tion as claimed, with costs.</p>
        <p>Sir Oliver Lodge is not to be allowed to have the last word in his controversy over Professor Haeckel. Messrs. Watts are now issuing for the Rationalist Press Association, at the popular price of sixpence,Mr. Joseph McCabe's reply to Sir Oliver's Matter and Life, under the title of" The Origin of Life." The argument,though necessarily somewhat controversial in tone. is MainlY consta • - in aim.</p>
