      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE INCREASES OF TEACHERS' SALARIES.</p>
        <p>Objection to School Fees.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held at the Town Hall on Monday evening under the presidency of Alderman Bennett. There were also present :—The Revs. Canon Morley Stevenson and John Yonge, Alderman Smethurst, Coun- cillors Craik, Sankey, Wilkinson, Fulton-Smith, Lord, Wright, Ellison, Burton, 3.laginu, B trtol and Brough, Messrs. A. J.Polley, E. Plinston, J. Upson, H. Kingdou, R. T. Fairclough, G. Walker, H. Roberts, T. May, J. Moore Murray (secretary and director), and J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>FINANCIAL MATTERS. SCHOOL FEES OBJECTED TO.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL MATTERS. SCHOOL FEES OBJECTED TO.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Finance Committee communica- tions from the Board of Education drawing attention to the amount received in school fees during the past school year in excess of the amount allowed at the following schools, viz ;—St. Ann's National, St. Barnabas', and Silver-street Wesleyan were submitted and noted. A suggesaon from the managers of the St. Barnabas' school to avoid such excess of school fees during the present school year was approved, and the director was instructed to endeavour to arrange with the managers of the St. Ann's National and Silver-street Wesleyan schools for similaraction to be taken at those schools. A comparative statement of the receipts and expenditure of the committee during the nine months ended the 31st December. 1905, with the estimates for the whole year, was submitted and noted. It was decided that the salaries of the under- mentioned officials be increased from the Ist April, 1906, as follows, viz. :—Nlr. it. B. Roberts, assistant director, from .£I.SS to £195 ; Mr. A. S. Elliott, stores clerk, from £l3O to £135 ; Mr. Ernest Haigh, secondary clerk, from £B4 10s. to £9l ; Mr. I. C. Pharoah, statisti- cal clerk, from £Bl to £B7 10s. . In moving the adoption of the minutes Mr.JoitEv said the final instalment of grants in respect of a number of c'ementary schools for the past year had been received and the members would be glad to know that in every case the grant was satisfactory. With regard to the in- creases of salaries they were,like thelncrea-ses of teachers' salaries which would be recommended by the School Management Committee, in accordance with the principles already laid down and approved by the Committee. With reference to the excessl of school fees, the matter had cropped up from time to time and the three schools mentioned had been dealt with on similar principles. When the fee grant was institutcd the Board of Educatioa insisted in return on either the abolition or the partial remission of fees in schools in which fees were charged. At the same time one of the conditions of the grant was that subsequently the fees in such schools should not exceed a limit varying in each case according to the amount previously charged. It had happened through varying conditions that in several instances the fees received during the year had slightly exceeded the departmental limit. The committee had therefore arranged with the managers to partially remit the fees of scholars who attended well in the higher standards and thus stimulate attendance. Mr. MAY drew attention to the absence of a compare with the amount of grants received in respect of the elementary schools for the preceding year. Although they were told that the grants were satisfactory, they were unable to draw any definite conclusion, because they had not the previous year's figures before them. They were informed that a comparative stets- ment of receipts and expenditure of the Committee in accordance with the estimates had been submitted. He would like that statement to be handed round for his information. At any rate, they hoped that under the very new brooms that were going to sweep the country clean very shortly, they would have these points attended to and that committees would be compelled to produce such compares as he had been struggling for during the past two or three years. (Laughter). The CHAIRMAN : lam afraid we can only deal with the law as it stands. We mast not anticipate. The , information you ask for is quite at your service if you will see the director. ..</p>
        <p>Councillor WRIGHT said he was surprised to see the excess of school fees. He hoped in the near future it would not be left to managers to adopt a system in regard to school fees but that it would be the privilege of that committee to abolish the fees altogether. That would be a sure and permanent way of preveqing the excesses. He ibelieved an absolutely free system of education would be of great advantage to all classes of children. He hoped the new brooms that Mr. May had mentioned would see to it that the fees were abolished and that we had a fair chance in this country along with other nations of getting the best we could out of education. _</p>
        <p>The CHALRMNN : T am sure we are all eagerly awaiting these blessings which may descend out of the heavens from some of these new brooms, but we must not discuss them to-night. Mr. H. ROBERTS : I think it would be as well not to..q(Laughter.) There might be itoo very strong diverse opinions expressed. (Hear, hear.) The CIIAIRMAN: That is perfectly true. That is the reason why, I ruled it out of order. Councillor BURTON alluded to the increases of salaries proposed ' by the Finance and School Management Committees. There was no , doubt that the advances gave pleasure and happiness to the psople who were going to ptrtici- nate in them, and he might say that they had his best. wishes thrown in. (Laughter.) But there was another side of the question to coosider—that of the people who were struggling hard for a mere existence, and who would have to contribute their share towards the £llOO required for the increases. Several people had spoken to him on the matter, and they seemed quite alarmed at this kind of thing. They wanted to know whether it was going to be an annual event, and when it was going to stop. If the Committee continued to give these £l,lOO increases he was afraid they would find themselves up a tree or, in other words, in the municipal bankrnptcy court, if they were not on the highway to it already. He would like to know if the committee had considered a max [-ilium rate to apply to these salaries. Mr. JOLLEY : In every case. The CHA.InNt \N pointed out that in the cases new under consideration a maximum rate was tis.ed by the committee but withdrawn by the Council last year. There was consequently no maximum as far as regarded the standing rules of the Council, but the committee had that idea before them.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jottev pointed out that the Finance Committee was simply carrying out the conditions of the scheme approved by the Education Committee. Scales were drawn up and the advances were within those scales in every case. With regard to Councillor Burton's anxiety as to the poor people outside, of course it was perfectly right as far as it went, but it did not go very far. He ventured to think that the majority of people who were in the room or outside, wanted to give people a fair day's wage for a fair day's work and he believed there was no official directly or indirectly in the service of the authority who was being overpaid. The comparison of expenditure &amp;c., referred to on the minutes was not all like the compare that Mr. May had so often asked for and which he appeared to imagine, by somealruost miraculous power might be derived either by legislation or some other process ere long. It was a physical impossibility to compare existing payments with previous payments under totally different conditions. The Finance Corn- mittee would certainly not attempt to mislead the committee by giving so-called compares which would only be misleading. If they wanted a comparison, by all means let it be a fair one. Until they had had three years' working under the new conditions they could not begin to print comparative figures of real value. With regard to the elementary grants, it might ease Mr. May's mind if he pointed out that so far the grants received exceeded those paid in respect of the same scbools last year by about £.500. It would be idle to say what the grants were on all the schools because they had not yet all been paid. Councillor Wright was under a misapprehension with regard'to the fees. It was not any greed on the part of the ma.nagers which had led to the imposition of the fees nor was it necessary for any fresh legislation if the committee wanted to abolish school fees. That power was given by the last Act and could be exercise!' by the committee. The managers were . simply. their agents ia the matter and the committee directed them to charge the fees. Tf the committee wanted to abolish the fees they had only got to say so, but they must remember that they could not " eat their apple and have it." If they wanted to ask the Town Council to levy another 2d. in thee in order to free the elemen- tary schools, let them say so straight out, "only," said Mr. Jolley, "don't let us have any sort of haggling about it afterwards," Couneilior WitTGIIT : Would a rate of 2d. in the £ enable the elementary schools to be absolutely free? Mr. Jot.t.r.v : I think so. I believe I sin under the mark in saying 2d. Continuing, he said that by a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Everybody knows that</head>
        <p>Everybody knows that</p>
        <p>EPPS'S</p>
        <p>is an admirable food, the nicest and most nutritious beverage for the breakfast table. It is made in a moment with boiling water or milk, and its sustaining qualities are COCOA</p>
        <p>Invaluable to all.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, jan.uary 27, 1906.</p>
        <p>majority the Committee resolved to retain the fees fot a time at any rate. Councillor MAGINN: How many schools are fees charged in, and why are they charged in some and not in others?</p>
        <p>Mr. FAIRCLOLGH : I object to these questions being asked.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : It is out of order. Mr. PAIRCLOUGH : These questions can be asked quietly and ascertained without taking up the time of the Committee.</p>
        <p>The Ciimf:MAN : We have'nt the information by us at the moment.</p>
        <p>Colll2CiiiOr MAGINN : I rise to a point of order. I am not prepared to be corrected by any member of this committee. This is the place to ask a pertinent question.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : You are out of order. The minutes were then approved. INCREASES OF TEACHERS' SALARIES. _</p>
        <p>---- - - - The School Mantgentent Committee submitted along list of iacreases of salaries of teachers in the elementary schools, in accordance with the committee's scale. The total amount of the increases was 1,015. The corn- mtttee further resolved that the salary of Mrs. L. I. I Cann, cookery mistress, be increased from £llO to £l2O per anumn as from the Ist April, DUG. Canon MORLEY 82kvEssoN in moving the adoption of the minutes said that if Iklr.Jollev had had to defend the rather small list of salaries which came under his department, he (the speaker) trembled to tuck what some gentlemen were going to say to him when they looked at the 321 pages whin!" covered the increases of teacher's salaries. (Laughter.) He could only reply as Mr. Jolley had done—that the increases were purely automatic. They were in accordance with the scale ' which the committee discussed and decided to adopt. ' Consequently it was hardly within their power to object oven it they wished to do so. Those wno did object would have to ask for a revision of the scale. Councillor FuLTos SmITIE seconded. There was no further comment on the increases which were unanimously approved.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YONGE : We are in terrible fetters That seems to me to be the common sense way of getting out of the difficulty and if we have to stand rigidly to the Stauding Orders business would be con- stantly delayed. Councillor ErxtsoN pointed out that the question was always open to revision. Alderman BENNETT : It would not be according to the wording of this resolution. Councillor ELLisixv said the the cases had been re-con- sidered before and the committee would do it again. He would however withdraw the minute.</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON suggested that the case should come up for periodical revision. Councillor ELLISON : That is the intention of the committee.</p>
        <p>--- - - The minutes with the exception of the one under discussion were approved.</p>
        <p>LAYING OF A MEMORIAL TABLET.</p>
        <p>It was decided that a memorial stone be laid at the " Beamont "Council school and that his Worship the Mayor (Mr. Alderman Bolton) be asked to perform the ceremony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Ikik: FIOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>Ikik: FIOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>Canon STEVENSON called attention to the fact that the school holidays for the year had been arranged. It was a question on which there would always be differ- ences of opinion, and he expected it was impossible to please everybody. All the holidays had been fixed for the same dates as last year. THE METALLURGICAL CLASS. A COMPLAINT.</p>
        <p>Mr. M.tv moved an amendment that the resolution approving the quarterly requisitions for the public elementary schools, the Secondary school,the Technical Institute, the Evening Continuation schools, the School of Art, and the Cookery Centre, be not, approved. He said he took that course only for the purpose of draw- ing attention to the fact that an application which had beau made to the School Management committee for the provision of furnaces to facilitate the melt- ing of iron in connection with the metallurgical class conducted by Mr. Ruddock at the Technical school had been refused. The result was that the class would have to be disbanded and nim students, some of whom came from Widnes, would lose the benefit of their previous attend- ances and be unable to sit at the examination. The cost of the furnaces would only have been .C 7. Ile hoped when it came again before the committee the grievance of the class would be seriously considered and that unless lowness of funds prevented it the necessary grant would be made.</p>
        <p>Councillor BCIITON seconded, Ile did not think the committee ought to refuse the expenditure on such an I important matter, seeing the groat change that was tak,ng place in the iron and steel trades of the country At least 2,000 tens of steel per week were used in War- rington and in ce-der to produce it a certain amount of science was required. It was therefore of oonsiderable importance that they should have a metallurgical class. He might on some future occasion propose that a class be held on Saturday afternoon, so that more iron- workers might have tke opportunity of attending. The CILAIRNIAN said he understood the matter was still under consideration by the committee, and the present decision was only temporary. It was with a view to making the best possible arrangements that the sub- ject had been deferred. Councillor BURTON pointed out that in consequence of changed methods of production a great quantity of steel was now used in iron manufacturing, and the whole of it was imported into Warrington. The consequence was that many ironworkers were thrown on the streets, and he considered it was important that classes should be held to familiarise the men with the steel process. Mr. JOLLEY said the committee had given the matter full and careful consideration. As one who had been associated with technical education for many years before the Education Act CarTl9 into force and who had striven very hard to secure tecknical classes of the type Councillor Burton had been advocating, he could assure that gentleman that he was only too delighted to find that in future they would have not adverse criticism but cordial support when they carne before the com- mittee to justify some expenditure out of the rates for the purposes which Councillor Burton had meationed. The amendment if carried, would simply mean with- holding supplies from the schools for another month. Mr. MAX withdrew his arneedment and the minutes we re approved.</p>
        <p>PHYSICAL INSTRUCTION. At a meeting of the School Nlanage nent Committee it was resolved that, provided a suflicient number of the teachers now attending the classes in physical exercises held at the Gymnasium desire to present themselves for such examination, arrangements he made with the Incorporate! British College of Physical Education for an examination of these teachers to be conducted by that college at the close of the present course of instruction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>SECONDARY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>THE SECONDARY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The committee resolved that the resignation of Miss Lily M. IVarburton, assistant mistress at the Secondary school and Technical Institute be accepted ;her engage- ment to terminate on the 31st January, 1906. The action of the director in issuing an advertisement inviting applications to fill the vacancy thus caused was approved, and it was resolved that a sub-committee,con- sisting of Mr. A. J. Jolley. Mr. J. Upson. the Rev. Neville Wilson, and the Chairman of the Committee (the Rev. Canon Stevenson), be appointed to consider the applications received and to select candidates to appear before the committee at their nest meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>VISITING CONIMITTEE.</head>
        <p>VISITING CONIMITTEE.</p>
        <p>It was noted that Nirs. Edelsten and Mrs. Stevenson had acceoLed the invitation of the Committee to serve on the Visiting and Advi3ory Committee in connection with the secondary school, but that Miss White was unable to do so, and it was resolved, that Mrs. E. J. Guest, of 4, Palmyra-square, be invited to act upon the Committee in question. All the above minutes were confirmed without com- ment.</p>
        <p>TEACHING TFIE DEAF. A QUESTION OF PRINCIPLE. The School Attendance Committee having considered the question of the fixing of parental contributions in respect of three children at the Royal Cross School for the Deaf, Preston, it was resolved that no contribu- tion be reouired from the parents of those children. The confirmation of the minutes was moved by Coun- cillor Ebt.rsos, and seconded by Councillor BartToN. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said he must move an amendment to the above resolution, which he thought violated the principle which should be maintained of upholding parental responsibility. That principle was a very important one, and he thought that at least some con- tribution:should be made by parents whose children were sent to such institutions in recognition of their respon- sibility. (Hear, hear.) He moved that the minute be not confirmed. Alderman SmExtruasT seconded. Mr. PLINSTON said he was sorry the chairman of the Committee had neigiven more information. The Com- mittee carefully considered the circumstances of the parents before passing such a resolution. In one instance the father of a child received alweekly wage of 13s. Bd. and his wife was an inmate of a lunatic asylum. In that case they had not fixed any contribution because of the utter inability of the parent to pay anything. ' In the other case the man had six or seven children and his earnings were less than £1 per week. He was not one who wanted to give a workingman something for nothing' but he thought that considering thecircum- stances Mr. Jolley could withdraw his amendment and appealed to him to do so.</p>
        <p>Mr. MAY suggested that the parents ought to con- tribute Is./ a week as a recognition of their responsi- bility. He did not think children should be absolutely dependent upon the rates in that way. Mr. H. ItonEaTs said he fully appreciated the principle which Mr. Jolley had in mind in moving the amendment and he thought everyone in that room would give Mr. Jolley credit for having no reason for moving the amendment except a strong regard for the question ot principle. But when, as they had in these cases the state of children's physical health calling for special , treatment in conjunction with the extreme poverty of the parents, he thought the committee was iustified in passing over the question of principle. Ile thought Mr. Jolley even with his strong regard for principle could withdraw the amendment. Mr. FAIRCLOUGH thought that a charge should be made upon the parents and if the parents asked for relief it could be granted by the committee. The com- mittee should not say that no contribution be required I because some years the position of the parents alight improve and they might be able to contribute. Alderman BENNETT : You would impose a charge and if necessary, remit it ?] Mr.FA IRCLOUGH : That is my point. I Councillor FULTON SMITIL : In the event of children not going to institutions of this kind the parents would be required to support them. When we relieve them of the upkeep of the children we should make a charge and then if necessary remit it. Councillor MAGI thought it was not a good prin- ciple to lay down that no contribution should be made. It was just possible that the parents some day might be in better positions but if that resolution were passed they would never be asked to pay anything. Councillor LORD suggested that the decision of the Committee could be revised every twelve months. Rev. J. l'oNoE : I suggest that we add to the reso- lution "for the year 1900." Alderman BENNF;TT pointed out that accerding to the Standing Orders of the Comiaittse that could aot be dam</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>DEFECTIVE CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>DEFECTIVE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee the following report of the Special Sub-Committee appointed for the purpose of obtaining information as to the extent to which children are debarred from obtaining full benefit from the instruction given in the public elementary schools in the Borough by reason of mental or physical defects, was considered viz "Since their last report the Sub-Committee havc met. once, viz :—on the 19th January, 190 G. An interim report by the Medical Adviser to the Com- mittee upon his examination of defective childreti in certain of the public elementary schools in the Borough was received, and it was resolved, (a) That the parents of all such children be advised of the nature of the defects, and, where possible, be asked to take steps to have them remedied ; (b), that, in view of the large number of children who may possibly need treatment, it is d,sirable that the Committee of the Warrington Infirmary be approached with a view to facilitating the applications of the parents of those children who may require treatment at that institution; and (c) that Mr. Councillor Ellison, Mr. Councillor Evans, and the Rev. Neville Wilson be appointed a deputation to wait upon the Committee of the I Warriugton Infirmary for this purpose. Instructions, were given for further information to be obtained as to the duties of the Medical Advisers to other Education Authorities."</p>
        <p>This report was approved and the minutes were con- firmed by the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>NO BETTER PROOF.</head>
        <p>NO BETTER PROOF.</p>
        <p>Time tries all things, and it is wonderfully encourag- ing to find that good news, which we scarcely dared believe, is confirmed after five years, more especially when the news is of great importance to so many of us. "Five years ago Doans backache kidney pills cured me of kidney disorders, and I have had no return of the troubles," says Mrs. M. Delooze, 13, Dohnan's-lane, Warrington. " I have had a number of people ask me about my cure, and I have always told them what benefit I received from Doan's pills. Mine is indeed a lasting cure." .</p>
        <p>Here are the facts of Mrs. Delooze's cure, which she gave us some years ago :---Por several years I was sub- ject to kidney complaint, and at times my back was so bad that I could hardly walk. There were dreadful pains round my loins, and I used to come over giddy. My limbs ached and swelled, I seemed full of wind, and my kidneys were disordered. " I could get nothing t do me any good until I used Doan's backache kidney pills. After taking some of these I felt eo much better that I was encouraged to keep on with the medicine,and I became all right again. I always keep some of the pills by me, and if I feel the slightest pain I take a dose before going to bed, and the pain if gone by the morning. (Signed) MARY DaLooza."—Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepenee). Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., B,Wells- street, Oxford-street, London, W. Doans are the pills that cured Mrs. Delooze.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES AND THE ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES AND THE ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The National Free Church Council has every reason to be satisfied with the result of its Election Campaigq. Owing to the action of the late Government on the Education Question it felt itself forced—though not associating itself with party politics—to undertake an active propaganda at the election,. in order to secure educational justice for Nonconformists. Reports from the constituencies, says the Rev. Thomas Law, show that the line taken by Free Church- men had a telling effect upon the polls. The National Council circulated millions of the leaflets on the Education Question, besides illustrated posters and a Manifesto. Great demonstrations were organised in the important centres, and smaller gatherings were held in practically every town and village through the entire land. _ _</p>
        <p>A uniqne feature of the campaign was a series of motor CA r tours undertaken by leading Free Churchmen. The Rev. F. B. Meyer,B.A. and the Rev. Thomas Yates spent a week in the Western Counties addressing five or six meetings a day, many of them in the open air. This was followed by a tour through the Kentconstitnencies. Dr. Clifford and Dr. Campbell Morgan motored through the Eastern Counties, and had a remarkably successful time in spite of some mishaps with the car. The Revs. C. Silvester Horne, and J. D. Jones toured through the Midlands. Many testimonies reached headquarters as to the good work done in thii way. A Liberal agent in one of the constituencies wrote assur- ing Mr. Yates that after the meeting addressed by him I a list of fifty electors who had bean marked "doubtful" 'in the oanvassing books were won over. The power of the education question as an election factor, and the solidarity of Free Churchmen are acknowledged by all who have had to do closely with the polling. Many Conservative candidates ascribe their defeat to Noncon- formist resentment of the Education Act. One Liberal agent speaks of the Nonconformists as absolutely I unanimous. Another says that during nine elections with which he has to eo he has never found the Non- conformists so enthusiastic and solid. At Guildford, where Mr. Brodrick's rejection caused absolute stupili- cation, it is recognised that the result is to be traced to the votes—and the hard work—of Free Churchmen. The same state of affairs is found in many other con- stituencies—gr. Balfour's Government united Noncon- formists politically for the first time for many years. A pleasing feature to Free Churchmen is the large number of their body who have found their way into the new Parliament. Not since Cromwell's time have so many .Free Churchmen obtained places in the house of Commons.</p>
        <p>BROKE HIS SPINE IN HIS SLEEP. A remarkable cause of death was revealed at an inquest yesterday. A cycle manufacturer, John ISlelmore, of Park Parade, Harlesden, has suffered from a diseased spine for years, but was in his usual health when he retired to bed. While asleep his head slipped forward, snapping his spine, death being instantaneous. Owing to the boom in copper the purchasers of an old warship discarded by the Admiralty realised several times the amount they paid for the vessel from the sale of eighty tons of copper fittings and sheeting taken from the hull.</p>
        <p>The Rationalist Press Association firm are publishing an essay by Mr. J. M. Robertson on What to read, and a somewhat important volume dealing with The Churches and Modern Thought, by Philip Vivian. The writer endeavours to set forth the reasons why many earnest and thoughtful men and women are unable to belong to the various Christian churches, and invites candbur in considering the grounds of belief.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <p>Bake at lome and make what the Chil- dren like best. Whether it's the wholesome "Little Break/Q.ll Scones," or the dainty "London Bums," or those delicious "Tea Cakes," or Big Cakes or Tarts, —they're all equally whole- some and equally easy to make with Brown &amp; Poison's raising powder 'Paisley Flour' (Trade Mark) And recipes are contained in every 7d. and 3!,d. packet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>STREET IMPROVEMENTS.</head>
        <p>STREET IMPROVEMENTS.</p>
        <p>APPLICATION FOR COMPULSORY PURCHASING P9WERS.</p>
        <p>Interesting Proceedings.</p>
        <p>Considerable interest centred in a Local Government Board inquiry which was conducted at the Town Hall on Tuesday by Mr. W. 0. E. Meade-King, M.M.T.C.E. The subject of the inquiry was a petition of the Corpora- tion to the Local Government Board to issue a pro- visional order to empower the Town Council to put in force, with reference to certain lands situated on the north side of Rylands-street required for street widening purposes, the powers of the Lands Clauses Act with refer- ence to the purchase and taking of lands otherwise than by agreement. The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) conducted the application on behalf of the Corporation and those present included Councillors Pemberton, Lord, Skelland, and Brough, Messrs. H. Thornton, E. England, H. J. ‘Vestbrook, H. Hatton' S. Gough, with T. Longdin (borough surveyor), A. M. Ker (assistant surveyor), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer) and others. ' The Town Clerk, in making the application, said I this was a very small subject for the purposes of a pro- visional order and affected only three pieces of property in the town. The Corporation &amp;stained an Act in 1889 I for the widening and improving of Bridge-street and in connection with the improvement scheduled certain pro- perties belonging to Mr. Robert Garnett. That I gentleman approached the Corporation and suggested I that instead of the Corporation making a 20ft. passage be would give them an extra 20ft. of land to make a new street 40ft. in width Mr. Garnett gave them the 201 t. of land, but it was part of the agreement' that the Corporation should pur- chase certain premises, and open out the street to Rylands-street within two years after the 7th Novem- November, 1903. That period had expired, but Mr. Garnett, with every reasouableuess and consideration, had extended the time for another year. The Corpora- tion were under a binding _agreement to acquire the property and open out the street 20 feet wide. As they could not persuade the owner to sell, they were bound to go to the Local. Government Board by petition for a provisional order. Cadet an agreement with Messrs. Hodgkinson, Ltd., the owners of the adjoining property, the Corporation proposed to make the stroet 40ft. wide. They did not believe this to bean expensive improvement. He had no doubt that some day the Corporation would decide to carry the street right through into Sankey- street and then it would become an important thorough- fare. The Corporation were, however, under no obliga- tion to continue the street into Sankey-street and the state of their finances unfortunately would not allow them to indulge in anything but what was absolutely pressing. Mr. Longdin then gave evidence and said the total area to be acquired udder the Order was 3463 square yards. The Inspeotor : What public purpose will the r?ad serve if it is not carried through to Sankey-street? Mr. Longdin: It will serve the purpose of getting to the backs of all the property which has been built under the Improvement Act. continuing he said that Alessrs. Garnett were now building very large work- shops with a frontage to the street. While the erection of these premises gave a higher ratable value than at present existed, it was requisite for their purposes that they should have a good entrance because the firm sent very large goods front the premises. That also applied to Messrs. Hodgkinson who had shop windows there, and to the back entrances of other Bridge-street property. It was proposed also to make a clear passage so that there would be a cartway into Sankey-street. Mr. Gough: I want to know what beuellt this is going to be to the ratepayers. The Inspector : Do you appear fOr the Ratepayers' Association?</p>
        <p>Mr. Gough: No, I appear as a ratepayer. Is there any immediate need for the present application? Mr. Longdin : I think the Town Clark has made that plain to the Inspector. Thelnspector : He has made it plain in this way— that the Corporation are under a binding agreement with Messrs. Garnett.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gough: Is there any other immediate need why we should go to this expense while we cannot carry the street through to Sankey-street? Mr. Longdin : I consider there is an immediate need. It is no use making two bitesat a cherry. Mr. Gough: Don't you think youprareasmagkloongeptawrot bites at a cherry now when you are urchin and not intending to carry it through to Sankey-street? Mr. Longdin : The Town Clerk has expressed the opinion that the finances of the borough will not per- mit us at the present time to carry it through to Sankey-street. As we are under a binding agreement with Messrs. Garnett, it is necessary that we should acquire the property and make the street 401 t. wide. If we do not, we enhance the value of the adjoining property. Mr. Gough : Why not carry it through now? Is there some other obstacle other than we have been told of?</p>
        <p>Mr. Longdin : There is no obstacle at all. Every- thing is clear and has passed the Council. But won't you have to make a similar application for other property?—Same,day--perhaps not in my time or in yours either. Wouldn't you have to buy Benson's shop in order to carry this into Sankey-street ?—Most decidedly. We shall have to buy it but we are not in a position to do it at the present time.</p>
        <p>_ . Could you carry this out without getting Parliamentary powers ? The Town Clerk : Yes if Mr. Benson would agree to sell to us.</p>
        <p>1 Why not make application when you want to carry the whole scheme out ? Is not this being carried out simply in the interests of private individuals to improve their premises ?—Mr. Longdin : Certainly not. According to the evidence produced here it is quite evident to me that that is the object—to help Hodgkinsons and other people, and not for public pur- poses.—lt must be for the public benefit if you get an increased ratable value, and it is essential to obtain that increased value that you should give proper approaches. The Town Clerk : The ratable value of Garuett's premises will be almost equal to the interest on the cost. They propose to spend £B,OOO on buildings. Have any obstacles been placed in the way of making the purchases ? The Inspector : The owners refuse to sell. I should like to know if there has been an offer made by the Corporation to the owners ?—Overtures have been tnarie to the owners, but we cannot agree, and we are therefore' obliged to apply for a Provisional Order. Councillor Pemberton then asked several questions. In answer to his opening question, Mr. Longdin said the street would be in accordance with the bye- laws; Is it not a cul de sac? The Town Clerk:. It is a col de sac, that is true. Councillor Pemberton : That is against the bye-laws. Mr. Longdin : It is not strictly a cul de sac, because there is a passage leading front it. The Inspector : The fact of the street being a cul de sac does not infringe the bye-laws? The Town Clerk : Our bye-laws require that a street shall be open at both ends, but that applies to new streets only. This is not a new street in the ordinary acceptatioh of the term. It is improving an Ancient highway. Mr. cough : It has only been a footpath up to recently ; a part of it now is only a footpath. The Town Clerk : This is not a new street within the meaning of the bye-laws. Councillor Pemberton : I am not a lawyer, but lam in the building trade, and I contend that if I bought the adjoining property and laid this street out, the Town Clerk would rule that I was laying out a now street. The Tows Clerk : Of course I should. . . _</p>
        <p>Councillor Petnberton : An-iff contend i that it should make no difference whether it is the Corporation or myself who carry it out. The Inspector : As far as the width is concerned it is in accordance with the bye-laws. Further questioning by Councillor Pemberton elicited the fact that the Corporation effected an exchange of land with Messrs. Hodgkinson, and there was a departure from the original plan. Councillor Pemberton inquired as to the cost of the scheme, but the Inspector ruled that the inquiry had nothing to do with that question. Councillor Pemberton : If these inquiries could be held before anything was done in the matter, I am afraid a great many of these things would be knocked on the head. (Hear, hear.) The Town Clerk: In other words you would dis- pense with the Corporation altogether? Councillor Pemberton in some matters. It is well for the Local Government Board and public to have a veto in Town Council affairs. (To the surveyor.) Can- not you say anything about the cost ? The Inspector: No ; we cannot go into that now. Councillor Pemberton : Vhen did the Corporation pass this by a resolution ? What date was it ? The Inspector : December 11th, 1905 ; that is the date of the petition to the Local Government Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>BLOOD GPITTING, AND WEAK LUNGS.</head>
        <p>BLOOD GPITTING, AND WEAK LUNGS.</p>
        <p>DOCTORS FAILED. CURED 13-17- VENO'S _LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>, Mr. CLARK, 811 Govan-road, Govan, N. 11., writes "1 have been suffering many year with chronic bron- chitis and asthma. For a long time I was in a low state, having been weakened by continual vomiting and spitting of blood. In July last I commenced taking VENO'S lITNING CHUG If CURE; I had then been off work six months. The first dose gave me relief end I rapidly improved. lam now cured, enjoying good health, quite a new man ; everybody is surprised. I breath freely, my cough and vomiting is stopped. J cannot realise after so much doctoring and suffering how your Cough Cure has cured me so quickly." VENO'S LJuIIrNINu Couoir CruE is the most efficient remedy procurable for coughs, colds, bronchitis, asthma, catarrh, weak lungs, and children's coughs. s.I and 2s. 9d. at Chemists everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <p>Councillor Pemberton: But I want to know the date it was passed by the Corporation. The Town Clerk: It was decided on the 31st of October that the Town Clerk make application to the Local Government Board fora Provisional Order.</p>
        <p>Councillor Lord : My position is this : that the particulars of this agreement between Messrs. Garnett and the Council have not been before the Council. Things are worked in a mysterious manner in this town. Continuing, he said he conteaded that it was the duty of the parties interested in this bottled up property to open out this street and pay for it. He did not see why certain persons' interests thould be looked after in this manner. This scheme had been before the Council for two years, and from what he could gather the parties interested bad only recently been approached in reference to the sale of property. That delay MS entirely against the interests of the town. He would liketo askthe Town Clerk whether a price had not been offered by one of the parties. The Town Clerk : Yes.</p>
        <p>, Councillor Pemberton: When did the plan pass the Corporation that this property should be acquired? The Town Clerk: I don't know without looking it up. You have copies of the printed proceedings. Why should you not look the information tip yourself? Councillor Lord : I don't think it has passed. The Inspoctor : It must have been passed. The peti- tion has not been manufactured. (Laughter.) The Town Clerk : No, sir. Councillor Pemberton: Are the owners of all the land the same to-day as when the resolution was passed? The Town Clerk : I think one piece of property has changed hands. Councillor Pemberton: Row soon after the Town Council passed the resolution to make thi : a 40-feet street, did you approach the owners of the land? The Town Clerk: I cannot tell you ; shortly after- wards.</p>
        <p>I Councillor Lord I -Might also ask whether one of ' the parties has offered to negotiate and had no reply. The Town Clerk: His solicitor has had a reply. It was arranged between me and his solicitor that he should attend before the committee. Councillor Lord: Then why gojfor these powers' The Town Clerk: Because they do not all agree. I The Town Clerk said the Town Council had for many I years, when carrying out their powers under the Private Improvement Act, appointed a special on mmittee called the Property Purchasing Committee, which consisted of six members, and they conducted the negotiations under this Act of 1899 throughout. They negotiated with Mr. Garnett and approved of the agreement. It was the practice followed in all towns to despute to a special committee the purchase of property. The Town Coun- cil could not negotiate. The Inspector said he would like the eate the agree- ment was approved by the committee. The Town Clerk looked through some books, but could not find the date at the monient,andthe Inspector said the date could be sent on to him. Mr. Gough remarked that having heard the evidence he objected that the ratepayers should be called upon to pay for the improvement now asked to be carried out. Mr. Thornton said that on some points he was not satisfied, and could not go back to the Property Owners' Association and say he accepted the matter as it stood</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk: The property changed hands before the resolution was passed. It changed hands between the date of theiflrst resolution which you allege your- self was thrown out of the Council, and the date of the second resolution by which it was decided to go on. Councillor Pemberton: Can you give us some idea of the cost? The Town Clerk: I object. The Surveyor : It is not fair to give it. The Inspector: You cannot ask that. 'Councillor Pemberton: And yet you allow someone else to purchase it. The Town Clerk: It was sold before the Corporation passed the resolution. Cottncillor Pembetton: I want the dates. The Town Clerk: You are a member of the Corpora- tion and you have got the proceeedlngs of the Council. Why don't you look them up? Councillor Pemberton: But you are the Town Clerk. The Town Clerk : I cannot be expected to go about with my head full of dates. Councillor Pemberton : When there was so ranch feeling in the town, the Corporaton ought to have acquired this property at the earliest possible moment instead of leaving it to private speculation to buy it. The Inspector: But they go in for a Provisional Order, It Is a matter for arbitration.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton : I should like you to note, Mr. Inspector, that the agreement has not been passed by the Council. I think that if that is so it is not legal. On that ground I strongly oppose the scheme now. ' The inquiry then concluded. \</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Thornton said he appeared as a representative of the Property Owners' Association, which included two-thirds of the property owners of the town having a membership of 200. The Association held their meeting last week when this matter came up for discussion. They had submitted to them a paper sent by the town authorities (the notice relating to the inquiry), but it was not quite clear whether it was intended to carry the street through to Sankey-street. rhey were, in conse- quence, a little upset because the rates were very high and likely to go higher. They considered that the continuation of the street to Sankey-street was a step that should not be taken considering the rates. The Inspector : They are not going to take it. Mr. H. Thornton: That I find to be the case now. Continuing, he said the rates were is. Bd, and the water rate is. 2d., making a total rate of Bs. lfkl. The Property Owners finding that they were a little mistaken as to the purpose of the Corporation upon points of purchase, wished him to say that they did not desire at all to oppose the application because the s treet was so far advanced, that they thought it would be a pity to oppose its completion although they thought it had been a mistake from the beginning. The association, he might say, wert satisfied that they received every consideration from the borough He thought the new street would, in the first place, be too near Bridge-street. It ought, he considered to have been as near midway as possible between Bridge-street and Cairo-street. He contended that Bridge-street, even when the widening had been completed, would be too narrow, but if they constructed the new street they would be unable to acquire any more land for Bridge- street on that side. To make a 40ft. street was out of all reason. If they had added say 20ft. to Bridge-street and made the new street only 20ft. wide it would have been a more businesslike move. The Town Clerk: The new street will be a relief street for Bridge-street. Mr. Thornton contended that with a borough debt of; nearly a millon and a quarter, taking it at 3.; per cent, The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- we were paying £52,000 a year interest. That meant trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present Messrs. S. Woodward, presiding, C. £l,OOO a week for a little town like Warrington. The Borough Treasurer : Those figures aro entire y Roylance, J. Balshaw, H. Eyes, P. Muunfield, George I Egerton Warburton, J. Stokes, T. Baxter, E. E. wrong. Mr. Thornton : They are very little wrong. I Linaker, J. H. Davies J. Sint, T. Wright, H The Borough Treasurer : We should he doing nothing Maivdsley, T. Guest, G. Pilling, S. Horton, J. L. Jones, else but pay interest if they were right. E. Bretherton, J. Spruce, W. M. Morris, T. Wood, J. Mr. Thornton, continuing said a 20-feet street would Rutter J. Hallows J. Whitley, W. Wier, A. G. Guest, , have been ample, and he could not understand why this T. Antivis, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant 40ft. street was wanted, unless it was in the interests of clerk), J. bwinton and W. Diggle (surveyors), .1. someone outside the officials and the Council. The Farrington (inspector) and Dr. Adams (medical officer). scheme was adding considerably to the borough debt. WARRINGTON AND son.rnwicH MOIST RAILWAY. He thought the Council had been slow in the matter in The Clerk reported the receipt of a letter, from the not looking after this property earlier, and he could not solicitors to the Warrington and Northivich Light Rail- understand the necessity for any secrecy in the pm.- way, with reference to the proposed application for an chases. In the majority of cases he thought the secrecy extension of time in which to construct the works. The would result inahe Corporation paying more. in con-. Solicitors stated that there were now a number of sub- elusion, he pointed out that the scheme finally passed stantial people interested in the scheme and asked that. only by the Mayor's vote. If this constant borrowing a conference between the Clerk to the District Council, of money was not checked, his Association would have Clerk to the Runcorn Urban Council and the promoters to take stronger measures. I of the scheme should be held.peuding which the Light Mr. E. England said he was a property owner, but he Railway Commissioners be ask.d to postpone the fixing always tried to keep well in view the interests of the of an enquiry with a view to the waiving of the town as a whole. While there were many believed this expenses of an enquiry. scheme a mistake, there were many who believed it The Clerk was instructed to attend such conference. would be to the future interest of Warrington, and i APPOINTMENT or MEDICAL OFFICER TO THE personally he hoped the Corporation would go on with • HOSPITALS. it and purchase the two shops in Sankey-street. The I At a meeting of the Isolation Hospital Committee it complete scheme would open out a direct thoroughfare was decided to recommend the Council to appoint Dr. , to the market. Ho thought, also, that having decided Bowers of Stretton as medical officer to the new upon a street, -10 feet was better than 20 feet. hospital and the smallpox hospitalata salary of £75 per annum, fuld further that an ambulance van be purchased Mr. Thornton said the people haying property on the sides of a street usnally paid for the making. Then at a costof £72 10s, again he objected that, the people who were handsomely In answer to Mr. Antwis, the Chairman said the served by the new street were not paying anything for tender sent in by Dr. Bowers was much lower than the betterment, whereas in the case of Messrs. Skelton, others. %hen Lynn-street was improved, betterment was avaazox SEWAGE SCHEME. • charged. The Town Clerk: Tt'is a matter of arrangement. The I The Clerk read a letter from the Local Government Board saying that before the Board decided upon the Corporation have no power to charge for betterment, application of the Council to borrow the sum of £I,G4Cf Councillor Pemberton said he would like the Local for sewerage works at Walton an inquiry would be held, Government Board, so far as they had power, to compel the date of which would be fixed as early as possible. the Corporation to make such applications as the pre- sent at the earliest possible moment after the matter had been passed by the Council— The Inspector: I don't know how they are going to Anglesey can boast of two very old voters who force that, registered their votes in the present election. One old Councillor Pemberton: And before any of the work man at Llanfechell who registered his vote was 100 or purchases have been proceeded with. • years old last August, and despite his great age he , The Inspector: You don't suggest that that has been walked to the polling booth At Dwy ran Mr. Pritchard done in this case? of Dinam Hall, recorded his vote, an CI he comes very point of age to the" ancient "at Llanf hell near in Councillor Pemberton: No.ec , The Inspector : Had you not better ask the Town being 98. • Council to compel the Local Government Board to I hold the inquiry earl ier—( laughter)—because the petition for this was signed on the 14th December, and the application was made on the 18th. Councillor Pemberton : No, that is hardly what I mean. It is now something like 12 or 14 months since this matter was passed by the Council. The Town Clerk Yes, but those proceedings were taken under the powers of the Act of 1899. Conncillor Pemberton, continuing, said his point was that if the application had been made earlier this inquiry would have been held earlier and then there would have been such opposition that the Local Government Board would not have granted the applica,- tion. This scheme would cost .£20,000 altogether, and things had gone so far that their opposition would not be effective now from a business point of view.i They ought to have the opportunity of opposing n the initial stage. The Town Clerk : There has already been an inquiry with reference to some of the property. Councillor Lord said lie had opposed this scheme from the beginning. The Town Clerk, earlier on, said it was acondition in the agreement with MessrslGarnett that the property at the Itylands-street end should be purchased by.therorporation.That conditon never came before the members of the Town Council and that did not appear in Messrs. Garnett's proposal to the Council. If they had been aware of that condition, he had not the slightest doubt that the scheme would have been further opposed. The Town Clerk : The. Property Purchasing Com- mittee agreed to it. Councillor Lord: It never came before the Coiincil. The offer as laict before the Council did not include the purchase of the property. This is the first time mem- bers of the Council—not the committee—have heard anything about this arrangement to purchase this property. It certainly is not embodied in the minutes of the Council.</p>
        <p>Everyone should know a fey.. sPiple but important facts about food. If the things we eat and drink are to do us any good, they must supply amongAt other things three main elements to the body. They must contain material for making flesh, blood and bone ; they must provide substances for creating energy and vitality, and those to give the body heat. Now, surprising though the fact may seem, there is hardly an article of food I that contains in itself so much nourishment ready for the body to use as the little dried currants which we put into cakes and bread. A pound of currants has in it thrice as much actual food as a pound of lean beef. A great physician has recommended currant bread as a perfect article of diet. He suggests twenty or even thirty parts of currants to seventy of dough. The currants render the bread not only more palatable, but generously amplify its nutriment. Cakes made in same way of similar ingredients form a welcome am wholesome adjunct to the household dietary, and at the merest trifle in the way of cost.</p>
        <p>The Inspector : I don't understand how such a thing can come about when here is talß petition signed by the Mayor on behalf of the Council.</p>
