      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <p>My Dear Children,— During the next few months I shall have monthly competitions for our members. Besides reading the weekly contributions, I want you all to take a further interest in our columns. This month I shall have a competition for girls only, as it is to .be for doll dressing. I want you all to try hard to win one of the prizes; they Will be three in number, and all well worth trying for. You need not get good dolls, nor dress them expensively. the points that will be taken into consideration will be neatness, good work, and taste displayed. The competition will close on March ist, so you all have plenty of time, and I advise you to com- mence at once. The dolls will be given to .the poor, or else to a Children's hospital, where I am sure they will give great plea- sure. Remember that the prizes will be'ex- ceptionally good ones for this competition, and, in fact, for all the monthly ones that I shall give. Next month I shall give a special competition for boys, so you must all be on the look-out for it tO..appear. •</p>
        <p>I have great pleasure in*telling• you all that the prizes in connection with the com- petition tor the making of articles of cloth- ing for the poor have been awarded. as follows :—First prize to Mi*ss Olive Thacker, second prize to Miss Doris Thacker, and third prize to Miss Alice Coates; and I feel sure that you will all be pleased with the prizes that I have sent. The work was ex- ceptionally good, and I am sure the poor will be most grateful for these useful articles. I hope those who have not received prizes this time will try for the •competition given above. I have a prize waiting for Miss Alice Latham, for the contributions printed below, but I have not her address, so if she will please send it to me, the prize shall be forwarded at ,dnce.</p>
        <p>, With all good wishes from UNCLE NED,</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l hope you are quite well, and that you spent a very happy Christmas. I need not tell you that I spent a most enjoyable time. Some of my sisters are grown up, and are away at business, but they were all home for Christmas Day. I also had a great many presents, and we had heaps of fun. Thank you so much for the book. I have read it all through, and I enjoyed it im- mensely. I am sending you a little con- tribution to the " Children's Corner," which I.hope yyou will like.—Much love from your little niece, FATHER IS COMING. The clock is on the stroke of six, The father's work is done; Sweep up the hearth and mend the fire. And put the kettle on; The wild night wind is blowing cold, 'Tis dreary crossing o'er the wold. He is crossing) o'er the wold apace, He is stronger than the storm;</p>
        <p>He does not reel the cold, not, he, His heart it is so warm: - For father's heart is stout and true As ever human bosom knew. He makes all tciil, all hardship' light; Would all men were the same! So ready to be pleased, so kind, So very slow to blame. Folks need not be unkind, austere; For love hath readier will than fear. Nay; do not close the shutters child, .For far along the lane The little window looks, and he Can .see it shining u plain; I've heard him say he loves to mark The cheerful firelight through the dark. And we'll do all that father likes; His wishes are so few: Would they were more, that every hour Some wish of his. I knew! I'm sure it makes a happy When I can please him any way. know he's coming by this sign— . That baby's almost wild; See how he laughs, and crows, and stares: Heaven bless the merry child! His father's self in face and limb, And father's heart is strong in him. Hark! hark! -I hear his footsteps now, He's through the garden gate: • Run, little Bess, and open the door, And do not let him wait. Shout, baby, shout, and clap thy hands, For father on the threshold stands. . MARGARET MARY PRESS.</p>
        <p>. PICTURES IN THE FIRE, Mother had gone down the. street; and left Bobbie and Nannie to play till she came back. "What shall we play at?" said Bobbie, after mother's bonnet. had disappeared round the corner. '</p>
        <p>maw</p>
