      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <p>LIST OF NtUT</p>
        <p>MEMBERS,</p>
        <p>Elsie. M. • Hall, Harold Sharpe, Connie Saunders, Nancy Porter, _ Sidney Hewitt, Lilian Deakin, Leonard Hewitt, Herbert Gray, Nellie Baxter, Walter .T.'Chatterton, C. E. Lowe, ThomasOram, William Price, Charles 'Wheeldon John Wheeldon, William Preece, George Taylor, Albert Malbon, Frederick Woodward, Thomas Corbett, Bert- ram Bakewell, George ,Orton, Alec Veitch, William Preece Brown, George Wilson, Mark Brownhall, . Samuel Brunt, Ernest Hackett, George Goodhead, Edward Gwii- liam, James Heath, William Heath, and Albert Wilson.</p>
        <p>AN ADVENTURE WITH A MAN-EATER,</p>
        <p>Soon after we had encamped, the Old man's grandson appeared. We sent, a message by him to the villagers that we were there to slay the tiger, and asked them to send their best 'hunters, with a bullock to be used for bait. We had our own tent with us, and this we set up on the outskirts of the village. Knowing that there was a man- eater in the neighbourhood, it behoved us to keep the closest watch during the night. In order to do this more effectually, we built a big fire, and divided the night into watches. One of the sowars had first watch, and we gave him strict orders that he was not to sleep even so much as a wink, for his life depended upon his vigilance. A tiger will never hesitate to attack a sleep- ing man, and he crawls up so quietly that the victim has ,no warning of the crafty animal's attack until the catlike spring is made upon the prey. •We ourselves lay down inside • the tent, previously, however, covering the sights of our rifles with pieces of white cotton, so that we might have some- thing to guide us if we should, have to aim them suddenly in the dark. It seemed to. me that my eyes had hardly been closed for five minutes, when I was startled by the most unearthly shriek I ever heard. It was but one, terrifying cry, and then all was silent. But too well- I knew what it meant. (To be Concluded.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <p>LAM, RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 27, 1906.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>OLD AGE PENSION</head>
        <p>OLD AGE PENSION</p>
        <p>SIR B(.)SIDIN T. LERCH (Aidlto). of " _Thee to .rroi.ide .for Ohl Ayr," 4c,),</p>
        <p>It has long been admitted to be a social crime that the thrifty should not Only have to. provide for themselves and their families, but, through the rates, have to keep !tie lacy and the dissolute in prisons, worlibouses and hospitals. A• coroner, in the north of England welt ex- pressed the feeling when he said : "If a man can spend money in drink be was able to save something for a time of necessity, and it was a crime for him to seek relief from the Poor Law Guardians: It was, in fact, as bad as if he put his hand into the pocket of a thrifty person, bud took money from it. Such thriftless people had no conscience, and looked upou. parish aid as a right to which they were entitled. • Anybody who constantly listened to the evidence given at inquests must come to , the conclusion that national assurance was an absolute necessity for the protection of the general public." Spurred by the importance of the question, ninny philanthropists have devoted themselves to suggestions for remedying the evil. It hav been ascertained that 1 in 35 of our population receive poor-law relief, and that 1 in 7 of the people over 00 years of age are in workhouses. By Parliamentary papers issued in 18P1, it is seen that 14,808 of the inmates had tried to be providen,t, but had come to grief largely through being members of rotten friendly societies.</p>
        <p>PENSION SCHEMES ALREADY SUGGESTED.</p>
        <p>Canon Blackley about 20 years ago first brought forward a provident scheme. He was in favour of a compulsory and universal pay- ment of £lO by persons of both sexes between 18 and 21, with cumulative interest at 4 per cent. • This was to secure Bs. per week during sickness, and ss. per week superannuation after the age of 65. The payments of those who did not reacts that age were forfeited. A Parliamen- tary Committee objected to it because of com- pulsion and on the ground that it might be financially unsound. Next came the proposi- tion of the Reverend William Moore Ede. He had been much struck by the unsoundness of many small friendly societies. Half of them dare not make returns to the Registrar ; and of those who did, 676 had each a deficiency of over £l,OOO. He reasoned that baying a national system of education; there ought to be a national system of provision for old age. He urged, too, that the cost of collecting societies was extrava- gant, often 40 to 60 per cent. on the amount re- ceived,, and that they only could exist through 6thie profit_ made on lapsed policies. Ile favoured superannuation only, mid his plan was that all young people, from the peer to the peasant, should be compelled to lay by 1:12 to £l4 their early years, which, accumulating at inter- est, and supplemented by unclaimed amounts, would provide a pension of 58. per week in Old age. He stated that £l4 10s. funded at the age of 21 would yield a sufficient fund, • and sug- gested one penny per week each being paid by the employer, the employed, and the State. Mr. Charles Booth proposed the simplest and most drastic scheme. He would simply provide for old age by a contribution out of the nafiontil exchequer of ss. per week for every one over 65 years of age. The cost he 'estimated at from £14,000,000. to £17,000,000 per annum. Another well-known man, Mr. Howard Vincent;i. M. I'., was in favour of the State making a capitation grant of 58. per annum per head to friendly so- cieties in order to secure a pension. of 10s. per week at the age of 65. Dr. Ilubter, M.P., formulated a scheme for Scotland which was to give, at the age of 65, 10s. per week to 1111 artisan, -is. 6d. to .a labfTurer, and ss. to a working woman, and suggested a payment of is. per week, of which a third was to be provided by the State, the estimated cost being £4,000,000 per annum. •</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY soclrTrEs AND SAYINGS BANKS.</p>
        <p>Having given a resume of various suggestions, let us next consider the existing agencies for thrift. First of all are the trustee and village penny savings banks. So long as responsible gentlemen can be found to devote themselves ,to their management, they are of inestimable bene- fit to the community. But repetitions of the Macclesfield and Cardiff fiascoes have to he guarded against, and it ought to be made clear that Government is only responsible for tlie money invested with them. Post Office Savings Banks are guaranteed by the State, give abso- lute security, and bring opportunities for thrift to the very doors of the people. They are a boon, and are doing a large and increasing business, as the deposits show. Yet, singular to say, notwithstanding the offer of great advant- ages, they have not yet been used or appreciated as much as they ought to be in the assurance and annuity department. In Austria the post office savings banks are helped by the postmen, each of whom is a collector, and empowered to receive deposits, and negotiate business. Some such cheap and convenisnt system might well be adopted in England. Friendly and trade union societies have long been the chief , avenues by Obich the working classes provide for sickness and incapacity. Without doubt they have sup- plied a great want, but sometimes through not being based on sound actuarial principles, mid making too large advances for sickness and death, they have found their funds depleted, when heavy calls have come upon them. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>T;NSOtiND IN PRINCIPLE.</head>
        <p>T;NSOtiND IN PRINCIPLE.</p>
        <p>In 1889, Mr. Sutton, an eminent actuary, gave evidence before a Parliamentary Committee, and made clear the danger. To the credit of Friendly Societies be it said that they have since been strengthening their position, and many of the best are now thoroughly sound. They deal with sickness, loss of wage, and old age, at a moderate cost, so far as working ex- penses are concerned, and embody compulsion , to a certain extent, because once a member a man cannot leave the ranks without forfeiting his previous contributions. Another provision ' for the future is made by collecting burial and assurance societies. Unfortunately the advant- ages they offer in the case of the death of chil- dren have caused assurances to be effected by drunken, dissolute, and careless parents, who have neglected their children in order to secure a stun of ready money at their decease. No doubt this has helped to increase the infant mor- tality. They too often fail when most wanted. As an instance, the Parliamentary Committee in 1889 reported that the Yorkshire Provident As- surplice Company had 150,000 policies in exis- tence, with an adverse balance of lls, at the bank, and £3OO in local debentures. This means ' that the remaining policy holders, who had sub- , scribed for years in order to provide for old age, would never get a penny. Time will not permit 1! a search into the history of Railway Pension Funds. Suffice it to say that they are formed by a compulsory payment by the servants of 2i per cent, to which a similar sum is added by the railway company. This provides for sickness and superannuation. The Civil Service, too, has established a superannuation fund, also on a compulsory basis. A SYSTEM IN SUCCESSFUL OPERATION.</p>
        <p>• The Manchester Corporation, judiiit-ig. that a system of thrift would benefit their employees, adopted a scheme peculiar to themselves. -After much difficulty, they 'got Parliament to grant them enabling powers. Then they took counsel with the representatives of the Trade Unions, and decided to leave sickness to such bodies, ancl make provision for death, infirmity, or old age. Entrance for old servants, and those earn- :3os. per week or under, was made voluntary, but compulsory to all others except casuals, and the Police and Fire Brigade, who had a fund, of their own. Warned by the experience of the former, who, through miscalculation, got £120,000 wrong,—which had to be made good out of the rates, they determined to avoid the prinoitiVe of averaging life, and build up a fund iii alm‘Mifely. sound piinciples. Every member has a separate account, and contributes W. in the eet of his wages, to which the Cor- poration add 3d. The accumulated mud is in- vested with the Corporation, who allow 4 per cent, for it. A man can leave at any time, and take • with him his own savings. When he reaches 65, or is incapacitated, he gets his own savings with the Corporation's addition. With this he can buy an annuity, start his family in business, or make provision for his family. The Manchester Corporation Thrift Fund has now worked smoothly since 1891, and the accumulated fund amounts to nearly £120,000. A committee , DC the clintributors manage the fund at a very small expense, and it is gratifying to say that , many of the lads earning 0 to 10 shillings per week join, and that two-thirds of the ev.do\w-,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <p>wno ate /104 compelled, nave voluntarily joined. The cost to the rittes is u third port c,i 0 penuy in the pound.</p>
        <p>CONTINENTAL STAT ASSURANCES.</p>
        <p>Abroad we find that Holland in 1880 formu- lated a voluntary system to secure ss. per week on reaching 60. The employers pay 21., and the workmen 31,td. per mint per week, at 21 years of age, increasing gradually, until if he enters at 35 it is Ild, per week. Denmark has in operation a law giving every man and woman a right tp a pension at 60 years of age, paid by the parish, the State on certain conditions repaying halt the money to the pariah. Criminals, beggars; and evil-livers are not eligible for a pension. • Gentling in. 1882, passed Neompulsory act for insurance against sickness, to which ethployer and employed contribute. In 1884 an act making it compulsory for employers' to form an Accident Fund was passed, and in 1889 her Purl hune»t passed is pension system for infirmity and old age. All wage earners of less than £lOO per im- lium are compelled to pay a sum varying front 4d. to 3d. per week, the employers a like amount, and also the Government. For this payment, a pension not exceeding 46. per week is granted when a contributor is 70. ile Must have paid 30 years, mid if be leaves earlier his contributions are forfeited. Seventeen million men are now insured.. The, whole system was built up with military precision and carried through by the dominating will of Prince life-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>THELWALL.</head>
        <p>THELWALL.</p>
        <p>MEETING or THE PARISH COUNCIL.—The quarterly meeting of the Thelwall Parish Council was held in the schoolroom on Tuesday evening when there were present ,Mr. Herbert Hatton presiding, Messrs. J. Reynolds, T. Skelland, and W. B. Cook (clerk). The Clerk said he had written to Mr. Turnbull, Lyme-street Station, Liverpool, drawing attention to the lighting at Thelwall station which required improving, and had received a reply stating that the matter would be given attention. The Chairman said that new lamps had now been put on the station and there was a great improvement. The clerk was instructed to write to the railway company thanking them for their concession.—The -Clerk said the year was commenced with a balance in band of V. The expenditure during the sear amounted to £1 16s, leaving a balance of £1 4s. There had been no call on the overseers, but the previous year there was a call for .210. The Chairman said it was the lightest expendi- ture that had been incurred since the establishment of the Council. The accounts were passed.</p>
