      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>LIBERAL WAVE.</head>
        <p>THE LIBERAL WAVE.</p>
        <p>MR. KING TRIUMPHS IN THE KNUTSFORD DIVISION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>Sweeping Victory in Carnarvon Boroughs. – MR. R. A. NAYLOR'S DEFEAT.</head>
        <p>Sweeping Victory in Carnarvon Boroughs. - MR. R. A. NAYLOR'S DEFEAT.</p>
        <p>MR. A. 3. KING (L) 5,29G HON. A. DE TATTON EGERTON (C) 4,59 G</p>
        <p>Liberal majority THE NEW MEMBER.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Alfred John King, is the second son of the late Alderman John King, father of the Manchester Council and mayor in 1875. He was born in Manchester in 1839, and educated at Oliver's Mount College, Scarborough, and at Owens College. By business he is a bleacher and finisher. Mr. King is a member of the Cheshire County Council, president of the Knutsford District Liberal Association, and a representative of Victoria University. Although always a Liberal, he took little, active interest in politics until 1900, when, it is said, as it member of the Society of Friends, his indignation was aroused at the policy which had led to the South African war. He is a B.Sc. of London University. After a strenuous fieht Mr. A. J. King has at last wrested the representation of the Knutsford Parlia- mentary Division from the hands of the Conservative party. " When he commenced " nursing " the constitu- ency Mr. King's chances were below zero, hut with the continued rise of the Liberal wave his prospects improved and the enthusiasm of his supporters increased to a corresponding extent. Stockton Heath, which is included in the Division, has long been regarded as the hot-bed and stronghold of Toryism, but constant and undiminished energy has produced a change and at the close of the poll on Saturday it was remarked by one prominent worker that if all other districts had polled as well as Stockton Heath a great victory was assured for the progressive forces. Saturday opened fine, but cold,and in Warrington's suburb the supporters of both parties were early at work. Motor-cars with which to carry the electors to the poll were placed at the dis- posal of each candidate and Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall were active in support of Mr. Egerton. The Tories outwardly .were confident but panic measures were resorted to as, early in the day, a poster was run out declaring that Mr. Egerton "was opposed and always had been opposed to any increased taxation of food ! "—forgetful of the fact that Mr. Egerton " backed " Mr. Chamberlain in preferenc to Mr. Balfour as being "the faster horse." Mr. Arthur Stanley, the new M.P. for the Eddisbury division, rendered yeoman service in the constituency with his motor throughout the day. At the committee-rooms the greatest eonfi- deuce and enthus:asm prevailed. From other districts in the scattered constituency (there were twenty-three polling booths) excellent reports were received from time to time. The rain, which throughout the morning and afternoon had kept off, began to fall about five o'clock, and did not cease after. Between six and seven it fell heavily. The Liberals, however, were nowhere disposed to be disheartened. They believed the bulk of electors had polled, and that the remainder would not be daunted by the weather. At the close of the poll the ballott boxes were con- veyed to Knutsford where the counting took place, the result being declared on Monday. Early in the morning a crowd began to assemble on the open space in front of the Knutsford Sessions House, electors from all parts of the Division being present, and when the returning officer appeared, accompanied by the two candidates, several thousands of people were waiting outside. Only those in the very front could hear the figures announced, but this was not necesssary as the result was known to all in an instant, red and white handkerchiefs, ribbons and favours being waved in the air amidst enthusiastic cheering. After the usual votes of thanks had been proposed and seconded by Mr. King and Mr. Egerton respectively, the candidates came down to meet their friends. The enthusiasm of the Liberals knew no bounds. Mr. King was seized and carried shoulder-high to his committee-room. It was a long time before he could speak—indeed, before he attempted to do so. No sooner had three cheers been proposed on one side of the crowd than three were proposed on the other side. Lusty cheers were also given for Mrs. King, who was helped on to the table where her hus- band was standing. Miss King and her two little brothers were then lifted up to their parents and more hearty cheers were given. When at length comparative silence bad been obtained Mr. King said :—" Ladies and gentlemen, I congratulate you all upon the magnificent victory which you have won in Knutsford for good government and Free Trade. I wish to thank everybody, all these splendid workers , who in all parts of the di vision have spared no pains, ' have turned from no sacrifice to bring about this magni- ficent result. We have had tremendous forces to fight against. We have fought against the tradition of a continuous Conservative majority and against territorial influences. (Loud cheers.) I recognise in this victory the awakening of the electors to a new sense of freedom. They have become free men in a free position. I believe that for the first time the majority of the electors in this division have voted according to the dictates of their conscience for the man they considered would best represent their cause. I must recognise, of course, that we have had splendid help from the Free Trade Unionists and Con- servatives,'and I thank them for the patriotic way in which they have put aside party considerations and voted for Free Trade (cheers). I believe now that we are perfectly safe to have a solid Cheshire for Liberalism. I know well that all eyes have been turned on to the struggle in this division, and I say confidently that what Knutsford has done to-day. Altrincham, Hyde, Crewe, Northwich and Wirral will doeto-morrow.Once more! thank you all most heartily." (Prolonged cheeing). A bouquet of red and white flowers was handed up for Mrs. King, who has rendered loyal assistance to her husband all through the long and arduous contest. Later in the day Mr. King made a tour of the division, and was everywhere most enthusiastically received.</p>
        <p>THE CONSERVATIVE CANDIDATE AND HIS DEFEAT. Immediately after the declaration of the poll the Hon. Alan de Tatton Egerton walked to the Town Hall from which building he returned thanks to his supporters. i He said : "We must not despair. Our honour s safe. We have perhaps been defeated by some of our friends departing from 119. (Shame.) We must remember also this. There have been for two years past a lot of shameful posters, especially with regard to Chinese labour. (Hear, hear). They affected the electorate very considerably. There is a wave passing over the country, no doubt, but that wave will come back, and will leave some of them high and dry. (Hear, hear.) We shall then come and help to pull them out of the mud. (Cheers.) I have to thank all those who have worked for me throughout the division.—(We will do it again)—especially those who have done such good work, voluntary work, in sending out the innumerable letters. I think I am indebted to a good many of the ladies for clerical assistance. We will have now to set our House in order and see where the leakage has taken place, and we shall have to put our hacks into it. I thought I , had done that for the past two years. (Hear, hear.) Of course now, according to Hr. King, the new member— (hooting and cries of "Bab ")—the division wanted somebody who would be always looking after the work to be done. lam afraid he will have enough to do if be intends to attend as many of the divisions in the House as T have attended in the past. I wished him good-luck in the counting-room, but I told him I could not wish my worst enemy a harder fate than to be in attendance as I have been in the House of Commons, morning, noon and night, for the last five years. I think be will soon have enough of it." (Hear, hear.) Without saying morc beyond wishing his supporters " Good-bye," Mr. Egerton left the hall for the carriage awaiting him in the street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>CONTEST IN CARNARVON BOROUGHS.</head>
        <p>THE CONTEST IN CARNARVON BOROUGHS.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. A. Naylor's Heavy Defeat.</p>
        <p>The election in Carnarvon Boroughs took place on Saturday and resulted in a sweeping victory for Mr. D. Lloyd-George who was opposed by Mr. R. A. Naylor, of Warrington. The Liberal majority was increased to 1,221 and a scene of jubilation such as has seldom been witnessed in Carnarvonshire took place at Carnarvon on Saturday I night when the figures were announced. The Mayor of 1 Crrnarvon (Mr. W. A. Darbishire) being the returning I officer, the ballot-boxes of all the boroughs were brought to that town, and the result of the poll was declared some time after midnight from the balcony of the Guildhall. At that time the space adjacent to the hall was packed with people, and the greatest excitement prevailed, as, indeed, had been the case for several hours previously. Though the issue was a foregone conclusion, yet intense interest was taken in the final scene. Processions of Mr. Lloyd George's supporters, sporting yellow badges, paraded the streets incessantly, with a police-constable stationed here and there in their ranks to guard against any unseemly scenes. Conflicts between the opposing parties were of freequent occur- rence, but everything passed off without any serious disturbance. One incident was greatly regretted. A crowd of youths, said to be mostly disaffected quarrymen from Nantlle, surrounded Mr. Lloyd Carter,the Under-Sheriff of the county, a well-known member of the Conserva- tive party, and treated him rather badly. By the aid of the police he was able to reach a hair-dresser's shop in Bangor-street, where he awaited a favourable oppor- tunity of proceeding home. Meanwhile several con- stables were engaged keeping tne crowd away from the shop. Mr. Carter complained of having been kicked obout the legs, awl on Sunday he was confined to bed suffering from shock. Damage was also done to the</p>
        <p>plate-glass shop window of Mr. J. It. Pritchard, a pro- minent Liberal. Counting of the votes proceeded immediately upon the arrival of the boxes from the southern boroughs at ten o'clock. By the same train Mr. Lloyd-George arrived, and the welcome which greeted him betokened the tremendous ovation which was in store for him at a later hour. Mr. Lloyd-George, who had been informed of the unfortunate display of hostility to Mr. Carter, lost no time in strongly depreciating such conduct. lie harangued a large crowd near the Guildhall on abstain- ing from acts of violence, pointing out that they were not his friends who resorted to the ill-treatment of opponents. From ten o'clock until long after midnight an immense surging concourse of people was assembled in Turf-square and Eastgate-street, patiently an aiting the declaration of the poll. Shortly after half-past eleven the word went forth that Mr. Lloyd George was in with a majority of 1,185, but nothing officially was known till 12.25, when the figures were announced as follows:— Lloyd George Naylor 1,997</p>
        <p>Majority Thereupon the multitude broke out into a deafening roar of cheering, which was taken up by groups of people within hearing distance in other parts of the town. After the declaration Mr. Lloyd George moved a vote of thanks to the Mayor. In the absence of Mr. Naylor, who had left the town early in the day, the motion was seconded by Mr. Nee,his election agent. The figures made it clear that the poll had been an unusually heavy one, not more than 442 voters being unaccounted for. There were six spoilt votes. Bangor, the largest lof the boroughs, polled 1,812, leaving 112 unaccounted for ; Carnarvon 1,563 leaving 120 ; Con ay 715, leaving 53 ; Pwelhili 588, leaving 47 ; Nevin 306, leaving 75 ; Criccieth 230, leaving 22. The final episode in the celebration of the Conserva- tive defeat was the singing of the Dead March by a ' procession of jubilant Liberals. They walked with measured steps, pausing for a short while in front of the Royal Hotel, Mr. Naylor's head-quarters when visiting the town. The effect at two o'clock in the morning was not a little weird. THE NEW MEMBER AND HIS CONSTITUENTS.</p>
        <p>ELOQUENT EXPRESSION OF THANES. By arrangement, Mr. Lloyd George, escorted by his supporters, was driven to tootle-square, and from his carriage he addressed a vast crowd. "The flag of Liberty has not yet been taken down from the hills of Arfon," he said, It braved the storms of 1895 and 1900 and, while the sun is in the meridian and the clouds are parted, it still floats in the breeze. The people of Car- narvon Boroughs have this day proved that the conscience of \Vales is not in the market. We have proved that the Welshman is too much of a gentleman to sell his principles for a dole. (Cheers.) The old nation has risen from one end to the other, and there will not be a single Conservative left this day week. (Laughter and cheers.) For the first time i Wales has become one in the cause of freedom, and the nation, like the Israelites of old, has commenced its march from the land of bondage without leaving a !single tribe behind. I thank you from the bottom of my heart for the victory )ou have enabled me to win. It is your devotion, your loyalty to principle, that has done it. You have faced all sacrifices and won the greatest victory ever achieved in Carnarvon Boroughs, I will ask you now to disperse to your homes. Remem- ber the principles we have fought for, and let us do nothing that will tarnish victory and drag our name in the mire." (Loud cheers). Mr. Lloyd George's carriage was then dragged as far as the residence of Mr. N. Roberts, his election agent, whose guest he was. Before leaving Canarvon on Tuesda yMr.Lloyd George called upon Mr. Lloyd Carter and expressed his great regret at the unfortunate occurence of the previous night, adding that he was really too angry to speak about it. The following laconic telegram was sent on Saturday to Colonel Bromley Davenport, who was defeated at Macclesfield :—" We are satisfied. Are you ?—Welsh Quarrymen's Union." INTERVIEW WITH MR. ..I`.7AYLOR.</p>
      </div>
