      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>NOT A PERSONAL DISAPPOINTMENT.</head>
        <p>NOT A PERSONAL DISAPPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>A Press repreeentative interviewed Mr. R. A. Naylor on Sunday. Mr. Naylor had taken cold on Friday night owing to motoring without the usual wraps, and was suffering from sore throat. He proceeded to Conway, and remained quietly over the week-end at the Castle Hotel. Asked it he would care to make a statement for publication, he replied that he was grats ful on behalf of the Unionist party for all that had been done for him during a very arduous contest by his many friends. When the ballot was almost over, and when he and his sister had visited every polling station except the new one at Deganwy, they considered as to how they would like the result to be, and they concluded that after their experiences of cam- paigning, if their personal feelings only were to be con- sidered, they would prefer to lose, but in the interests of the cause for which he had fought he would very much regret being defeated. He had done his best on behalf of the cause which hehad championed in response to great pressure, but was in no sense personally dis- appointed by the result. If he had been elected he should have riven up his whole time to the work en- trusted to him, and would have served the boroughs faithfully, and well, but that would have meant his giving up other matters in which he and hi a sister had taken an interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>DISGRACEFUL SCENES AT CONWAY".</head>
        <p>DISGRACEFUL SCENES AT CONWAY".</p>
        <p>It is to be regretted tnat some disgraceful scenes were witnessed in Conway after the declaration of the poll. The first news of Mr. Lloyd-George's success was communicated to the crowd shortly after midnight in Lancaster-square. At that moment the square was filled with a concourse numbering several thousands. The majority of the people went homewards when they had heard the result, but a crowd of 800 to 1,000 young fellows marched round the town by High-street, Castle- street, and Rose Hill-street. They were singing and cheering as they went along. When opposite the Castle Hotel they " boohed "au,l groaned for Mr. Naylor, who makes this hotel his headquarters when in Conway, but as a couple of police-constables were at hand there was only a momentary halt. Further expressions of adverse [feeling were forthcoming when the crowd passed the vicarage, and it appears, instead of marching back into Lancaster-square, the crowd turued into the by-street where the Conservative Club is situated. In the club were assembled a large number of Con- servatives who had waited there for the telegram giving Mr. George's exact majority. It had been freely alleged in the town on Friday morning that the Con- servatives at the club meant to have a hosepipe ready for the Liberals if they came into the vicinity after the declaration of the poll and that the crowd would get a good sousing. The ch:l3 premises comprise a billiard- room and reading-room, and are approached by a rather long passage. The strong iron gates at the entrance to the passage were closed and locked. When the crowd approached the club they sang the refrain ending with the words, "Poor Mr. Naylor," halting when they were opposite the gates, which, according to the version of persons inside the club, were shaken violently. This is denied by Liberals who were outside who state that the crowd was perfectly orderly until someone in the club passage threw a bucketful of water into the street. The water passed between the bars of the gate and fell upon several young fellows whe were standing near. This convinced the crowd that the hosepipe threat was being carried out, and: there was a great outcry, and some stones were throwa.</p>
        <p>....„ The crash of breaking glass as the stones hit the windows of the club committee-room and the caretaker's apartments on the right and left of the entrance and above it increased the excitement both inside and out- siee the club, and missiles began to fly in both direc- tions while the men around the gates wrenched them otf direc- tions, stout hinges and Hung them open. A board with the name of the club painted upon it was torn down. Evidence of the fact that both beseiged and beseigers used projectiles is afforded by the appearance of the lead-light window over the door. in one part the lead strips were bent outwards, and in another part inwards. When the gates were burst open the crowd without rushed into the passage, but were met by a compact body of the Unionists inside, and did not penetrate far. The small force of police in the town with Superintendent Rees,arrived,and with considerable difficulty the crowd were induced to disperse. The only instance of physical injury appears to be a blow on the nose sustained by the caretaker, from his being struck by one of the gates as it was forced open, and a slight cut on the forehead by a stone thrown from the club sustained by a young man who was on the outskirts of the crowd. All the glass in the committee-room window was shattered, and panes were broken in four other windows. ft appears that Superintendent Rees had some reason to think that a hostile demonstration might be made at the Castle Hotel and at the vicarage where, in fact,two panes of glass were broken, and he had these places guarded by policemen, but it was not thought any trouble would arise at the club, it being off the usual route of political processions. Several constables had been brought in from Llandudno, but they were even then only a very small body. After the dispersal of crowd it a-as noticed by tbeconstable on duty in Lower Gate-street that the Liberal Club premises there were intact, but a few minutes later, on again passing the Iclub, he found two panes of glass smashed. This, it is supposed, was the retaliatory deed of some women.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>-- IN THE NEWTON DIVISION.</head>
        <p>-- IN THE NEWTON DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Victory for Labour. J. A. SEDDON (Lab.) I'COLONEL R. PILKINGTON (C)</p>
        <p>Labour gain—Majority 541 r—it keen contest in the Newton Division has resulted in great victory for labonr, in the person of 111 r. J. Seddon, over the Conservative forces as represented by Colonel Pilkington. The seat had always been regarded as one of the Tory strongholds of Lancashire, one which Mr. Gladstone himself had failed to secure, but the local Labour organization, encouraged by their successes in St. Helens and Ice, which divisions adjoin the Newton one, were hopeful of snatching it from</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, January 27, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 27, 1906.</p>
        <p>Colonel Pilkington. There were thirty-six polling booths throughout the division, which is a very scattered one, and comprises Newton, Earlestown, Ashton, Haydock, Billinge, Winwick, Bamford, Eccleston, and parts of St. Helens and Warrington. In most of the places polling was very steady till five o'clock, then there was a rush. Colonel Pilkington was very well supplied with motors and conveyances in the various districts ; while Mr. Seddon's workers, although not having nearly so many vehicles to bring the electors to the poll, were not dismayed, but performed their daties lin a most unassuming manner. At St. Helens I and Newton the voting was stated to have favoured the late member, whileltainford, Warrington, and Rain- hill were pretty evenly divided. Earlestown Havdock, andishton were the main places upon which Mr.Seddon relied to be returned. _ _</p>
        <p>At Haydock and Earlestown work was suspended at noon,on Tuesday and at the former place large crowds of , peopleasserubled near the committee-rooms and polling I stations. Early in the afternoon about a dozen work- I men from Pemberton dressed as Chinese and represent- ! ing Chinese slavery caused much excitement in Haydock as they passed through the district on their way to Earlestown. They, however, had a warm reception on ' arriving at Earlestown, for, as they were passing the Conservative Club in Earle-street, they were attacked by the opposition and broken up. On the advice of the police the " poor Chinamen " doffed their rig-out, washed themselves at the police station, and resumed their ordinary attire. A regrettable accident occurred at the Eccleston polling district about noon yesterday. It appears that Mr. John Shaw, of Ecclestou, and Mr. Arthur Robinson, who were helping Colonel Richard Pill ington and bringing up voters by means of motor- carp, arrived at the polling station at Christ Church, Eccleston, about the same time. When they were going round the corner the motor-cars collided very violently and a man named James Owen, of Springfield-lane, who was also helping to bring up the voters, was thrown out and very badly hurt, his leg being broken. He was taken borne and attended by a doctor,and afterwards conveyed to the hospital.</p>
        <p>.11. L. OUTHWAITE.</p>
        <p>I have been carried off their feet, they have been deluded by the most scandalous misrepresentations, they have NOTES ON GAMES AND failed to use their judgment and intelligence." It may, perhaps, interest the public to know how the verdict of Birmingham was secured, and how Mr. AMLETICS. Chamberlain appeals to " judgmeet and intelligence." In the first place, it has to be remembered that Mr. Chem berlain does not control the Birmingham electorate Rugby Football. by virtue of his reputation as a statesman, but as "boss" of a Tammany organization. Birmingham is worked by the "machine "on New York lines. The control of [By " Ovalist."l the machine does not extend beyond Birmingham, and Warrington have won once more, and they will hence the Liberal victories in the adjacent divisions. The organization is perfect. Professional men dare not probably return to see-saw methods to-day by losing oppose, tradespeople find it advantageous to give lit at Salfor.l. There is no stability about the team, their adherence, Boss " Chamberlain dictates civic somehow, and anything but winning form is di3played appointments, can humble the exalted, and exalt the on foreign soil. good deal is due to want of honest base. When I was honoured by an invitation from endeavour. lam sure of this, for to watch the War- the West Birmingham Liberal Association to contest the division I replied that before giving rington mea away from home one would think a definite answer I should hold a preliminary they were . a different team altogether. Though series of meetings. Mr. Poulteney was then Editor of their record is a poor one, they are a stronger team the " Birmingham Daily Post," and gave an assurance than most people think, and if I had each man's that I should have fair treatment in the news columns assurance that he meant to do his utmost at Salford of his paper. Mr. Chamberlain was on the Continent. to-day, I should pin my faith to the primrose and This campaign of about a month's duration was in blue. They usually show what they are capable of every way a success, and made a marked impression. I ' I held fifty open-air meetings, and the " Daily Post " when at home,as witness the fact that they have only failed to get the. maximum points out of their visitors published some remarkable articles in vindication of Free Trade. Then Mr. Chamberlain returned. Mr. on one occasion since the beginning of October. The Poulteney resigned. He accepted loss of his high teams which have won at Warrington are Broughton, position rather than do violence to his "judgment and , Hull Kingston Rovers, and Salford, so that it is lintelligence "and to his personal honour by remaining obvious hew little of their 40l per cant Warrington in charge of a journal now to be devoted to " Tariff have gained away from home. Reform." It meant, as it happened,renunciation of life I itself, for the shock prostrated Mr. Poulteney, and he ' died re the shouting had ceased that hailed the victory ! of "judgment and intelligence "in Birmingham. j It must not be thought that lam making a personal ; complaint as regards the electors. The men of West , Birmingham gave me fair treatment at my meetings, which made amends for happenings of a contrary nature for which they were not responsible. An electorate of 13,000 cannot be reached in a few weeks' campaign in Board schools, and it was something to find in the result at the poll 2,000 Liberals and 4,000 neutrals despite the misrepresentation of the entire Press of Birmingham and the efforts of the Unionists and the Imperial Tariff League. But at the same time I think it right that the public should get at least an inkling how it cornea about that the' judgment and intelligence of Birmingham " is so much at variance with the rest of the country. The result is a triumph, not to the statesman and his policy, as he would have the public believe, but to the methods of the " Boss," and the efficacy of the "machine,"—Yours etc.,</p>
        <p>There is much good reason for excuse in the injuries which prominent Warrington players have sustained. Few teams could have done so well with such men as Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson, Hockenhall, and Brooks, out of action at the same time. Seeing that all these men took part in the winning of the Cup last April, it is clear that the Club must be well off for reserves to have made even a moderate show during the season. Such is the case. There was probably never to much capable reserve talent on the Warrington register before, and with all the men fit two strong teams could be . put in the field. ' Another proof of this fact, if one were needed, is seen in the success of the "A " team which, not- -1 , withstanding repeated calls on its forces still heads , the " Combination " with an unbeaten record. The ' Warrington club ought, therefore, to do something great before the close of the season. Perhaps all the glory will come with the Cup-ties.</p>
        <p>Though they have so many capable players, War- rington are badly in need of a half back. Out of half-a-dozen claimants for first-team honours there is not one man who rises much alaove mediocrity. A. half of ' the S. James type would revolutionise the team. At present the three- quarters are hampered continuallv,by slowness at the base of the serum, and have 'little opportunity of showing their capabilities as an attacking force. Every match makes one sigh for the cleverness of "Smack" Bate in getting the ball away. So much time is lost in setting the third line going, that defence is made easy for the opponents. Possibly the defect will be remedied. If it is, Warrington should be able to put a side in the field capable of meeting the strongest opposition confidently.</p>
        <p>Swinton were the visitors on Saturday, and they I were easily vanquished though the score was only 5-0 I against them. The Warrington forwards took matters into their own hands in the way we know they can do, and the "Blues" were practically only active resisters for the rest of the afternoon. Slowness at half-back, and consequent want of finish prevented the Warring- ton score being trebled without any further expendi- ture of energy. The combination was of poor quality, and attacking movements on either side were easily checkmated. Davies did practically nothing, aid Lloyd is lacking in smartness and originality. The three-quarters were all good,and with proper attention would have penetrated the Swinton defence on more occasions than one.</p>
        <p>There were two important changes in the home team. The most interesting was the appearance of Ike Taylor of Widnes at centre. His transfer had only been completed the previous day, but the crowd knew all about it and gave him a warm welcome. Taylor was the best of the"chernicals,"and on Saturday he was the best of the Warrington backs. Being only 22 years old, and a player of the robust type, he should do Warrington excellent service in the centre. We only want Isherwood back again for the Club to have an excellent string of threequarters at command. A word of commendation is due to the Committee for bringing off the capture of such a useful man as Taylor in time for the Cup-ties. If they will only find a class half-back they will be forgiven all sins whether of omission or commission.</p>
        <p>On the Swinton side there was not much cleverness noticeable. The team were so well beaten forward that the backs had few opportunities. Still Dixon, full back, Oilfield, right wing. and the two halves, Dan Davies and llolyneux, did not do badly. Special mention should be made of Heath, Thomas, Boardman, and Swift, who were always conspicuous in the War- rington vanguard, In the League table, Hunslet now have a substantial lead, while both Leigh and Broughton have better prospects than Oldham. Leeds have fallen away completely and will, apparently, soon descend to War- rington's humble level.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows For. Agst. Per- ?. W. L. D. P. G. T. P. G. T. P. centage Hunslet 2O 17 303446 49 239 10 19 77 8500 ' Leigh Bro'ton R2O 15 4. 13132 72 280 18 19 93 77.50 Oldham 24 18 51375 i 64 254 18 14 78 77'08 Halifax— 2l 13 2 63227 35 159 15 12 66 7619 Bradford 2O 13 5 22332 56 232 18 23 105 70'00 Hull ICH 23 15 6 23225 38 168 27 25 129 69.56 HrulTeld l9 12 5 22021 33 150 14 19 88 6842 ' Runcorn Wigan l9 12 612531 53 221 15 23 99 65.78 Keighley 2O 13 7 02629 47 199 19 25 113 65.00 Leeds Salford 2l 12 7 22831 50 207 21 10 00 6190 St. Helens 18 11 7 02226 43 181 21 23 111 6111 Swinton 2O 10 9 12122 29 131 17 27 115 52.50 WakTeld T2l 9 8 42230 31 153 21 34 150 5238 Warrington 21 11 12 12527 33 153 21 30 132 4791 Hull 2l 812 11728 30 146 23 34 148 4017 York 21 8 12 1 /7 25 19 107 24 31 141 40.47 Pontefract 19 711 115 23 27 127 24 29 135 3947 flramley Dewsbury 23 616 11313 22 92 23 41 179 2826 Castleford l3 3 9 1 711 6 40 35 51 223 2692 Barrow 18 413 1 9 917 69 37 62 260 2368 Batley 2O 314 3 920 9 67 28 31 149 2250 Rochdalell 21 211 5 912 22 90 23 58 220 2112 Widnes l6 212 2 6 210 34 17 37 145 1875 Morecambe 15 212 1 510 19 77 21 47 189 1616 Brighouseß 18 211 2 612 11 57 31 59 236 1616 Milloni l2 110 1 311 9 49 29 50 208 1250 Normanton 16 113 2 4 5 3 19 28 44 188 12.50 The points are two for a win and one for a draw. The percentage is calculated upon the basis of the total points possible.</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League up-to-date is as follows : _Points.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts. Wigan 7 .- 6 -. 1 .- 0 .- 59 21 .- 12 Leigh Warrington Runcorn St. Helens Widnes 7 . 1 G 0 ... 14— 59 ... 2 -- Warrington will have a strong team for the Salford match to-day, though such good men as Isherwood, Dickinson, and Fish are still absentees. Hockenball takes the place of Davies, and it is to be hoped that the rest he has had will have restored his form. Team : Berry, full back; Widdeson, Kenyon, Taylor, and Harris, threequarters ; Hockenhall and Lloyd, half backs ; Preston, Boardman, Shugars, Thomas, Belton, Naylor, Heath and Harmer, forwards.</p>
        <p>It a determined effort is made, there should be a fair chance of success at Weaste, for it is only at half back,that Salford are likely to be in any way superior. The Warrington threequarters look a sounder lot,aml I have hopes of Taylor and Kenyon keeping Lomas well in hand. The forwards um do a lot towards securing the points by giving a display equal to that of Saturday last.</p>
        <p>The selection committee of the Salford club met on Wednesday evening, and decided to play the follow- inc. team:—J. Cochrane; W. APWhirter, I. Lewis, J. Lomas (Capt.), and J. E. Davies; D. Preston and W. D. John; J. Rhapps, D. Rees, E. J. Thomas, A. Buckler, J. Spencer, S. Warwick, W. Brown, and S. Holbrook. Reserves, C. C. Swindells and E. Drake. An alteration has been made in th 3 three-quarter line, J. E. Davibs, who hails from Birkenhead, taking the place of V. Sampson. Davies, it will be remem- bered, played for Salford last season.</p>
        <p>Warrington " A" made no mistake in their com- bination match at Pemberton on Saturday, a victory by 11-6 being recorded. I understand that Hallam ,gave a display more like his true form, and there are</p>
        <p>possibilities of his returning to the premier fifteen ere long. To-day, the "A " team are at home to Salford St. Bartholomews and should win with ease. The team will be : Hallam, full back ; Jordan, Jones, Hewitt, and another; Davies and Brooks ; Ashton, Forster, Fairhurst, Cook, and Morrison.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath paid a visit to the North Manchester club and had vet), hard lines in being beaten by a dropped goal to a try, after having by far the best of the game. W. Davenport at full back played a sound game and saved his forwards a lot of running about by judicious use of touch. The threequarter line with only one regular man playing was not a great success, the centres hanging on to the bail far too long. B. Bradshaw on the wing played his usual good gaine,liis try being a really fine piece of work. C. Melbourne on the other wing was safe and got through a lot of work. The centres, W. H. Bell and J. Dai ntith, who made a first appearance in this position, were sound in defence but did not combine very well. The halves, H. Davenport and P. Hazelhurst, both played well, getting the ball away from the scrums in splendid fashion. The f orwards to a man played a hard and keen game and in the second half controlled the scrums and were quite the equal of the opposing' eight in the loose. W. Smith and K. Gibbs were particul trly prominent.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath " A " team met frynton on the Ellesmere-road ground and were defeated by 18 points to nil after a hard game.</p>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>Last week Stockton Heath -met Western at Arpley, and although the home side were minus three regular players, a victory was expected. The result was 2-0 in favour of Western, they being the first club to win on the Stockton Heath ground.</p>
        <p>The victory went to the better side, but the ground was in a wretched condition, being almost as bad as a clay pit and this brought scientific play down to almost minus quantity. For Western Griegson played a splen- did game, and was quite the best player on either side, while on the home side W. Richardson played a hard game but lacked support.</p>
        <p>This week the club play South Manchester 1 and 2 when the following teams will represent Stockton Heath :-Ist (away), R. G. Pierpoint, goal; E. E. Woods and E. Pierpoint, backs ; J. G. Hatton; H. Kirkconnel and Eric Fairclough, halves, T. Day, W. Richardson, W. H. Banks, R. Pierpoint, and H. Dolan, forwards. Train leaves Central 12.55. -Referee, W. Carter. -- 2nd (home), Arpley Cricket ground, F. G. Kertland, goal ; P. B. Hawtin and B. Silcock, backs ; B. Guest, E. R. Jones and A. Hoclgkinson, halves ; W. Linnel, W. Howard, C. W. Walker, S. Baxter and E. Fairelough, forwards. Bully-off 2.30. Referee, N. W. Bellamy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>DECLARING THE RESULT.</head>
        <p>DECLARING THE RESULT.</p>
        <p>Exciting Scenes.</p>
        <p>The representatives of the Press were treated with scant courtesy at the declaration of the poll at Newton- le-Willows"Pown Hall on Tuesday, The result WAS not declared until quarter-past twelve (midnight), and the six or seven Pressmen in attendance were excluded from even the doorways, corridors, and staircases of the building until long after the Returning Officer (Mr. Appleton) had announced the figures from one of the windows to a crushing, shouting,cheering, and hooting crowd of 12,000 or 13,000 persons in the street below. Tremendous enthusiasm prevailed after the declara- tion, and the Returning 0:I1cer stood with uplifted hands for a long time at the window before even a semblance of quietude among those close by could be obtained. It was with difficulty that the exact figures could be heard, but the "majority 541 " was sufficient. There were excited cries of "Seddon for I ever !" and "No more slavery ?" Hundreds of voters had tramped from outlying districts, whil,-; thousands from Earlestown. Ashton, and Haydock marched along 1 the roads homewards shouting and cheering for Seddon. I Speaking from the Town Hall windows Mr. Seddon who was frantically cheered, referred to the glorious victory which Labour had achieved that day, and attri- buted it to the persistency and energy of his workers. Colonel Pilkington, the unsuccessful candidate, also thanked those who had voted and worked on his behalf, and expressed his determination to again fight the con- stiuency at the first opportunity. Mr. Seddon was followed by a large crowd to the F,arlestown Labour Club, where congratulatory speeches were delivered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>LIBERAL VICTORY AT NORTHWICH. -- -- Sir John Brunner's Record Majority. The result of the polling in the Northwich Division was declared on Wednesday as follows : BRUNNER (L) NORTH (U) 4,551</head>
        <p>LIBERAL VICTORY AT NORTHWICH. -- -- Sir John Brunner's Record Majority. The result of the polling in the Northwich Division was declared on Wednesday as follows : BRUNNER (L) NORTH (U) 4,551</p>
        <p>1,792</p>
        <p>The counting was carried out at the Northwich Technical Schools, 87 per cent. having polle3. Colonel North was present throughout the count. Sir John Brunnar visited Bootle first thing to vote, returning before the declaration. There were scenes of wild enthusiasm when the result was made known. The Conservatives had looked for a reduced majority, and at the turnover they were crestfallen. Sir John Brunner was escorted in triumph to the Gladstone Club. Sir John Brunner, on the result being announced,shook haeds with Colonel North, declaring he had never met so courteous an opponent. The band played the" Con- quering Hero." Addressing the surging crowds, Sir John said he had never before been able to thank his opponent. It was his record majority (cheers). Cheshire would present a solid front of Liberalism and he was proud to welcome the Labour representatives. (Cheers).</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO SIR JOHN BRUNNER.. On enterin,, his carriage, which was dragged to Winningtonll by a hundred supporters, Sir John Brunner bad the misfortune to tear the muscles of his thigh just above the knee. He was unable to deliver an address at Winnington, and had to be assisted and Dr. I Browne, Northwich, summoned. Later Dr. Robert Jones, of Licarpool, was called in to consult with Dr. Browne. The patient has been ordered two or three weeks' absolute rest. A great demonstration to com- memorate Sir John's sixth Parliamentary triumph had been arranged to welcome him at Runcorn, Winsford, and Nliddlewich, but had, of course, to be postponed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>MR. R. PIERPOINT AND HIS DEFEAT.</head>
        <p>MR. R. PIERPOINT AND HIS DEFEAT.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. 11. Pierpoint on Monday evening paid a visit to the Junior Conservative Club where they were received with great heartiness. Mr. PIP:RPM-NT returned nis thanks to the members of the Junior Conservative Club for the assistance which they had given him during the recent election. They had had a great deal to contend against in the elec- tion in which they had unfortunately failed. They had had the much-talked-of swing of the pendulum," which undoubtedly was a living fact in almost every election. Men said—one could hardly conceive for what reason— "We must have a change." If they were asked why he did not think they knew. (Hear hear.) Well, they had got their change, and he could not exactly say he hoped they would like it, hat it might be that they might like it. The other side had not been too particular in its , staternenes, and its placards had not been entirely satisfactory to the pure lovers of truth. (Hear, hear.) As to themselves, he thought they would all acknow- ledge that, at all events, he had shown how an English- man ought to take a defeat—with good humour end with courage. (Hear, hear, and applause.) He did not mean to say for a moment that he liked being defeated, or that he was not eggregiously disappointed, but he felt it was his duty to show how a man should take the evil event when it came, and he thought he had suceeded. (Applause.) He need scarcely tell them that personally his happiness was not suffering. He was very happy and comfortable. (Hear, hear.) He would say in pass- ing, though it might seem A little selflsk, that after all the present condition of affairs had its compensation, because he was—although they might not think it—in every way a busy man—a man never contented unless he was doing'something, and he had now some time at his disposal for doing those things which particularly interested him, and freedom from the House of Commons would give him the opportunity which he had not bad for a great many years. On behalf of his wife and himself he thanked them one and all, and also their wives or sweethearts,for the work they had done. (Loud applause). and a Voice: "You will go in next time.") MR. DRAPER AND ."MIS-STATEMENTS AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>MISREPRESENTATIONS.</head>
        <p>MISREPRESENTATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. DRA-17E-1i7,-;;TC i);3iiiiii-,-)i-t-I;;Meaers and him- self, thanked Mr. and Mrs. Pierpoint for their kind- , ness in coming there that night. Their opponents had ' won but he could not help thinking that their success would not be very prolonged. Had they won their battle on great principles,supported by good and sound arguments, they might have expected their success to last, hut having regard to the fact that their success was based chiefly, or to a very great extent, upon what they (the Conservatives) could not help looking upon as very intentional mis-statements and misrepresenta- tions, he could not help thinking the electors would not be for any length of time deluded by them. One of their great cries was Chinese labour. He (Mr. Draper) did not think a greater political fraud had ever been perpetrated on the British electors. (Hear, hear.) There was also the big and little loaf. If the proposal to put a 2s. duty on corn were carried out it would not materially affect the size of the loaf. Nevertheless they had had displayed on the walls a huge Free Trade loaf and a tiny Tariff Reform loaf, which was absolutely misleading and created a false impression. Those were the two chief points, he thought, on which their opponents had succeeded. He could not congratulate them on the truth and honour- able manner in which they had put them forward. Before long the working men of Warrington would see how they had been deluded, and when they did find out they would sweep their opponents away from office with a huge majority. On behalf of the members and himself he wished Mr. and Mrs. Pierpoint happiness and pri,sperity through a long series of years. I (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>WHAT WON IN BIRMINGHAM. Mr. Outhwaite on Tammany Methods. A STRIKING INDICTMENT.</head>
        <p>WHAT WON IN BIRMINGHAM. Mr. Outhwaite on Tammany Methods. A STRIKING INDICTMENT.</p>
        <p>THE CHANGE IN CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>NOT A TORY MEMBER SURVIVES</p>
        <p>THE ROUT. Altrincham Rejects Mr. Disraeli.</p>
        <p>Assuming that Crowe will again return a Liberal to- day (Friday), Cheshire has been swept by the Free Traders from end to end. Thursday's great victory at Altrincham completes their long roll of triumphs. Chester and the second Stockport seat were wrested from the Conservatives by the Liberals,and the Labour party captured Birkenhead. This made the five urban seats solidly Liberals and the attack on the county divisions resulted in the winning of KnutsFord,slaccles- field, Eddisbury, Hyde, the Wirral and Altrincham. How remarkable these triumphs are may be gathered from the fact that Knutsford has been held since the division was formed in 1835 by the Hon. A. de Tatton Egerton, of Tatton Hall, who had only twice previously been opposed, winning in each case by a big majority. Eddisbury since its formation has always easily put in a Tory. Macclesfield has been Conservative since 1883 and the Wirral and Altrincham since they were con- stituted. Hyde returned a Liberal in 1385, but has not done so since. - _ _</p>
        <p>Crewe, on the other hand, was Liberal from 1885 to 1895, when the seat went to the Tories, till it was won back by Mr. Tomkinson in 1900. Northwich was Liberal in 1985, Unionist in 1880, and has been Liberal since 1892.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE. The following. table shows at a glance the remarkable change in Cheshire. _ _</p>
        <p>1905. 1906. Chester Eddisbury Knutsferd Macclesfield Noithwich L Stalybridge Stockport L &amp; C L &amp;Lab Birkenhead C ...- L Hyde Wirrall Altrincham C Crewe</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM ELECTION. -- Mr. Disraeli Badley Beaten. 1900.—C. majority, 1,508. Eleatorate, 16,492. W. J. CROSSLEY (L) *CONINGSBY DISRAELI (C) 5,672</p>
        <p>Majority • 2,619</p>
        <p>Altrincharu Division polled on Thursday, and as was expected, Mr. IV. J. Crossley, the Free Trade Liberal candidate was returned by a big majority over the sitting member, Mr. Coningsby Disraeli. LANCASHIRE'S SOLID FRONT.</p>
        <p>With Clitheroe the Lancashire contests are at an end, and as the result 45 Conservatives, one Labour and one Nationalist have been replaced by 29 Liberals, 12 Labour M.P.'s, a Nationalist, and 15 Conservatives. two of whom are Free Traders. The following table shows the representation of the seats in the county from the redistribution onwards :- 1885. 1886. 1892. 1895. 1900. 1906. Conservatives 37 ... 45 ... 33 ... 48 ... 48 15 Lib. and Lab. 20 ... 12 ... 24 ... 9 ... 9 ... 42</p>
        <p>THE NEW HOUSE. -- Present Distribution of Parties.</p>
        <p>The number of members so far returned to the new House of Commons is 60'3. They are distributed as follows among the different parties : Liberals 344 Labour Conservatives l4l Nationalists</p>
        <p>The 'Majority of the Liberals alone over all parties combined is 79 The majority of Liberals and Labour men com- bined over the Conservatives and Nationalists com- bined is 163 The majority of Liberals, Labour men, and Nationalists over Conservatives is 327 PARTY GAINS AND LOSSES.</p>
        <p>Liberal Gains 10 Conservative Gains</p>
        <p>Net Liberal Gains</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>ALLEGED FRAUDULENT BANKRUPT. Serious Charges Against a Former Warrington Tradesman.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED FRAUDULENT BANKRUPT. Serious Charges Against a Former Warrington Tradesman.</p>
        <p>Edmund Green Barratt, formerly of Warrington. and last from Blackpool was charged on remand at the War- rington Borough Court on Wednesday with having been adjudged a bankrupt and not disclosing certain assets; not delivering up certain property; concealing certain items; and making a false statement in his "statement of affairs "—all with intent to defraud. Mr. Rylands (Messrs. Boote and Edgar, Manchester) appeared for the Director of Public Prosecutions, and Mr. T. S. Steel for the prisoner. Mr. Maids said that the prisoner commenced business in August, 1903, as a biscuit and cake merchant. and in April, 1904, he presented his own petition, and was adjudicated bankrupt. Prisoner made the usual report as to his position to the Official Receiver, and then underwent the public examination. Subseonently certain things came to the knowledge of the Official Receiver, and he obtained an order to prosecute the prisoner. The charges against the prisoner were under section 11 of the debtors' act, and under sub-section 4 the onus lay upon the bankrupt to prove that he had no intend to defraud. The alleged offences against the prisoner were :—Not disclosing to the Official Receiver book and other debts due to him amounting to £49 17s. sd, a piano value £29, a rolled gold watch value £2 10s., a proportion of a residuary share of his grandfather's estate of the value of £l6 3s. 7d. : and further with not disclosing possession of a secretaire, hencote, and his share in a coal business. The offence was a serious one, because of the smallness of the estate as a whole. The total amount of the prisoner's liabilities only reached £125, and if the properties which he had mentioned had been put in there would have been 204. in the £ for the creditors. The estate, as disclosed by the bankrupt, only amounted to £32, or about 4s. in the £. " Evidence was then given by Mr. J. W. Bibby. chief -- The following letter appeared in the "Daily News on Thursday. I clerk at the Warrington County Court ; Mr. E. Johnson, Sir,—Speaking at Halesowen on Monday Mr assistant officiel receiver; Mr. C. J. Dibb, late official receiver, and other witnesses. Chamberlain said : The further hearing was ncl;ourned, hail being allowed "The verdict of Birmingham has, at all events, justi- prisoner in £5O and two sureties of £5O each. fled the policy I have purstts•cl, but elsewhere people</p>
        <p>EINIME</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Unitarian Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The anniversary celebrations in connection with Cairo street Unitarian Church have in every way been attend- ed with success. On Sunday special services were held at which the Rev. F. K. Freeston, of London, a former minister at Cairo-street, addressed large congregations both morning and evening. The annual congregational gathering was held on Monday and proved highly enjoy- able about 200 sat down to an excellent tea, their wants being ably superintended by the lady members of the con- gregation. Mr. F. NV. Monks presided over subsequent proceedings and was supported, amongst others by the 'Revs. F. K. Freeston, J. P. Matthews, and C. Harvey Cook, Mr. F. NV. Purse (superintendent of the Sunday school), Messrs. F. Monks, J. Rymer Young and Hill. The CHAIRMAN expressed his pleasure at the excel- lent attendance. It was also encouraging to see such large congregations at the services on Sundry, when they were able to welome Mr. Freeston among them. The Unitarians were not a large body in Warrington, and somehow they did not seem to grow in numbers as rapidly as he would like. They had beard a good deal about "the flowing tide " lately,and he wished it might extend to their Church. (Hear, hear.) However, they must hope on and put their shoulders to the wheel. and see if they could not create such a flowing tide as would help them on, not only in the Church, but to a larger and fuller work in Warrington. (Applause.) In conclusion, Mr. Monks pointed out that the committee had been faced by financial difficulties and expressed the hope that the present year would see an improvement. Mr. PURSE presented a fairly satifactory report upon the work of the Sunday school and its attendant institu- tions. He said their school like the church did not grow in numbers, though its usefulness was as great as ever. Last year the number of scholars decreased—and consequently the average attendance was affected, but the decrease was rather on the boys' side of the school. He regretted that, but they all knew that nowadays boys seemed inclined to despise the Sunday school more than was the case with their forefathers. The Girls' Club had been very active ; the penny bank flourished more than ever; the Band of Hope was on the increase—a very healthy sign—while the Lads' Club was doing much to bring the boys together. Ho was sorry the school library was not being used as much as it might be, but taking the school work as a whole it had been very active. As Mr. Monks had said,he hoped they would witness a flowing tide in their Church. They stood somewhat isolated, and did not get the outside support and interest other Churches had, but he hoped they would pull together with the one end in view. (Applause.) The Rev. F. MATTHEWS said he feared his Unitarian friends were rather tempted to accentuate their isolation a little bit too much. All had one common field, and worked for one great object—to establish the Kingdom of God—(hear, hear)—and surely everyone could unite on that. (Applause). Sometimes we were apt to forget the commonplaces of life, and it would perhaps be well to remember one or two things. First, he would ask them to believe that their Church stood for something —and to always have that something before them in their Church work: Secondly, they must believe in their parson, not because he wasa parson, but because behind the cleric there was the man. (Hear, hear.) The pastor of a Christian Church stood as the leader not only in all good work-- public or private—but be should he a leader in all that made for the splendid elements of high and beautiful character. (Hear, hear.) Thirdly, let them believe in their Sunday school, which was the feeder of the Church and fourthly, let them believe in themselves. Let them always remember they were working for something, and always keep the one great object before them and then success would attend their efforts. (Applause). The Rev. P. K. FRERsToN agreed with Mr. Matthews that they were a little toO sensitive about their isolation and thought that if they ignored it,or thought it did not exist, they would enter a new period of their congrega- tional history, and find more sympathy and friendship' than they had found in the past. (Applause). Ho thought a change was coming over the religious world —or at any rate, the Nonconformist world. The days of bitter controversy were gone, and they realised that the many things upon which they were agreed were of far more importance than the few things concerning which they differed. (Applause). He had to thank them for the cordial welcome both he and his wife had received from them, and he could say from his own experience that Cairo-strret was always loyal to its ministers. (Applause). The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK thanked his congregation for their work and support during the past year, and reported favourably upon the doings of the various institutions associated with the church and school. - During the evening the choir, under the direction of Mr. Muir, ably gave ftwo items, and Miss Taylor ably contributed two songs in a very tasteful manner. At the close Mr. F. Monks proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Matthews for his address, to the Chairman for presiding, to the choir and Miss Taylor' to Mr, Freeston and his wife, and to the ladies who had assisted at the tea. - •</p>
        <p>„,,.. Mr.J. R YAI ER YONG seconded and the proposition was carried with acclamation.</p>
        <p>Annual Exchange of Pulpits. The annual exchange of pulpits in connection with the local Evangelical Free Churches took place on Sun- day evening. There being good congregations. A united communion service was held in the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church at the close of the usual evening service. The Rev. C. L. Stowe presided, and there were also present the Rev. 3. S. Hughes (secretary Nonconformist Minister's Association), the Revs. S. Pitt, J. Yonge, J. F. Matthews, F. Vaughanand H. Wall. An address was delivered by th Rev. S. Pitt on "The Bond of the Sacrament" pointing out that the Saciarnent brought all men together and it was a bond that united all Christians, whether Anglican, Roman or Evangelical. An offertory was taken to defray expenses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>Property Sale at Warrington.</head>
        <p>Property Sale at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Blue Bell Inn on Wednesday Mr. Frank 'Hickman of (Messrs. W. Hickman and Son) submitted I for auction 0 Lots of property. There was a good attendance and the bidding was brisk, 5 Lots out. of the six being sold. Lot 1 was bought by the auctioneer, Lot 2 was withdrawn, Lot 3 was sold for £4OO to Mr. Brough, Lot 4 fetched £515. at winch price Mr. Birch was declared purchaser, Lot 5 at Stockton Heath six houses realized .£9lO. The auctioneer congratulated the various buyers on their bargains and was pleased to see such a free spirited bidding on the part of the buyers and snob a bona-fide desire on the part of tho , vendors to sell. Messrs. Davies and Forsbaw were I the solicitors, Mr. W. J. Forshaw was in attendance to read the necessary particulars.</p>
