        <p>THE EXAMINER. JANUARY 27th 1906.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chas. N. Nicholson, a cousin of the member Local Licensing Committee. for Warrington, has won the Doncaster seat by a, The following magistratss have been elected to form majority of 3,669. In 1900 he was beaten by 365, so the licensing committee for 1906 :—Messrs. John Fair- that his triumph to-day is a great one. dough, E. J. Guest, Charles White, R. W. Francomb, * * * John Geddes, J. W. Hayward, Henry Roberts, E. A. A seat has at last been found for Mr. Balfour, in Fox, David Tinnion. The annual Licencing Sessions the city of London, where there is a Conservative will be held on Thursday, February 9th. majority of over 10,000. Mr. Alban Gibbs has ASK for CUNNDIGHAAI'S HIGH-CLASe MLNERAL offered to retire in his leader's favour, and the latter eu ATERS.—Advt. has accepted. " Pretty Fanny " ought to feel safe 'here from the ferocious Free Traders. Mr. Frank Roberts at Warrington. * * * On Wednesday, Mr. Frank A. Roberts and his son The President of the Junior Liberal Association paid a visit to Warrington. They had been helping (Mr. Edmund Osborne) is to give his presidential Mr. Lief Jones to success in the Appleby Division,and address to the members of that energetic body of called at the Liberal Club in the evening. Mr. 'young men on Monday evening next. Messrs. H. W. Roberts, who is the landlord of tho Club, was cordially Guest and Harold Gray are the hon. secretaries, and welcomed by many of his old friends, and chatted for there is plenty of room yet for new members. about an hour. He expressed great satisfaction at the * Liberal victory in Warrington, and seemed in the best " TThe exactions of working men's shillings be the of health and spirits. 48. C. "is quite shocking the respectable ' Press. Up-to-date Fire Places.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, 4'hatever shall we do now- that real live working Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Ten are to sit in the House of Commons? We hope Dicky the dirty clothes of labour will not contaminate the " Thomas's Sad End. " On Saturday morning " Dicky " Thomas, a former well-dressed aristocrat. It would be altogether too I , Warrington and Widnes footballer was found dead in bad—don'cherno. I a lodging-house in Rose and Crown-street. Deceased By hi occupancy for years of the headmaster- * * ' who was about 30 years of age formerly played for s Mary's, and afterwards for a short time 'ship of the National Schools, Church-street, Mr. John arrington St. My Bewhurst has established a record which only a small for Warrington A " team, stibsequently assisting number are privileged to equal. On Thursday the W the he isdotnesh African war, as a volunteer going out first team for several seasons. He served .ice with Warrington contingent. Deceased will be of the highest tributes to Mr. Dewhurst's worth was respect of his old scholars found expression in the in t presentation to him of a handsome testimonial. One twice : interred on Saturday with full honours. ~ paid by Mr. Brown, an old scholar, who succeeds him I ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH.CLASS MINERAL as headmaster of Church-street schools, and who said WATERS.—Advt. he largely owed his success to Mr. Dewhurst's excel- I Entertainment at the Workhouse. lent training. On Monday eveniug last the Sr. Clement's Minstrel Troupe gave their entertainment to the inmates of the Workhouse. The programme which was throughout of a most amusing character was well received by the inmates. Considering that this was their first appear- ance in public, the various characters were well sus- tained. With a little more practice, the troupe will be able with confidence to appear before any audience. The guardians present were Mrs. Wilkinson, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, S. Davies and W. Hickman, together with the Master and Matron, and several of the nurses. A hearty vote of thanks to the troupe was proposed by Mr. Hickman, seconded by Mr. Davies, and was passed with loud cheers. Oddfellowship. The annual meeting of the Loyal Garibaldi Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows was held on Saturday at the Blue Lion, Market-place, N.G. John Wooley presiding over a large meeting. The balance sheet presented total funds of :£l,OOB 12s. 5d., an increase of over :f.,101, and a membership of 186, an increase of two. The report and balance sheet were adopted. The election of delegates of District Manage- ment Committee for 1906 resulted in P.O. Nathaniel ' Lawton, P.G. J. W. Meachin, P.G. R. Black-shaw, and Bro. J. Wooley, N.G., being appointed. It was reported that one of the most prominent members (P.G. John Hughes) had been seized with a serious , illness on coming to the lodge, and it was resolved ' that the secretary send a letter of sympathy. The election of officers then took place, and P.G. Joseph W. Perry was elected N.G. ; Bro. Richard Dennett, V.G. Br). G. W. Hart, elective secretary; Bro. Thomas Dickinson, secretary 1 and P.P.G.M. James Smith, treasurer. It was decided to present the retiring Lodge Grand Master with a Past Grand's' certificate. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST i BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. South Lancashire Sergeants' Dinner. 'The first annual dinner of the warrant officers and sergeants (past and present), of the Prince of Wales' Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment), was held on Saturday night at the Lion Hotel, Warrington, when about 60 members of the Dlnner Club sat down to an excellent repast, served by Mr. Lee. The proceedings were presided over by Sergeant-Major W. Delmage. During the progress of the.dinner, a number of tele- grams and letters wishing success were received, in- cluding those from the officers and sergeants of the 2nd Battalion. at Blackdown. By the permission of Major Hartley, the band of the combined depots (King's and South Lancashires'), under Sergeant Drummer Wainwright (Kings' Regiment), contributed to the enjoyment of the evening by rendering a first- class programme of music, which was much appre- ciated, while at intervals songs were given by Colour-Sergeant J. O'Brien, Colour-Sergeant Kelly, Mr Crossby and others. Mr. Boyle acted as accompanist. Potrolemn Oil in barrels and drums delivered free.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, bridge at., Warring- ton.—Advt. Animated Pictures at the Parr Hall. A programme, popular with Warrington audiences is being presented this week in the Parr Hall by the New Century Animated Picture Company who have booked for a stay of a fortnight. On Monday there was a crowded audience at the opening performance and this has been repeated every evening since. The pictures are of great variety from grave to gay and are amongst the finest ever shown in the town. in: the programme is included a reproduction of incidents ' in connection with the visit of the Prince and Princess ,of Wales to India. One of the most interesting of ' the series of bioscopic works is a visit to the British , mint the processes in the production of coins being clearly shown, but probably the most realistic and effective reproduction is that which shows the Swiss enjoying their national pastime of tobogganiag. The I programme is changed every v--cuing and there is , every promise given that the visit will be a highly successful one. WEDDING PRESENTS.—Large selection of Useful and Ornamental Articles.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.--Advt. Overcrowding at the Workhouse. A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday under the residency of Mr. J. Billinge. There were also present :—Rev. Dr. Mercer, Rev T. R. Matthews, Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkin- son, Miss Broadbent, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, R. Forshaw M. W. Seton, J. Cook, R. Flannagan, G. Mather, A. Brooks, T. H. Ashcroft. S. Davies, J. F. Awberry (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate schools).—ln moving the adoption of the minutes of the Home Committee, Mr. Forahaw said he thought the Board ought to be placed in possession of the true state of affairs at the Workhouse and especially in the Infirmary. When he visited the latter institution on Friday, Superintendent Nurse Biggin drew his atten- tion to the fact that there were 124 inmates on the female side and only 96 beds. In the case of one Child a bed had actually to be placed in a bath. When a state of affairs like that obtained, it was time the Board moved in the matter, and he wanted it to be on record that the Board were cognisant of the facts. Warrington people, he thought, ought to consider the question when they wanted to call the town "Beautiful Warrington." The state of affairs he had mentioned was not by any means beautiful.—Miss Broadbent seconded. The Infirmary, she said, was sadly over- crowded, and the conditions made it difficult for the nurses to manage the work. It certainly did not make for economy in the long run, and the Board would have to expend all the more in the end through not facing the situation at once.—The minutes were approved. Galvanized 13oofing Sheets, Roofing Felts, &amp;c. Large stook.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Presentation to the Rev. H. A. Lester. On Tuesday evening the Rev. H. A, Lester was the recipient of a handsome testimonial from the parish- oners of Daresbury in recognition of his services to the parish. The proceedings opened with an excellent tea which was provided by Miss Milner, and which was greatly enjoyed. The subsequent meeting was under the chairmanship of the Rev, T. 'l'. Lee Jones,who stated that during the past- few years unfor— tunately, there had been several interregnums, times when they had been without a vicar, either through death, or removal,or preferment, and on all these occa- sions Mr. Lester had filled the gap, with energy and ability. (Applause). Miss Milner said it gave her great pleasure, on behalf of the subscribers, to present to Mr. Lester a silver salver, an ink-stand, silver candlesticks and a silver cigarette case.—(Applause) —together with their heartiest wishes for his health, happiness and success in the future. The Rev. H. A. Lester briefly returned thanks. He felt glad he said, that lie liad had the privilege of ministering amongst them, and the parishioners had made it a privilege by the kind way in which they had always welcomed him. 'lt was his . first start practically in parochial work at Dareshury, and if he had succeeded at all it was a source of thankfulness to him. Mr. J. L. Jones proposed, and Mr. John Whitlew seconded, a hearty vote of thanks to Miss Milner for her hospitality, and the proposition Was carried with much heartiness. Mr. John White briefly replied on Miss Miler's behalf. Oil Stoves, Coke Stoves, Lamps, &amp;c.—J S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge at, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The writer of "Observer " notes states that : "We are informed on excellent authority that the Labour party in Warrington is already in touch with a probable candidate for the next election." This is Teally surprising. We should have imagined that all the " probable' candidates' at liberty, would be found in the House of Commons long before there is another election in Warrington. And we learn, too, on equally "excellent authority " that the Labour party in War- rington is totally ignorant of the matter. ....We are pleased that the undoubted claim of the warrington District Nursing Association to the gener- "a support of workingmen Is at last being realized. A strew, Workingtnen's committee has been formed With the'object of obtaining contributions from the *lll.ployees at the various works, and it is hoped to raise at least £250 per annum. Already noble service has been rendered to the Infirmary and it seems highly probable that the appeal on behalf of the sister institution will call forth a ready response. * * * Mr. Draper, speaking at the Junior Conservative 'Club the other evening said: " The Tory working men of Warrington would see how they had been deluded, and. when they did find out they would sweep their opponents away from office with a huge majority." That is just what has happened. The workingmen of Warrington had so long been deluded that they did not intend to be deluded any longer hence the 1500 majority last week for the progressive candidate. The old Tory Party will never again have the support of the workingmen. The days of 'milk and watery "representatives are no more. The electors want to be represented by • men who have a kkeen desire to promote the best interests of the whole . community and not of a select few. BALE! SALEII SALE l W. T. \VILMA NIS WILL COMMENCE HIS ANNUAL wtsras SA.LE TO-MORROW, SATURDAY. LARGE REDUCTION 3. MILLINERY. COATS. COSTIM4, YURS, DIIIISSIO4, ELOUSR3. an. WHITE HOUSE. SAN KEY S Hum% WARRINGTON.</p>
