        <p>—et's play at doctors," said Nannie. irou are ill, and I will bring the, medicine ;o cure you.&gt;3 "All right," he said, stretching himself iefore the fire on the rug. Nannie departed, and, after a/ time, re- :urned, arrayed in father's long coat and shiner that he wore before he went away. She entered, after thumping at the door, and said, in her blandest tones, " I've called in answer to your urgent request "(a grand speech which she used as often as possible in their little childish plays.. But she stopped, scandalised, on beholding her " patient " kneeling on the rug, and gazing into the glowing embers of the fire. He looked up, and, forgetting the play, cried excitedly, "Oh! Nan, do come and look in the fire; there are beautiful things. Nan drew herself up. "I'm the doctor," she said. "But don't you want to play ? " she added, looking disappointed. "Play at what ? ' he asked, looking be- wildered. Then, suddenly remembering, he said quickly, "Oh ! I know now. Never mind. Oh! do come and look—a picture of , daddy fighting." "Oh! where, where ? " Nannie shouted, for she dearly loved her father. "There! He pointed to a blitck heap of cinders, on which a black man was stand- ing, holding a gun. on which a white ash of paper fluttered like d white flag. " He's going to surrender, and there's the Boers coming to take him." Nan surprised him by bursting out im- petuously, "He wouldn't give in; he wouldn't surrender; he's too brave for=that " ; and she took up the poker, and wrathfully gave a blow to the advancing Boers. Crash! All the fire moved, and altered the picture. " Oh ! you've spoilt it," cried Bobbie, re- proachfully. "Oh ! no, I haven't. Look again in the corner. Thei-e's our house, and there's a waggon coming to it, full of soldiers. There's dad getting out of it, and mummie running to meet him, and me, and you." She paused at length for want of breath. Bobbie said wistfully, "I do wish daddy would come home; then mother wouldn't have to work so hard." "He hasn't surrendered," Nan persisted; "so he will come home, I know." Just then the door flew open, .and a tall, soldierly-looking man came in. The children stopped in their play and stared. He glanced at the queer dress, and an amused twinkle came into his eye.* Does Mrs. Wilson live here ? " he began. But he got no further, for, with a shriek of delight, they flung themselves into hiS outstretched arms. ph! daddy," gasped Nannie, nestling up to him, 01 knew you would come home." In the next few happy moments the long dreary life of struggling with' poverty, the longing for daddy, and their little play were all forgotten. Bobbie said, " The fire was true, after all." So they were happy once more. ALICE LATHAM.</p>
